\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira Falls as Below-Forecast Rate Rise, Easing of Capital Controls Weigh -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2023\par 12:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1043 GMT - The Turkish lira continues to fall after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered a smaller-than-expected interest rate rise last week followed by an easing of capital controls over the weekend, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "Simultaneously, policymakers are claiming that banks are no longer supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their own balance sheets--a claim which is difficult to verify," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Tatha Ghose says in a note. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  exhausted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves trying stabilise the lira while rates were being cut and there are tentative signs net reserves could slightly improve in the next readings, implying less aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's presidential election, he says. USD/TRY rises 0.7% to 26.0530 after hitting a record high of 26.1844 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 GMT - While the full impact hasn't been felt from \uc2 ECB\uc2  interest-rate hikes--which have risen 400 basis points since last July--the bank's job isn't yet done, President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  says. The level of rates will have to be continuously reassessed over time, based on uncertainty about the persistence of inflation, Lagarde says in a speech to the ECB Forum on Central Banking in Portugal. There is also uncertainty of the strength of policy transmission, since the eurozone hasn't been through a period of rate hikes since the mid-2000s, she says. "Under these conditions, it is unlikely that in the near future the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to state with full confidence that the peak rates have been reached," Lagarde adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT - EUR/USD is expected to edge higher from current levels to 1.11 in the third quarter, having moved in a range between around 1.06 and 1.10 since the beginning of the year, \uc2 Erste Group\uc2  analysts say in a note. "Our current assessment of the market is that as soon as the U.S. economic data make an end to interest rate hikes clear, the pressure on the dollar should increase," they say, adding that the maximum support for the dollar would then be reached. "It makes perfect sense to sell the dollar not then but at the peak of support," the analysts say. They consider U.S. economic data to be decisive for the exchange rate. EUR/USD rises 0.3% to 1.0943. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT - The marginal benefit of further interest-rate rises to U.K. banks is likely to be limited and could become a headwind, \uc2 Shore Capital\uc2  says in a note after the Chancellor told lenders they are expected to pass on higher interest rates to savers. "Banks will still benefit from the tailwind to income from their huge structural hedges, which only re-price slowly as the hedges are gradually rolled onto new (higher) swap rates," analyst Gary Greenwood writes. The brokerage sees net interest margins at their peak already and assumes they will stabilize rather than substantially reverse. Shore keeps a positive stance on U.K. banks, whose balance sheets are in good shape to withstand potential losses if loan defaults increase, Greenwood says. (elena.vardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT - European stocks lack direction as investors continue to eye the fallout from the weekend's aborted armed uprising in Russia, though Asia stocks mostly rise and Wall Street looks set to gain. The Stoxx Europe 600 drops 0.2%, but the FTSE 100, CAC 40 and DAX trade broadly flat. IG futures data show the Dow opening at 33740, versus Monday's close of 33714. Brent crude drops 1.1% to $73.50. "It was a more bullish session in Asia overnight, with strong gains for the Hang Seng and the Shanghai composite," IG analysts write. "The Chinese premier said the economy would be stronger in the second quarter than the first. On today's calendar are U.S. durable goods orders and Canadian CPI." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 GMT - The gap between the yield on U.S. and European corporate bonds and the yield on 'risk-free' government bonds of the same maturity narrowed in the second quarter of 2023, supported by strong investor appetite for risk, say HSBC Global Research analysts in a note. Spreads on riskier high-yield corporate bonds "narrowed significantly" compared to investment-grade spreads, the analysts say. Dollar-denominated high-yield credit performed best and posted the most aggressive spread tightening during the quarter, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0953 GMT - \uc2 PZ Cussons\uc2  shares drop 7% after the Imperial Leather soap maker said its near-term financial performance in 2023/24 could take a one-off hit from the devaluation of the Nigerian naira, though it said results for the year to May 2023 wouldn't be affected. At this point, Cussons might reasonably question the Nigerian market's role in its business, \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2  says. "The country is a big contributor, accounting for nearly a third of \uc2 PZ Cussons\uc2 ' revenue, but volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sometimes the country itself often seem to be a problem and may prompt questions about whether a sale of this part of the business could aid the long-term stability of the group," \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2  investment director \uc2 Russ Mould\uc2  writes. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT - Sterling could fall as there's likely to be growing skepticism over whether the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has raised interest rates enough and will be able to get inflation under control, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "The BOE seems to be chasing inflation developments rather than fighting them with an active monetary policy, which is damaging for sterling," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst You-Na Park-Heger says in a note. The market is pricing in 100 basis points of rate rises by year-end, which is unlikely given the \uc2 BOE\uc2 's hesitant stance, she says. Moreover, price pressures should ease in coming months, leading the BOE to end rate rises, she says. EUR/GBP could rise above 0.90 in the coming quarters from 0.8594 currently, she says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0943 GMT - The recent run of weak eurozone economic data has had a limited impact on the euro as expectations of interest-rate rises for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain intact, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. "The eurozone rate market still expects the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to deliver two further 25 basis points rate hikes this year," \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has signalled it will be monitoring core inflation and it's expected to pick up in summer, supporting expectations for two rate rises and leaving the euro trading at stronger levels despite weak data that have increased downside risks, he says. EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.0933. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT - \uc2 ECB\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to increase rates in July, but she refrained from commentary about future meetings. "My intention is not to signal any future decisions, but rather to frame the issues that monetary policy will face in the period ahead," Lagarde told the ECB Forum on Central Banking in Sintra. In other words, she refuses to get pinned down on the decision in September, according to Pantheon Macroeconomics' chief eurozone economist \uc2 Claus Vistesen\uc2 . While he sticks with a view that the bank will raise its key policy rate by 25 basis points in September, the risks are tilted toward the further hikes in 4Q, especially judging by Lagarde's tone of a second phase of rising inflation that is proving strong and persistent, he says. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT - A key to tackling persistent inflation may be in whether the services sector takes more of an impact from the effect of higher interest rates than seen so far, according to \uc2 ECB\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 . Monetary-policy shocks are typically transmitted more quickly and forcefully to manufacturing, reflecting the sector's higher interest-rate sensitivity, while there is a more muted and delayed impact on services, Lagarde told the ECB Forum on Central Banking in Sintra, Portugal. The question is whether services will "catch down", rather than catch up, with manufacturing, which has been seen in previous cycles, or whether it will be insulated from the effects of policy tightening for longer than in the past, given the strength of demand and employment in the sector, she says. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT - \uc2 JD Sports Fashion\uc2  drops 5% after the retailer reported a trading slowdown in May. While organic sales increased more than 15% in the first three months of its 2023/24 financial year, they rose by 8% in May, JD said, adding that it had expected the slowdown and that it still believed full-year 2023/24 headline pretax profit before adjusted items would match market expectations. The trading slowdown wasn't surprising, given the company had forecast it in its full-year results, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. "JD has historically been very conservative with guidance and subsequently upgraded as the year has progressed, but this may be tested given a weaker macro and rising interest rates in important markets," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts write. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 27, 2023 06:43 ET (10:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230627ej6r0012e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
