\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms TEB at 'B-'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 2,907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2023\par 17:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 22 Jun 2023 11:12 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms TEB at 'B-'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 The following is a press release from \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-22 June 2023:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S\uc2 .'s (TEB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B-' and Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR at 'B'. The Outlooks on the IDRs are Negative. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b-'.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is detailed below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Risk Caps IDR: TEB's LTFC IDR is underpinned by both its VR and its Shareholder Support Rating (SSR). The LTFC IDR is capped at 'B-', one notch below Turkiye's, despite a high propensity of \uc2 BNP Paribas S.A\uc2 . (BNPP; A+/Stable) to provide support, due to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. The LTLC IDR is one notch above its LTFC IDR as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk is lower in LC. The Negative Outlooks on the bank's IDRs mirror those on the sovereign while that on LTFC IDR also reflects operating-environment pressures. The bank's 'B' Short-Term (ST) IDRs are the only option mapping to LT IDRs in the 'B' category.\par \par \uc2 Capital Constrains VR: The VR reflects the concentration of TEB's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the challenging Turkish market and moderate asset quality, but also adequate profitability through the cycle despite its modest franchise (end-1Q23: 2% of banking sector assets) and reasonable FC liquidity. TEB's VR of 'b-' is below the 'b' implied VR due to adjustment for capital and leverage, reflecting only adequate core capitalisation for TEB's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 Support: TEB's SSR reflects support from BNPP, reflecting TEB's strategic importance to, integration and role within the wider BNPP group and its small size relative to BNPP's ability to provide support. However, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the SSR at one notch below the sovereign rating, reflecting \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede TEB's ability to service its FC obligations is higher than that of a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 TEB is 55%-owned, but fully controlled, by TEB Holding, in which \uc2 BNP Paribas Fortis SA / NV\uc2  (BNPPF; A+/Stable) has a 50% stake. TEB is fully consolidated by BNPPF, a core subsidiary of BNPP. BNPP ultimately holds a 72.5% stake in TEB, including a 23.5% stake held directly.\par \par \uc2 Only Adequate Capitalisation: TEB's common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio declined to 11.2% at end-1Q23 (11% net of forbearance) from 12.4% at end-2022 due to a tightening of forbearance in risk-weighted assets (RWAs) calculation. Capitalisation is supported by high pre-impairment operating profit (1Q23: equal to 12% of average loans, annualised), full reserves coverage of non-performing loans (NPLs), and ordinary support from BNPP but remains sensitive to Turkiye's economic outlook, lira depreciation and asset-quality risks.\par \par \uc2 Below Sector Growth: TEB has historically pursued a fairly conservative growth strategy. The bank also has below sector average FC lending (end-1Q23: 22%) although not all borrowers are likely to be fully hedged against lira depreciation. SME exposure (23% of performing loans; solo basis) increases asset -quality risks given the segment's sensitivity to macro-economic volatility.\par \par \uc2 Asset-Quality Risks: TEB's impaired loans ratio fell to 1.6% at end-1Q23 (end-2022: 1.7%), outperforming the sector average and supported by collections and write-offs, while total reserves coverage increased. Asset-quality risks remain given its exposure to economic and market volatility, loan seasoning, moderate Stage 2 loans (7% of loans, 21% of average reserves) and exposure to SME and unsecured retail lending (about half of performing loans; solo basis).\par \par \uc2 Boosted Profitability: TEB's annualised operating profit was 7.6% of RWAs in 1Q23 (2022: 7.6%), as customer-driven trading income and low impairment charges offset the impact of muted lending growth, contracting net interest margin partly due to slower inflation-driven gains, and the impact of macro-prudential measures. Profitability remains sensitive to slower GDP growth, asset-quality risks and economic and regulatory developments.\par \par \uc2 Significant FC Liquidity: Customer deposits comprised 83% of total funding at end-1Q23; 45% were in FC and 19% were \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 )-protected lira deposits. Wholesale funding (79% in FC) comprised 17% of total funding (9% net of parent funding).\par \par \uc2 Refinancing risks are manageable given the bank's reasonable FC liquidity and potential liquidity support from BNPP. Nevertheless, FC liquidity, mainly comprising \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkiye, could come under pressure from sector-wide FC deposit instability or a prolonged loss of market access.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Negative Rating Action/Downgrade\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of the bank's IDRs would follow a downgrade of both its VR and its SSR. A downgrade of the bank's LTLC IDR would follow a downgrade of the SSR.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of Turkiye's sovereign rating or an increase in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk could lead to a downgrade of TEB's SSR. TEB's SSR is also sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of BNPP's ability and propensity to provide support.\par \par \uc2 TEB's VR could be downgraded on further marked deterioration in the operating environment, a material erosion in the bank's FC liquidity buffers, for example due to a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability, or in the capital buffers, if not offset by shareholder support. The VR is also sensitive to a sovereign downgrade.\par \par \uc2 Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Positive Rating Action/Upgrade\par \par \uc2 Rating upgrades are unlikely given heightened operating-environment risks and market volatility, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign rating, and our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the VR is unlikely given heightened operating-environment risks, which exert further pressure on TEB's thin capital buffers and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 TEB's senior unsecured debt ratings are rated in line with the bank's FC IDRs, reflecting average recoveries for this type of debt. The Recovery Rating of these notes is 'RR4', reflecting average recovery prospects in a default\par \par \uc2 TEB's 'AA(tur)' National Rating is driven by shareholder support and is in line with foreign-owned peers'.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 TEB's senior unsecured debt ratings are sensitive to changes in the bank's IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to a change in TEB's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b-' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: sovereign rating (negative) and macro-economic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and a high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 TEB's IDRs are either driven or underpinned by shareholder support from its majority shareholder, BNPP.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has revised TEB's ESG Relevance Score for Management and Strategy to '4' from '3' to reflect an increased regulatory burden on all Turkish banks. Management ability across the sector to determine their own strategy and price risk is constrained by increased regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also by the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenges of implementing regulations at the bank level. This has a moderately negative impact on the credit profile and is relevant to the rating in combination with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S\uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ; Shareholder Support Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ahmet Kilinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1272\par \par \uc2 ahmet.kilinc@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ege Birol,\par \