\par\par \b \uc2 Erdogan Signals Economic Shift -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,116 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2023\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A18\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Newly re-elected president also changes foreign-policy and security leadership\par \par \uc2 By Jared Malsin and Chelsey Dulaney\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL -- Newly re-elected Turkish President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  introduced a new cabinet that changed the leadership in the areas of foreign policy and security and signaled a shift in economic policies that have pushed the country to the brink of financial instability.\par \par \uc2 In a televised speech Saturday evening, hours after he was sworn in for another term as president, Erdogan said he was reinstating \uc2 Mehmet Simsek\uc2  as finance minister. Simsek, who previously served as finance minister and deputy prime minister under Erdogan, is a symbol of a time when Erdogan adopted more-conventional economic policies earlier in his 20-year run ruling Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is at an increasing risk of financial ruin largely because of the president's own policies, which have pushed the country's reserves into the red and driven millions of Turks closer to poverty. He won re-election in May despite rising costs of food, housing, medicine and other essentials, a crisis that his rivals had hoped to ride to victory.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan also reshuffled the leadership of his foreign-policy and security team, moving some of his key loyalists into new roles. His new foreign minister is \uc2 Hakan Fidan\uc2 , the longtime head of Turkey's intelligence agency, MIT. He replaces Mevlut Cavusoglu, one of the longest-serving members of Erdogan's cabinet, who was in office for nearly eight years. Turkey's new defense minister is Yasar Guler, formerly the chief of staff of the armed forces, who succeeds the long-serving minister \uc2 Hulusi Akar\uc2 , who oversaw a series of wars and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Erdogan.\par \par \uc2 Though new officials are overseeing \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign policy and security, the reshuffle isn't expected to have a major impact on the country's approach to a series of crises abroad, including Russia's invasion of Ukraine and a standoff in which Erdogan has been refusing to approve Sweden's entrance to the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2 . Erdogan himself has taken broad control over foreign policy, and the new ministers are his longtime deputies in new positions.\par \par \uc2 He also replaced his long-serving interior minister, the hard-liner \uc2 Suleyman Soylu\uc2 , appointing the Istanbul provincial governor, Ali Yerlikaya, in his place.\par \par \uc2 The installation of a new team to handle Turkey's economy could have wide-ranging consequences for the country, a member of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  and once a leader among emerging markets that has been in the grips of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The appointment of Simsek, widely respected in the international banking world, is seen as a signal that Erdogan is trying to stabilize the country's economy after the president pressured the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into cutting interest rates despite high inflation, an approach that has plunged Turkey into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis.\par \par \uc2 While Simsek's return is seen as returning some credibility to the Turkish government's handling of the economy, it doesn't necessarily mean that Erdogan is backing down from his stance on interest rates, which has been widely criticized by economists and triggered a flight of Western investment from Turkey.\par \par \uc2 "There is the Erdogan model, and there is the orthodox model. It's going to be a mix," said \uc2 Hakan Akbas\uc2 , a senior adviser at Albright Stonebridge Group who advises firms on investing in Turkey. "These competent loyalists will do everything to bring inflation down, to address the current-account deficit," he said of the new economic team.\par \par \uc2 Simsek, a former \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  strategist in London who held key ministerial positions under Erdogan from 2007 to 2018, is well-known among foreign investors who were once enthusiastic participants in Turkish markets. Kieran Curtis, head of emerging-market local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt at the U.K. asset manager \uc2 Abrdn\uc2 , recalls meeting Simsek many times during investor meetings and bond roadshows in London. Simsek was known for presenting long lists of changes he hoped to implement, Curtis said.\par \par \uc2 "He never was really able to do what he wanted to do," said Curtis. "He didn't get the political support from his boss."\par \par \uc2 Simsek left government in 2018 after Erdogan won re-election for a second term, following months of speculation that the two were clashing over the economy.\par \par \uc2 While a return of foreign investment would help to plug Turkey's financing gaps, many investors remain cautious about what Simsek will be able to achieve. Erdogan has fired or replaced a number of senior officials, including several \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors, who opposed his economic policies.\par \par \uc2 The government aggressively defended the lira before the country's election in May, holding it at a level stronger than 20 to the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke through that level last Monday, a day after Erdogan won re-election, and by Friday weakened to a record of about 21 lira to the dollar, an almost 5% drop, marking its worst week since 2021.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Turkey's benchmark stock index soared 12% this past week and government bonds rallied on hopes that Simsek's return will usher in a shift to an economic policy more attractive to investors.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan has concentrated power in the presidency in recent years, taking broad control over institutions and tightening his grip on the country's finances. He fired three \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in three years and pushed aside other senior finance officials in pursuit of his policy of cutting interest rates, which he says are needed to stimulate growth. He has said lower interest rates will eventually bring down inflation, an unorthodox view.\par \par \uc2 Simsek and his team will face the challenge of dealing with a president who has grown more powerful, and who has his eye on another election in which control over the Istanbul and Ankara city governments is up for grabs next year. The municipal governments of both cities are currently controlled by Erdogan's opponents.\par \par \uc2 "Erdogan has political power with his new five-year term. It will be hard for them to resist his policies," said Tugberk Citilci, an economist at Istanbul's Nisantasi University, who in recent years has been briefed by the government on economic policies. "For Simsek, it's going to be difficult to take a sharp U-turn from heterodox policies to orthodox policies."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jared Malsin at jared.malsin@wsj.com and Chelsey Dulaney at chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 5, 2023).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 05, 2023 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230605ej65000fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
