\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Will the Turkish economy endure five more years of Erdogan?\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  was re-elected as President of Turkey last Sunday, May 28, in a narrow victory in the second round. All this amidst criticism of democratic irregularities, denounced by the opposition and by the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE).\par \par \uc2 Erdogan, who after two decades at the head of the Eurasian nation will preside the country for another five years, has received congratulations from the main world leaders, although some of the most effusive have come from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , \uc2 Lula da Silva\uc2  or Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro.\par \par \uc2 But the markets have not taken the election result with such joy, with the Turkish lira sinking this week to new historic lows, moving between 21 and 22 units per euro. And all while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which already finds its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in negative - tries to artificially prop up the value of the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a scheme of highly remunerated savings accounts, limits on cash withdrawals or the sale of gold and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The first years of the Turkish leader's administration, when his openness and moderation allowed the country to experience a particular economic miracle,are now a thing of the past. At that time, political stability, healthy public accounts and institutional quality provided high growth rates, controlled inflation and moderate unemployment, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  to develop powerfully so far this century.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  turn But Islamic dogmas have become increasingly important in Erdogan's social and economic policies, while political repression has intensified and civil liberties have been weakened. And this, coupled with economic instability, is making Turkey an increasingly uncomfortable and problematic partner for Western nations.\par \par \uc2 In the specific case of economic management, Turkey has adopted an ultra-loose monetary policy, which has weakened the value of the lira and triggered a serious inflation problem. Erdogan, contrary to all economic orthodoxy, argues that lowering interest rates is the best way to ensure growth and fight inflation. And to implement his plan, he has shattered whatever independence the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had left. But clearly, this policy has done nothing but fuel rising prices and destabilize the economy. Thus, with the recent falls, the lira has already lost nearly 80% of its value against the dollar in the last five years, and the manipulation of the official exchange rate has meant that it no longer corresponds to that offered by private money changers, as is the case in countries such as Argentina.\par \par \uc2 This loss in the value of the lira translates into a massive increase in the price of products. Specifically, inflation in April stood at 43.7% compared to the same month of the previous year. The data accumulates on the escalation of prices already experienced in 2022, which closed with an inflation of 72% or those experienced since 2017, when it began an uninterrupted path with CPI above 10% per year.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the country's economy is faltering, with growth forecasts moderating over the next few years, registered unemployment remaining among the highest levels in the \uc2 OECD\uc2  - above 10% - and international distrust growing more and more in the face of Erdogan's rapprochement with leaders such as Putin and his distancing from Western powers. The Index of Economic Freedom illustrates the picture perfectly: Turkey has fallen from 58th globally in 2018 to 104th in this year 2023, largely due to the deterioration of the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 Given this situation, and with the elections already won, \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  faces a complex scenario in his new legislature. If he wants to decisively control inflation, he will have no choice but to turn monetary policy around by raising interest rates, currently at 8.5%. The problem? Well, this measure, while stabilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would contribute to a definitive stagnation of the Turkish economy, at least in the short term, with the attendant risk of soaring unemployment and social unrest.\par \par \uc2 For the moment, however, the Turkish leader has not shown any hint of a shift in his economic policy. His proclamations, rather, continue with the expansionary strategy: salary increases for civil servants, elimination of the retirement age, promises of free gas? And of course, all this does nothing but shore up the country's chronic public deficit and further weaken the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So, whichever way he chooses to go, Erdogan can rest assured that his next 5 years at the helm of Turkey will be far from easy. Nor, of course, will they be easy for his countrymen.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230603ej63004v6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
