\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Vote Heightens Tensions -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2023\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Erdogan has an edge in runoff, but he faces concerns about ailing economy, democracy\par \par \uc2 By Jared Malsin\par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL -- Turkey will hold a runoff presidential election later this month, officials said Monday, with President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  emerging from this weekend's polls with a surprise advantage over his main challenger in a vote with far-ranging domestic and geopolitical implications.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan won 49.51% and his opponent \uc2 Kemal Kilicdaroglu\uc2  had 44.88% in the first round of the election on Sunday, with all of the votes counted, according to the head of the Turkish Supreme Election Council.\par \par \uc2 Neither candidate won the 50% majority needed for a first-round victory, with both saying they are prepared to accept a second round of voting, which would take place on May 28.\par \par \uc2 The outcome heightens anxieties within Turkey as both camps prepare for what is likely to be two more weeks of intense campaigning for the country's first-ever runoff election.\par \par \uc2 The result of Turkey's election could have ripple effects both at home and abroad. Mr. Erdogan has deftly leveraged Turkey's position at the crossroads of East and West, inserting the country into great power rivalries and wielding influence in the Middle East, the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2  and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But at home, Mr. Erdogan faces anger over economic policies that have left many Turks poorer, as well as resentment over moves to tighten his grip on power and erode the country's democratic institutions.\par \par \uc2 Sunday's results also raise the risk that Turkey could face a financial reckoning in the coming months and years, investors and analysts said. Turkish stocks and bond prices fell on Monday after the inconclusive vote that gave Mr. Erdogan the lead.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have pulled money out of Turkey under the rule of Mr. Erdogan, who has pushed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adopt an unorthodox policy of cutting interest rates despite soaring inflation. The country's economic imbalances include a wide current-account deficit and a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is still overvalued.\par \par \uc2 The outcome of Sunday's vote dashed the hopes of Mr. Erdogan's opponents, who had buzzed with optimism in recent days that they could unseat Mr. Erdogan in the first round of voting.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kilicdaroglu, backed by an alliance of six parties, had hoped to ride a wave of public discontent over a cost-of-living crisis and fatigue with Mr. Erdogan's one-man style of rule to a historic victory after two decades in which the incumbent has towered over Turkish politics and concentrated power in the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan trailed Mr. Kilicdaroglu in a series of polls in the days before the election. The sudden withdrawal of a third-party candidate also lifted Mr. Kilicdaroglu's chances of a first-round victory, political analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Sunday's election results show that Turkey remains deeply divided, with Mr. Erdogan beloved by millions as a champion of the country's conservatives, and reviled by others as a leader who has eroded the country's democratic institutions and jailed his opponents.\par \par \uc2 "What we see again is that Turkish society is cut into two halves," said Murat Somer, a professor of political science and international relations at Istanbul's \uc2 Koc University\uc2 . "This polarizing politics is actually very powerful once it becomes a state of politics, a state of equilibrium."\par \par \uc2 In a speech early on Monday, Mr. Erdogan claimed a majority for his ruling alliance in Parliament, dealing another significant blow against the opposition that will likely shape the perception that the president has an advantage heading into a runoff election.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan's Justice and Development Party, known as AKP, remained the largest party in Parliament, winning at least 266 seats. Together with its coalition partners -- including the right-wing Nationalist Movement Party, which claimed 50 seats -- the ruling bloc will retain control over the chamber, according to results reported by state news agency Anadolu.\par \par \uc2 "We are well ahead," the president told a throng of cheering supporters in Ankara early on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Unbowed by the initial returns, the opposition vowed to fight on.\par \par \uc2 "I am here, right here, and you are here. I will fight until the very end," said Mr. Kilicdaroglu, pounding his fist to his heart and slapping his hand on a desk in a video he tweeted on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The runoff election will pit Mr. Kilicdaroglu, a longtime campaigner for Turkish democracy and rule of law, against Mr. Erdogan, the country's consummate political survivor who in the past defied challengers and overcame a military coup attempt in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Rallying tens of millions of voters to his side, Mr. Erdogan has proven the enduring power of his brand of politics with its appeals to Muslim solidarity and national pride. During the election campaign, Mr. Erdogan also attacked his opponents, calling the opposition "pro-LGBT" and accusing them of siding with "terrorists."\par \par \uc2 "I think the economic problems will come and go. What really matters for me is the unity and stability of the state," said Gonul Tasbasi, a 38-year-old mother of two who voted in Kasimpasa, the working-class Istanbul district where Mr. Erdogan grew up.\par \par \uc2 Results reported by Turkey's state news agency showed Mr. Erdogan pulling ahead generally in more rural and conservative districts, though he also drew millions of votes from major cities and held a lead in Bursa province, home to Turkey's fourth-largest city of the same name, and in Gaziantep, another large city and an important industrial center near the Syrian border.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kilicdaroglu led in the country's largest cities, including Istanbul, Ankara, and Izmir, and claimed large majorities in the Kurdish-majority provinces of southeastern Turkey, according to the state news agency. The country's pro-Kurdish party put its support behind the opposition candidate, in a decision that leaders hoped would deliver millions of votes and a possible decisive margin.\par \par \uc2 A third party's candidate, far-right politician Sinan Ogan, claimed 5.17% in the first round of voting, according to the state news agency, meaning that his bloc of votes could play an important role in deciding the outcome in the runoff. Mr. Ogan called for an acceleration of a clampdown on refugees amid a rise in anti-immigrant sentiment within Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Though the ruling coalition hung on to its majority in Parliament, Mr. Erdogan's AKP party declined in its share of votes from 42.56% in the last election in 2018 to 35.49% in the initial results reported by the state news agency, reflecting public concerns about Turkey's economy, which is under deep strain due to a collapsing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira has lost more than half its value in the past two years despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have depleted \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves. According to a government assessment, Turkey also needs more than $100 billion to rebuild, after earthquakes in February devastated a swath of the country and killed 56,000 people across Turkey and Syria.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey has always had vulnerabilities but for the past three years the country has increasingly been on borrowed time," said Richard Briggs, a senior fund manager at Candriam.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan's outsize role in global affairs means that the election has important stakes for the future of Turkey, the Middle East, and for Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 While Turkey is a key member of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , Mr. Erdogan has nonetheless drawn closer to Russia and has cultivated a personal relationship with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , ties that have caused friction with the U.S. and its European allies.\par \par \uc2 In the Ukraine war, Mr. Erdogan has sought to play all sides, selling weapons to Kyiv while also expanding trade with Moscow. The U.S. has expressed concern that Turkey is helping Russia to evade Western sanctions. During the electoral campaign, Mr. Erdogan has argued that Turkey has become a more powerful player on the world stage under his leadership.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kilicdaroglu has promised to rebalance Turkey's alliances, improving ties with the West, resurrecting the country's application to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and strengthening Turkey's role in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He has also said he wants to maintain a friendship with Russia and avoid conflict with the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 --Elvan Kivilcim and Chelsey Dulaney contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jared Malsin at jared.malsin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (May 16, 2023).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 16, 2023 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230516ej5g000k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
