\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Turkey \highlight\uc2  Policy  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Fail to Prevent Weaker Lira -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par 11:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0959 GMT - The Turkish lira continues to weaken at a steady pace as a raft of policy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including direct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward price setting by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fail to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The underlying cause of the lira's depreciation remains the same imbalance between Turkey's current account deficit, high inflation and pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates by President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 , \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Tatha Ghose says in a note. "At some point, stop-gap capital control measures may no longer work." USD/TRY rises 0.1% to 19.4085. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT - Goldman Sachs Asset Management sees an uncertain outlook for the U.S. dollar due to high inflation and risks to economic growth and therefore maintains a neutral stance on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, it favors other perceived safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Japanese yen and Swiss franc, on which it has an overweight stance, the asset manager says in a note. "A tight labor market and high inflation alongside downside growth risks creates a nuanced outlook for the U.S. dollar," it says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT - U.K. consumer-price data showed month-to-month momentum in core inflation is still strong, meaning it is hard to see how the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's tightening cycle can be stopped, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Bruna Skarica says in a note. With administrative wage increases in April, and with collective bargaining rounds too, the risks of a June move--on top of a likely May rate hike--are clear, Skarica says. But with 2Q economic-growth and labor-market data likely to be softer than the BOE expects, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  is refraining from calling for a June hike so far, she says. However, today's figures imply that the 2% handle on core inflation will be reached only in the first half of 2024, Skarica adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT - Governments in developed economies are increasing financial support for families to have kids, but this may not be enough to reverse a trend of declining birth rates, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economists say in a note. Birth rates are falling sharply globally, with 2022 experiencing record low readings in China, South Korea, Japan and many European countries, they say. Deteriorating demographics are a major economic headwind as an aging population puts a greater burden on government finances, the economists say. "We suspect financial support may stem the tide of lower birth rates but not be enough to lift them, so governments may have to get more ambitious or creative to turn this around, or the world's population may start shrinking sooner than many projections assume," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT - The euro is likely to remain rangebound versus the dollar until the Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hold their next meetings, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The market is in a "standby-position" at present as it awaits for more clarity on when the Fed and \uc2 ECB\uc2  will reach peak interest rates and the timing of rate cuts, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says in a note. "The issue there is that more detailed information on this matter will only become available in early May when the \uc2 ECB\uc2  board and FOMC will convene for their next meetings." That means EUR/USD may be comfortable in the current range of 1.0920-1.1070 for now, Praefcke says. EUR/USD falls 0.1% to 1.0960. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT - U.K. inflation data, which was in double digits for the seventh consecutive month, makes a \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  rate hike in May seem like a done deal, Daiwa Capital Markets economist Emily Nicol says in a note. Importantly, having risen the prior month, core inflation failed to deliver the expected fall, remaining unchanged at a lofty 6.2% on year, she says. Headline inflation should fall sharply in April, probably close to 8%, due to a favorable base effect associated with last year's extreme hike in household energy bills, Nicol says. But core inflation--particularly in services--raises the risk that next month's hike won't be the last, she adds. (edward.frankl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT - Malaysia's trade performance may weaken in 2023, weighed by a high-base effect and after a lower-than-expected 1Q performance, MIDF Research says in a note. The research house lowers its projection for 2023 exports growth to 6.2% from 9.2% previously, and trims its imports growth estimate to 6.4% from 9.5%. Exports jumped 25.0% for 2022 while imports climbed 31.3%. An expected boost from China's reopening may be delayed to later this year, \uc2 MIDF\uc2  says. \uc2 MIDF\uc2  maintains a cautiously optimistic view that Malaysian trade may continue to grow, but the outlook could be capped by rising inflation, higher borrowing costs and supply-side issues, including geopolitical conflict or trade disruptions, which could further weaken global demand. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT - The U.K. 4.125% January 2027 gilt is in an attractive position on the supply cycle, and Wednesday's tap of the gilt by the Debt Management Office is expected to go well, say \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  analysts in a note. "Given the attractiveness of the bond on various micro-relative value structures, in the context of today's tap being its penultimate one, we think the auction should go very well." There is typically strong demand for bonds from a penultimate tap, the analysts say. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT - Sterling rises on the prospect of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raising interest rates further in May after Wednesday's higher-than-expected U.K. inflation data but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains may remain limited, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. Higher short-term Treasury yields, which are supporting the dollar, will curtail sterling's strength, \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analyst \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2  says in a note. The possibility of a 50 basis points rate rise by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will also limit GBP strength versus EUR although technically EUR/GBP is beginning to look like a move lower is plausible." GBP/USD rises 0.1% to 1.2441 and EUR/GBP falls 0.3% to 0.8804. U.K. annual inflation fell to 10.1% in March from 10.4% in February, above the 9.8% expected by analysts in a WSJ survey. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0747 GMT - The FTSE 100 falls 0.3% to 7885 points as higher-than-forecast U.K. inflation data support the case for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to raise interest rates further. Annual inflation fell to 10.1% in March from 10.4% in February, above the 9.8% expected by analysts in a WSJ survey. "This will likely put pressure on the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to almost reluctantly continue with its interest rate hiking policy in May, adding further pressure to an already beleaguered consumer where wage growth is being outstripped by inflation and with economic growth stalling," \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  analyst Richard Hunter writes. Mining group Antofagasta drops 1.7% after reporting a fall in first-quarter copper production while lower metal prices also weigh on the sector's shares. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT - U.K. gilt yields rise after official data showed March inflation was stronger than forecast, raising expectations that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will raise interest rates further on May 11. U.K. March annual CPI inflation was 10.1%, above the 9.8% consensus by economists in a WSJ poll. Core inflation remained unchanged at 6.2%, above the 5.9% consensus expected. "Given the high print in U.K. inflation, we expect gilts to drive the rates complex higher," write \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  analysts. The 10-year gilt yield last trades at 3.817%, up 8 basis points, while the 2-year gilt yield rises 7 basis points to 3.782%, \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  data show. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0730 GMT - Higher-than-expected U.K. wage growth and inflation data this week mean the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  should raise interest rates in May but that may be the final rate increase, Monex Europe says. Data Wednesday showed inflation eased less than expected in March but there were "still signs of positivity," especially in the \uc2 BOE\uc2 's preferred measures with core services inflation continuing to grow a slower rate than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  anticipated, Monex \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Nick Rees says in a note. "Given the preliminary signs of optimism, which will soon filter through into headline readings, we think another 25 basis points hike by the BOE in May will be the last of this cycle, leaving Bank Rate at a [peak] level of 4.5%." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 19, 2023 05:59 ET (09:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230419ej4j001xk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
