\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Stocks hit five-week low, but Turkish shares rally on trade resumption\b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2023\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Turkish Lira hits record low, stocks rally 9.6%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Polish inflation at 17.2% y/y in January, below forecast\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 South Africa's January inflation slows to 6.9% y/y, as forecast\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stocks down 1.1%, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  down 0.4%\par \par \uc2 By Shubham Batra and Bansari Mayur Kamdar\par \par \uc2 Feb 15 (Reuters) - Stocks in emerging markets fell on Wednesday after U.S. inflation data cemented fears of a more aggressive Federal Reserve, while the Turkish bourse jumped at the open after five days of closure and steep losses in the wake of devastating earthquakes.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI's index of emerging market stocks fell 1.1% to its lowest level since Jan. 9 as markets digested data that showed American consumer prices accelerated in January although at a slower pace.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng index led China stocks lower, with geopolitical concerns and worries about China's economy weighing on investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were down 0.4% at 0904 GMT as the dollar firmed.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's Lira was down 0.2% against the dollar, hitting a record low, while Istanbul's bourse rallied 9.6% on resuming trade after five days of closure.\par \par \uc2 Trading was halted on Turkey's equity and derivatives markets last week in the wake of earthquakes that claimed more than 40,000 lives in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  and neighbouring Syria.\par \par \uc2 "Turkish stocks have benefited from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market we have seen from the government there, all designed to prevent a crash following the closure of the exchange a week ago," said Stuart Cole, head macro economist at Equiti Capital.\par \par \uc2 "So far the policy appears to have worked, judging by the performance at the open."\par \par \uc2 South Africa's rand fell 0.6% after data showed headline consumer inflation slowed to 6.9% year on year in January from 7.2% in December, in line with estimates.\par \par \uc2 Russia's rouble fell to 74.19 against the greenback on the possibility of fresh Western sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty gained 0.4% against the euro, leading gains among central and eastern European peers, after data showed consumer prices inflation rose less than expected.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint gained 0.1% despite the economy slipping into a technical recession, as shown by quarterly growth figures on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu slipped 0.1%, with investors eyeing comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Mugur Is\u259\'01\'03rescu\uc2  who is expected to present the quarterly inflation report later in the day and also release the inflation forecast for 2023 and 2024.\par \par \uc2 Ghana's finance ministry\uc2  on Tuesday said that around 85% of eligible bondholders had registered for its domestic debt exchange programme, bringing it one step closer to securing a $3 billion \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) bailout. (Reporting by Shubham Batra and Bansari Mayur Kamdar in Bengaluru; Editing by David Holmes ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-2-15T10:23:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230215ej2f01imp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
