\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Economic 'shock' coming to Turkey after the earthquake\b0\par\par\uc2 832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 A grim economic outlook will exacerbate the humanitarian catastrophe caused by a pair of earthquakes in Turkey and Syria, as early damage estimates point to rising inflation and budget risks.\par \par \uc2 Turkey has suspended trading on its main stock exchange after a sharp drop and the lira remains underpinned by support measures. Meanwhile, Turkish financial assets whose movements remain unrestricted largely stabilized, with some even appreciating Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are trying to gauge the exact impact of Monday's tremors on Turkey's $819 million economy, which has seen massive imbalances amid unorthodox policies aimed at curbing inflation while cutting interest rates. Falling stock prices and a jump in bond yields after the quake pointed to fears about economic growth and overspending.\par \par \uc2 "The costs of this disaster hit the Turkish economy at a time when sentiment was already fragile," said Nick Stadtmiller, head of product at Medley Global Advisors in New York. It also "increases the risk of another market collapse given pre-existing vulnerabilities in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and external account."\par \par \uc2 While the Istanbul stock market was closed for a second day, the iShares MSCI Turkey UCITS ETF, an exchange-traded fund tracking Turkish assets in Europe that is still trading, rallied after three days of declines. It was 0.5 percent higher at 10:08 a.m. in London after losing 14 percent in the previous three days.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Turkey's new dollar bond maturing in 2033 fell 2 basis points to 9.80 percent Thursday from last week's close of 9.61 percent.\par \par \uc2 The death toll in Turkey and neighboring Syria topped 16,000 as of this Thursday morning, with thousands more trapped inside buildings damaged in the tremors that struck three days earlier. While the immediate focus is on rescue \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the need to make plans for reconstruction ahead of elections scheduled for May is likely to put President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's government under even greater pressure to unveil improved spending plans.\par \par \uc2 Signs of changes in economic policy and what is likely to be a massive increase in spending were already evident Wednesday when Erdogan visited the affected areas. He said a reconstruction blitz in 10 provinces would be completed within a year, announced a 10,000 lira ($531) handout to each affected family, and said survivors housed in tents could be moved to hotels on the coast.\par \par \uc2 "We make an initial rough estimate that public spending on Monday's earthquakes may be equivalent to 5.5 percent of GDP," \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  economist Selva Bahar Baziki wrote. "A likely government-backed credit scheme could result in a higher number," resulting in a breach of budget targets.\par \par \uc2 How much does an earthquake 'cost'?The task of estimating the exact impact of earthquakes is complicated by the region's role in Turkey's economy. On the surface, the 10 provinces most affected by the earthquakes account for a relatively small share of GDP on their own.\par \par \uc2 However, they also form an industrial and agricultural corridor that plays a key role in the prosperity of Istanbul and other larger cities. Oxford Economics said short-term disruptions to activity in the 10 provinces alone will reduce GDP by 0.3 to 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "A relevant precedent for recent natural disasters could be the floods in Pakistan, which may have wiped out 5 to 10 percent of GDP and obviously had a negative impact on external account vulnerabilities," said Hasnain Malik, a strategist at Tellimer in Dubai. "The impact is probably significantly less on Turkey in terms of GDP, but it exacerbates the existing pressure on its external account and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Damage to agricultural productive areas could result in food shortages and increase inflation in a country where the consumer price index hovers just under 60 percent. Erdogan's well-known support for looser monetary policy has left prices unanchored and given Turkey one of the highest inflation rates in the world.\par \par \uc2 Earthquake 'blow' to Turkish liraThe impact of the earthquakes on the lira has so far been muted, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in a narrow range largely managed by the authorities. They have installed support measures and a series of regulations to counter the impact of Erdogan's low interest rate policy on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The lira is not the most accurate barometer of market sentiment toward \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given that it remains underpinned by backdoor \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Piotr Matys, senior analyst at In Touch Capital Markets. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely under even more pressure from the Erdogan administration to cut rates to fund the recovery."\par \par \uc2 Turkey's "blue chip" corporate issuers have also seen their international bonds hold up, as most have diversified their businesses with euro or dollar revenues, according to Todd Schubert, head of fixed income research at \uc2 Bank of Singapore\uc2 . "While the earthquake was indeed a human tragedy, it is not an event that will negatively affect the credit trajectory in a meaningful way," he said.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230209ej290094p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw90\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1350\pichgoal600
