\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Turk Eximbank at 'B'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 2,810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2022\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11 May 2022 10:50 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turk Eximbank at 'B'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 The following is a press release from \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-11 May 2022:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Turkiye Ihracat Kredi Bankasi A.S.'s (\uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  ) Long-Term Foreign- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B' and Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR at 'B+'. Both ratings have a Negative Outlook. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's LTFC and LTLC IDRs are driven by support from the Turkish authorities, if needed. The LTFC IDR is driven by its 'b' Government Support Rating (GSR), reflecting \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of a high government propensity to support the bank in case of need, given its 100%-state ownership and its policy role. The rating also takes into account significant \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2  ) funding and the record of support from the authorities.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR, one notch above its LTFC IDR, is equalised with the sovereign rating on the basis of support, reflecting a higher sovereign ability to provide support and a lower risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in lira, in our view. The Negative Outlooks on both Long-Term IDRs mirror the Turkish sovereign's Rating Outlook.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B' Short-Term IDRs are the only possible option in the 'B' rating category.\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's senior unsecured debt is rated in line with the bank's FC IDRs, reflecting average recoveries for this type of debt.\par \par \uc2 The bank's National Rating at 'AAA(tur)' is driven by government support, as reflected in its LTLC IDR, based on its policy role, state ownership and funding guarantees.\par \par \uc2 Government Support: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's GSR is one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR, despite a high government propensity to provide support, reflecting its fairly large absolute size and volumes of external market funding relative to sovereign resources, given Turkey's weak financial flexibility in FC. The latter is reflected in its weak net FC reserves position, amid increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability and external financing.\par \par \uc2 Operating Environment Pressures: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  is exposed to heightened Turkish operating-environment risks, including inflationary pressures and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. The bank's role as Turkey's credit export agency also exposes it to deterioration in the economies of Turkey's main trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Export Policy Role: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  is the country's credit export agency. Exports are a key pillar in the government's strategy to support growth and reduce its current account deficit. The bank mainly provides short-term trade finance to corporate exporters, mostly through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's low-cost rediscount loan programme, and export credit insurance (the latter risk is to a large extent transferred to reinsurers). The bank also aims to increase export loans to SMEs. In 2021 the bank set up the Export Development Fund (5% shareholder), which provides export guarantees to SMEs.\par \par \uc2 Government Guarantees: The loan book partly benefits from government guarantees for loans issued under the Credit Guarantee Fund (whereby the exposures are typically guaranteed by the Turkish Treasury up to a certain non-performing loan cap). The bank's policy role is reinforced through regulatory privileges, including exemption from corporate taxes and reserve requirements, and access to compensation from the Turkish Treasury for losses incurred in credit, insurance and guarantee transactions due to political risks.\par \par \uc2 Sound Asset Quality: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's asset quality has historically outperformed peers', due to its effective use of credit mitigation whereby loans are covered by local bank guarantees. Its impaired loans and Stage 2 loans ratios were low at 0.2% and 1%, respectively, at end-2021.\par \par \uc2 Moderate Profitability: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's profitability is moderate, underpinned by low-cost funding sourced from the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  , as well as low impairment charges. The bank's operating profit/risk weighted assets ratio increased to 2.8% in 2021 from 1.9% in 2020 on the back of higher net interest income and lower loan impairment charges.\par \par \uc2 Moderate Capitalisation: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's capitalisation is underpinned by sound asset quality and support from the authorities. The bank received a TRY3 billion capital increase in 1Q22, to help offset the sensitivity of capital to lira depreciation. Core capitalisation is moderate (end-2021: 12.95% common equity Tier 1 ratio), and flattered by regulatory forbearance.\par \par \uc2 Rediscount Loans, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Funding: \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's funding is almost entirely FC-denominated and sourced (56% at end-2021) from the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  in the form of low-cost rediscount loans - which in turn underpin Turkey's FC reserves. Remaining borrowings comes from mostly wholesale funding sources and mainly comprise international financial institution (IFI) funding, Eurobonds, syndicated loans and bilateral funding.\par \par \uc2 As is usual for development banks \uc2 Fitch\uc2  does not assign a Viability Rating to \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  . This is because the bank's business model, which is driven by its dedicated policy and development role, is strongly dependent on support from the state, in our view.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's Long-Term IDRs are sensitive to a sovereign downgrade and to \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of an increase in government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. At the 'B' rating level, the bank's LTFC IDR is constrained by our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 A sovereign downgrade is likely to trigger a similar rating action on \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  , particularly if it reflects a further weakening in the sovereign's ability to provide support in FC. A material weakening in \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's policy role could also result in a downgrade of its LTFC IDR, although this is not our base case.\par \par \uc2 The GSR could be revised down if the reliability of support in FC for the bank from the Turkish authorities reduces further.\par \par \uc2 The Short-Term IDRs are sensitive to changes in the Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The bank's senior unsecured debt ratings are primarily sensitive to changes in the IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  's LTLC IDR and in its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's Long-Term IDRs and senior debt ratings is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ratings and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A revision of the sovereign's Outlook to Stable would likely result in a similar action on the bank.\par \par \uc2 Upward revision of the bank's GSR is unlikely given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign ratings and its weak ability to provide support in FC.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The bank's ratings are linked to the Turkish sovereign ratings, as they rely on our assessment of sovereign support.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  , either due to their nature or to the way in which they are being managed by \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  . For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*