\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B', Outlook Negative; Downgrades VR to 'b'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2022\par 21:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11 May 2022 15:27 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B', Outlook Negative; Downgrades VR to 'b'\par \par \uc2 The following is a press release from \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-11 May 2022:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S.'s (TSKB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B' and Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR at 'B+'. The Outlooks are Negative.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also downgraded the bank's Viability Rating (VR) to 'b' from 'b+' and removed it from Rating Watch Negative (RWN). \uc2 Fitch\uc2  placed TSKB's VR on RWN in February 2022 following the Turkish sovereign downgrade (see "\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades 22 Turkish Banks' Ratings; Outlooks Negative" on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The VR downgrade reflects heightened risks to the bank's credit profile, given the concentration of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the volatile and challenging Turkish operating environment. We consider the strength of TSKB's credit profile and capital and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) liquidity buffers to be commensurate with the risk of the Turkish operating environment, given its policy role (providing investment loans and funding projects in areas of strategic importance to the authorities among others) and exceptionally high balance sheet dollarisation.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 TSKB's LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are driven by its 'b' VR, or standalone credit profile. The LTFC IDR is constrained but not capped by our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Operating environment risks, potentially feeding to the bank's financial profile, are the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR is based on government support, reflecting a stronger sovereign ability to provide support and a lower risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in lira. The Negative Outlook on the LTLC IDR reflects that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B' Short-Term (ST) IDRs are the only possible option in the 'B' rating category.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's VR considers its policy role and development bank focus, which underpin its niche franchise and business model, despite its small size (1% of banking sector assets at end-1Q22). It also considers the bank's record of reasonable asset quality and consistent performance. We consider core capitalisation and FC liquidity to be moderate, in view of TSKB's risk profile and predominantly FC-denominated balance sheet amid volatile market conditions. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  downgraded the operating environment score for Turkish banks to 'b'/negative in March 2022, signalling increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, risks to the bank's credit profile remain skewed to the downside, given policy uncertainty in the run-up to the 2023 elections and Turkey's large external financing need amid tighter global financing conditions. Turkish banks are vulnerable to exchange-rate volatility due to refinancing risks, high sector FC lending, given that not all exposures will be fully hedged against lira depreciation, and risks to FC liquidity, due to banks' exposure to external FC wholesale funding amid exposure to investor sentiment and high deposit dollarisation.\par \par \uc2 Below Sector-Average Loan Growth: TSKB has grown below the sector-average every year since end-2018 and has shrunk its loan book on an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted basis since end-2020. This reflects its conservative risk appetite as the operating environment has deteriorated but also capital constraints and subdued demand for long-term FC investment loans given lira weakness. Management guides for further loan book contraction on an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted basis in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Asset Quality Risks: Asset quality risks remain significant for TSKB, given the operating environment pressures, single-name risk (notably in project finance; around 60% of loans at end-2021), material FC lending (93% of gross loans at end-1Q22), as not all borrowers will be fully hedged against the lira depreciation, and exposure to pressured sectors, such as energy lending (a high 45% at end-2021, primarily comprising project finance). However, the loan book contraction in recent years limits seasoning risks, and loans are closely monitored given concentration risk. Energy exposures largely also relate to renewable projects, which are mainly covered by a government-guaranteed feed-in tariff set in FC. The long-term slowly amortising nature of FC lending means asset quality problems should feed through fairly slowly.\par \par \uc2 The bank's impaired loans ratio increased to 3.4% at end-1Q22. Its Stage 2 loans ratio was a fairly high 13%, of which a high 63% was restructured. Average reserves coverage of Stage 2 loans was 19%. The bank's total loan loss allowances covered 156% of Stage 3 loans at end-1Q22.\par \par \uc2 Reasonable Profitability: The bank has historically outperformed the sector in terms of its operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio, underpinned by its reasonable net interest margin, reflecting access to concessional Treasury guaranteed funding and exposure predominantly to FC interest rates, reasonable cost control and manageable but increased loan impairment charges (2021: 57% of pre-impairment operating profit). Profitability in 2022 will be boosted by nominal loan growth and gains on CPI linkers in the high inflation environment, largely offsetting pressure on costs.\par \par \uc2 Weak Core Capitalisation: TSKB's common equity Tier 1 ratio fell to 8.9% (excluding forbearance) at end-2021, representing only a moderate buffer over the regulatory minimum. Core capitalisation is sensitive to lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs) and asset quality weakening, given seasoning risks (including in the restructured and Stage 2 portfolios), high FC lending and concentration risk. However, pre-impairment profit provides a solid buffer to absorb losses through the income statement (2021: equal to about 6% of average loans; adjusted for hedging gains), while impaired loans are fully covered by total reserves.\par \par \uc2 The total capital ratio (14% excluding forbearance at end-1Q22) is also supported by USD200 million additional Tier 1 from its majority owner \uc2 Turkiye Is Bankasi A.S \uc2 . (issued in 1Q22 to replace Tier 2 debt).\par \par \uc2 Wholesale Funded, Adequate FC Liquidity: TSKB is fully wholesale funded, primarily in FC, by development financial institutions (\uc2 DFI\uc2  ) and a significant portion is Treasury guaranteed. Funding maturities are reasonably diversified with well-spaced out repayment schedules mitigating refinancing risks. Nevertheless, FC liquidity is only adequate, given TSKB would rely on FC loan repayments and undrawn \uc2 DFI\uc2  funding to cover short-term maturing FC wholesale borrowings up to one year in case of market closure. FC liquid assets typically comprise mainly deliverable FC swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  and placements with foreign banks.\par \par \uc2 Government Support Rating (GSR) of 'b-': Our view of support for TSKB is based on a high propensity of the Turkish authorities to provide support given the bank's policy role. However, the GSR is two notches below Turkey's LTFC IDR, due to the sovereign's weak financial flexibility to provide support in FC, in case of need, given its weak external finances and weak sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, as well as TSKB's private ownership.\par \par \uc2 The bank's National Rating has been affirmed at 'AA(tur)' on the basis of government support, as reflected in TSKB's LTLC IDR, and considers the bank's policy role, but also its private ownership.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to the bank's standalone creditworthiness or VR. In addition, the ratings are sensitive to an increase in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which currently caps most Turkish banks' LTFC IDRs at 'B', one notch below the sovereign rating, and to a sovereign downgrade.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's VR could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, or a greater than expected deterioration in core capitalisation or asset quality, or a prolonged market closure that puts pressure on TSKB's FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's GSR could be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support for the bank in FC from the Turkish authorities, or in case of a lower sovereign propensity to provide support, for example due to a weakening in the bank's policy role.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's LTLC IDR would be downgraded in case of a sovereign downgrade, if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes the sovereign's propensity to provide support in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reduced or that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking sector in lira has increased.\par \par \uc2 The Short-Term IDRs are sensitive to changes in their respective Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in TSKB's LTLC IDR and creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the bank's LT IDRs and senior debt rating are unlikely in the near term, given the Negative Outlook on the bank, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 A revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would reduce downward pressure on the bank's ratings, particularly if accompanied by a reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and could lead to similar actions on bank ratings.\par \par \uc2 Downside pressure on the bank's VR could ease in case of marked improvement in the operating environment, likely characterised by a reduction in macroeconomic and financial volatility, and potentially also a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable, particularly if this reduces risks to the bank's capital and FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's GSR is unlikely given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign rating and its weak ability to provide support in FC, as reflected in its low net FC reserves.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 11 May 2022 15:27 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B', Outlook -2-\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: Sovereign rating (negative) and macroeconomic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has made an adjustment in its financial spreadsheets for TSKB that has impacted \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's core and complementary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as it believes this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The bank's LTLC IDR is linked to the Turkish sovereign.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Downgrade; b; Rating Watch Off\par \par \uc2 ; Government Support Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1027\par \par \uc2 huseyin.sevinc@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Maria Rodriguez,\par \par \uc2 Analyst\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1802\par \par \uc2 maria.rodriguez2@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Olivia Perney,\par \par \uc2 Managing Director\par \par \uc2 +33 1 44 29 91 74\par \par \uc2 olivia.perney@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.f