\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms Vakifbank at 'B', Outlook Negative; Downgrades VR to 'b'\b0\par\par\uc2 2,346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2022\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 01042022\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi T.A.O\uc2 .'s (\uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 ) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B' and Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR at 'B+'.\par \par \uc2 The Outlooks are Negative.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also downgraded the Viability Rating (VR) to 'b' from 'b+' and removed it from Rating Watch Negative (RWN). \uc2 Fitch\uc2  placed \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's VR on RWN in February 2022 following the Turkish sovereign downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The VR downgrade reflects heightened risks to the bank's credit profile resulting from its concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish market operating environment. As the second-largest systemically important bank in Turkey, we consider the bank's credit profile and strength of its capital and FC liquidity buffers as commensurate with, but not above the risks of the Turkish operating environment. In addition, \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has a high share of FC wholesale funding, exposing it to material refinancing risks, although the risks are somewhat mitigated by the diversification of funding by type and tenor.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are driven by its 'b' VR, or standalone credit profile. At this rating, its LTFC IDR is constrained but not capped by our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's VR reflects the bank's strong domestic franchise (end-2021: 11% of sector assets), reasonable asset quality, moderate capitalisation and profitability and a reasonable funding and liquidity profile. However, it also reflects the bank's concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish operating environment.\par \par \uc2 Risks to the VR remain significant, given Vakif's concentration in the volatile Turkish market, as reflected in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's downgrade of the operating environment score for Turkish banks to 'B'/negative, which signals increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability at the lower sovereign rating level. Exposure to operating environment risks is the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, risks remain skewed to the downside given policy uncertainty in the run-up to the 2023 elections and Turkey's large external financing need amid tighter global financing conditions. Turkish banks are vulnerable to exchange-rate volatility due to refinancing risks, given high exposure to external FC wholesale funding amid exposure to investor sentiment, high sector FC lending, given the impact on asset quality, and deposit dollarisation, due to risks to FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's common equity Tier 1 (CET1)/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio was 10.0% at end-2021 (8.1% net of forbearance) representing a limited buffer over the regulatory minimum and to absorb unexpected shocks. The total capital ratio (14.7%, including forbearance) was higher and includes FC subordinated Tier 2 debt and FC additional Tier 1 capital, which provide a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 The bank has since received a TRY13.4 billion CET1 increase (about +250bp uplift to capital ratios) from the Turkish authorities (March 2022). We factor in ordinary capital support in our assessment of \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's capitalisation based on its record of support, including TRY7 billion of CET1 in 2020 and EUR700 million of additional Tier 1 in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is also supported by moderate pre-impairment operating profitability (2021: equal to 2.3% of average gross loans), full total reserves coverage of non-performing loans (NPLs) and limited free provisions (equal to about 32bp of RWAs).\par \par \uc2 However, leverage is high (end-2021: equity/assets ratio of 5.6%), and has increased, while downside risks to capitalisation remain significant from lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs), although the risks are somewhat mitigated by regulatory forbearance on RWAs, and asset-quality pressures given credit concentrations. Consequently, we consider capitalisation to be only adequate.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  is exposed to refinancing risks given its high, but falling share of FC wholesale funding exposure (over 20% of total funding), the highest among large bank peers. These risks are somewhat mitigated by the diversification of funding by instrument and tenor and the bank has a solid record of external market access despite challenging market conditions. Significant deposit dollarisation also creates risks to FC liquidity in case of outflows. However, at end-2021 the bank's available FC liquidity (USD6.4 billion) covered maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months (USD3.9 billion, bank-only basis; excluding interbank deposits) plus about a tenth of FC customer deposits.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability. FC liquidity also largely comprises of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2 , access to which could become uncertain at times of systemic stress or material FC deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's reported NPL ratio improved to 3.1% at end-2021 (end-2020: 4.0%), despite operating-environment pressures, reflecting loan growth and collections. We expect the NPL ratio to rise moderately in 2022 given risks to macro and financial stability and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Asset quality pressures are heightened by seasoning risks, exposure to SMEs (end-2021: 25% of loans) and unsecured retail borrowers (16%), which are sensitive to the growth environment and unemployment, respectively, and the transportation (13%), construction (11%), energy (7%), real-estate (4%) and tourism (4%) sectors. Stage 2 loans are also fairly high (12% of loans, a third restructured).\par \par \uc2 While FC lending (end-2021: 37% of loans) is slightly lower than at private bank peers, it is significant and heightens risks given that not all borrowers are fully hedged against lira depreciation, albeit the risks are somewhat mitigated by exposures generally being to large, diversified corporates or exporters with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or project-finance loans under government feed-in tariff (energy loans) or with revenue- or debt-assumption guarantees.\par \par \uc2 Total reserve coverage of NPLs increased to 139% at end-2021, a level that compares well with large bank peers, as \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  increased provisioning in response to heightened market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's operating profit/RWAs ratio decreased to 1.0% in 2021 (1.8% in 2020; including some uplift from forbearance on RWAs), significantly below the sector average, mainly reflecting margin pressure (due to the origination of fixed-term low yielding loans, including Credit Guarantee Fund) and loan impairment charges (equal to a 51% of pre-impairment operating profit). The bank reports an average cost of funding above large private bank peers due to its higher share of lira deposits and lower share of demand deposits.\par \par \uc2 We expect \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's performance to improve in 2022, given lower funding costs following the lira interest rate cuts (4Q21) and the bank's short-term negative repricing gap, which should support a widening of its net interest margin. The inflationary environment should also support loan growth and revenues, including CPI linked gains, offsetting pressure on costs. Impairments could moderate in 2022, although there are downside risks given risks to macro stability.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has a large, contractually short term but stable, granular deposit base (end-2021: 60% of total funding; 11% market share), and an above peer-average (large banks) share of lira deposits, reflecting its solid franchise and state-related deposits (14%). FC deposits are nevertheless high (58%). \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's loans/deposits ratio underperforms peers, due to its high share of wholesale funding (40% of total funding; over half in FC).\par \par \uc2 ENVIRONMENT, SOCIAL AND GOVERNANCE SCORES\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to typical Relevance Scores of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over the board's effectiveness and management strategy in the challenging Turkish operating environment. The latter has a negative impact on the banks' credit profiles and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT SUPPORT RATING (GSR)\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's 'b-' GSR is two notches below Turkey's LTFC IDR, despite the bank's state ownership, policy role, systemic importance, state-related funding and the record of capital support, due to the sovereign's weak financial flexibility to provide support in FC, in case of need, given its weak external finances and weak sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR is equalised with the sovereign rating on the basis of support, reflecting a stronger sovereign ability to provide support in LC. The Negative Outlook reflects that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating has been downgraded to 'CCC+'/RR6 from 'B-'/RR5 and removed from RWN. The notes are notched down twice from \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's VR anchor rating, reflecting our expectation of poor recoveries in case of default.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs and senior debt ratings are primarily sensitive to the bank's standalone creditworthiness or VR. In addition, the ratings are sensitive to an increase in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which currently caps most Turkish banks' LTFC IDRs one notch below the sovereign rating, and to a sovereign downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment. It could also be downgraded in case of a material erosion in the bank's FC liquidity buffer, for example due to a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability, or in the bank's capital buffer.\par \par \uc2 The bank's GSR could be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes stress in Turkey's external finances materially further reduces the reliability of sovereign support for \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  in FC.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's VR.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the bank's LT IDRs and senior debt rating are unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would reduce downward pressure on the bank's ratings, particularly if accompanied by a reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and could lead to similar actions on bank ratings.\par \par \uc2 Downside pressure on the bank's VR could ease in case of marked improvement in the operating environment, likely characterised by a reduction in macroeconomic and financial volatility, and potentially also a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable, particularly if this reduces risks to the bank's capital and FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's GSR is unlikely given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign rating and its weak ability to provide support in FC, as reflected in its low net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 'The operating environment score of 'b' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: Sovereign rating (negative) and macroeconomic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 The business profile score of 'b+' has been assigned below the implied 'bb' category implied score, due to the following adjustment reason: business model (negative). This reflects the bank's business model concentration on the high-risk Turkish market.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has ratings linked to the Turkish sovereign rating, given the ratings either rely on or are sensitive to our assessment of sovereign support or country risks.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to typical Relevance Scores of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over the board's effectiveness and management strategy in the challenging Turkish operating environment. The latter has a negative impact on the banks' credit profiles and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 RATING ACTIONS\par \par \uc2 Entity / Debt\par \par \uc2 Rating\par \par \uc2 Recovery\par \par \uc2 Prior\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi T.A.O\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LT IDR\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 ST IDR\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 LC LT IDR\par \par \uc2 B+\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B+\par \par \uc2 LC ST IDR\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Natl LT\par \par \uc2 AA(tur)\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 AA(tur)\par \par \uc2 Viability\par \par \uc2 b\par \par \uc2 Downgrade\par \par \uc2 b+\par \par \uc2 Government Support\par \par \uc2 b-\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 b-\par \par \uc2 senior unsecured\par \par \uc2 LT\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 RR4\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 subordinated\par \par \uc2 LT\par \par \uc2 CCC+\par \par \uc2 Downgrade\par \par \uc2 RR6\par \par \uc2 B-\par \par \uc2 senior unsecured\par \par \uc2 ST\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Page\par \par \uc2 of 1\par \par \uc2 VIEW ADDITIONAL RATING DETAILS\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020220404ei440004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
