\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Ziraat at 'B', Outlook Negative; Downgrades VR to 'b'\b0\par\par\uc2 3,984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par 17:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1 Apr 2022 11:34 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2   Affirms Ziraat at 'B', Outlook Negative; Downgrades VR to 'b'\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-01 April 2022: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Turkiye Cumhuriyeti Ziraat Bankasi A.S.'s (Ziraat) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B' and Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR at 'B+'. The Outlooks are Negative.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also downgraded the bank's Viability Rating (VR) to 'b' from 'b+' and removed it from Rating Watch Negative (RWN). \uc2 Fitch\uc2  placed Ziraat's VR on RWN in February 2022 following the Turkish sovereign downgrade (see "\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades 22 Turkish Banks' Ratings; Outlooks Negative" on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The VR downgrade reflects heightened risks to the bank's credit profile resulting from its concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish market. As the largest state-owned bank in Turkey, we consider the bank's credit profile and strength of its capital and FC liquidity buffers to be commensurate with but not above the risk of the Turkish operating environment, notwithstanding its significant franchise.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also affirmed the support-driven Long-Term IDRs of Ziraat's Islamic bank subsidiary, Ziraat Katilim Bankasi A.S. (ZKB ), in line with Ziraat's.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 LTFC IDR, VR, SENIOR DEBT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are driven by its 'b' VR, or standalone credit profile. At this rating level, its LTFC IDR is constrained, but not capped, by our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 The VR reflects the bank's market leading position, including the highest deposit market share in the sector, which underpins its business model and strong domestic franchise (end-2021: 15% of sector assets). It also considers the bank's reasonable asset quality, moderate capitalisation and profitability and reasonable FC liquidity for its risk profile.\par \par \uc2 Risks to the VR remain significant, given Ziraat's concentration in the volatile Turkish market, as reflected in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's downgrade of the operating environment score for Turkish banks to 'b'/negative, which signals increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability at the lower sovereign rating level. Exposure to operating environment risks are the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, risks remain skewed to the downside, given policy uncertainty in the run-up to the 2023 elections and Turkey's large external financing need amid tighter global financing conditions. Turkish banks are vulnerable to exchange-rate volatility due to refinancing risks, given high exposure to external FC wholesale funding amid exposure to investor sentiment, high sector FC lending, given the impact on asset quality, and deposit dollarisation, due to risks to FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's reported common equity Tier 1 (CET1)/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio was 12% at end-2021, or 9.6% net of forbearance, representing only a moderate buffer over the regulatory minimum. The total capital ratio - including FC additional Tier 1 capital, which provides a partial hedge against lira depreciation - was stronger at 15.7%. However, the bank has since received a TRY21.8 billion CET1 increase (about +200bp uplift to capital ratios without forbearance) from the Turkish authorities in March 2022. We factor in ordinary capital support in our assessment of Ziraat's capitalisation based on its record of support, including TRY7 billion of CET1 in 2020 and EUR1.4 billion of additional Tier 1 in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is supported by reasonable pre-impairment operating profitability (2021: equal to 3.7% of average gross loans), full total reserves coverage of non-performing loans (NPLs) and fairly limited free provisions (equal to about 40bp of RWAs).\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, leverage is fairly high and has increased (end-2021: equity/assets ratio of 6.8%), while downside risks remain significant from lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs), although the risks are somewhat mitigated by regulatory forbearance on RWA, and asset quality pressures. Consequently, we consider the bank's capital buffer to be only moderate.\par \par \uc2 Ziraat is exposed to refinancing risks, given its fairly high, but falling, share of FC wholesale funding exposure (including FC bank deposits; end-2021: 14% of total funding). Significant deposit dollarisation also creates risks to FC liquidity in case of outflows. Nevertheless, the bank has a record of external market access despite challenging market conditions. At end-2021, its available FC liquidity (USD12 billion, mainly comprising FC swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  ; \uc2 CBRT\uc2  ) covered maturing FC debt due within 12 months (USD7 billion) plus about a moderate share of FC customer deposits.\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability. FC liquidity also largely comprises \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  , access to which could become uncertain at times of systemic stress or material FC deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's asset quality metrics (end-2021: NPL ratio of 2.1%; consolidated basis) have historically outperformed peer and sector averages, underpinned by good-quality, largely subsidized agro lending (end-2021: 15%), residential mortgages (11%), Credit Guarantee Fund loans (5%), which carry a Turkish treasury guarantee typically up to a 7% NPL cap and lending to payroll borrowers and pensioners.\par \par \uc2 We expect the NPL ratio to rise moderately in 2022 (although restructured loans could also rise) amid risks to macro and financial stability and high inflation. Stage 2 loans are also fairly high (10%; two-thirds restructured) and concentrated. Risks are heightened by the bank's exposure to SMEs (end-2021: 36%) and the construction and real estate (20%), energy (8%) and tourism (4%) sectors, and seasoning risks. High foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) lending (34%; sector: 41%) also increases risks, given that not all borrowers are fully hedged against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 These risks are somewhat mitigated by the fact that exposures are generally to large, diversified corporates or exporters with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or project finance loans carrying a government feed-in tariff (energy loans) or revenue or debt assumption guarantee. Total reserve coverage of NPLs increased to 163% at end-2021 (end-2020: 126%) partly reflecting higher average reserve coverage of Stage 2 loans (18%).\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's operating profit/RWAs ratio decreased to 1.2% in 2021 (1.6% in 2020), reflecting margin pressure (due to the origination of fixed-term low yielding loans, including CGF) and loan impairment charges (equal to a high 61% of pre-impairment operating profit).\par \par \uc2 We expect its performance to improve in 2022, given lower funding costs following the lira interest rate cuts (4Q21) and the bank's short-term negative repricing gap, which should support a widening of the net interest margin. The inflationary environment should also support loan growth and revenues, including CPI linked gains, offsetting pressure on costs. Impairments could also moderate in 2022, although there are downside risks given macro stability.\par \par \uc2 Ziraat has a sizeable, granular and stable (but contractually short-term) deposit base (end-2021: 74% of total funding), reflecting its leading franchise and fairly large public-sector deposits (10% of customer deposits). It reports a solid loans/deposits of 88% (consolidated basis), which decreased in 2021 due to rapid deposit growth, in part inflated by lira depreciation (2021: 51%; 11% \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted), given high FC deposits (61%, broadly in line with the sector average). We expect dollarisation to remain high notwithstanding the recently introduced \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -protected deposit scheme.\par \par \uc2 ENVIRONMENT, SOCIAL AND GOVERNANCE SCORES\par \par \uc2 Ziraat and ZKB have an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to typical Relevance Scores of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over the boards' effectiveness and management strategy in the challenging Turkish operating environment. This has a negative impact on the banks' credit profiles and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT SUPPORT RATING (GSR), LTLC IDR\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's 'b-' GSR is two notches below Turkey's LTFC IDR, despite the high propensity of the Turkish authorities to provide support based on the bank's state ownership, policy role, systemic importance, state-related funding and the record of capital support, due to the sovereign's weak financial flexibility to provide support in FC, in case of need, given its weak external finances and weak sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR is equalised with the sovereign rating on the basis of support, reflecting a stronger sovereign ability to provide support in LC. The Negative Outlook reflects that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of Ziraat's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers' is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs and senior debt ratings are primarily sensitive to the bank's standalone creditworthiness or VR. In addition, the ratings are sensitive to an increase in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which currently caps most Turkish banks' LTFC IDRs one notch below the sovereign rating, and to a sovereign downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment. It could also be downgraded in case of a material erosion in the bank's FC liquidity buffer, for example due to a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability, or in the bank's capital buffer.\par \par \uc2 The bank's GSR could be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes stress in Turkey's external finances materially further reduces the reliability of sovereign support for Ziraat in FC.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in Ziraat's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 1 Apr 2022 11:34 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2   Affirms Ziraat at 'B', -2-\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the bank's LT IDRs and senior debt rating are unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would reduce downward pressure on the bank's ratings, particularly if accompanied by a reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and could lead to similar actions on bank ratings.\par \par \uc2 Downside pressure on the bank's VR could ease in case of marked improvement in the operating environment, likely characterised by a reduction in macroeconomic and financial volatility, and potentially also a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable, particularly if this reduces risks to the bank's capital and FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's GSR is unlikely given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign rating and its weak ability to provide support in FC, as reflected in its low net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARIES & AFFILIATES: KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 ZKB 's ratings are equalised with its parent's, reflecting its strategic importance to, and integration with, the group and common branding between the two entities. ZKB 's Shareholder Support Rating has also been affirmed at 'b'\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARIES AND AFFILIATES: RATING SENSITIVITIES Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 ZKB 's ratings are sensitive to adverse changes in its parent's Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 ZKB 's ratings are sensitive to positive changes in its parent's Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: Sovereign rating (negative) and macroeconomic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 The business profile score of 'b+' has been assigned below the implied 'bb' category implied score, due to the following adjustment reason: business model (negative). This reflects the bank's business model concentration on the high-risk Turkish market.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 Ziraat's ratings are linked to the Turkish sovereign ratings and ZKB 's ratings are linked to those of its parent bank.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Ziraat and ZKB have an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to typical Relevance Scores of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over the boards' effectiveness and management strategy in the challenging Turkish operating environment. This has a negative impact on the banks' credit profiles and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 ZKB 's ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure also takes into account the bank's status as an Islamic bank. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and activities need to comply with sharia principles and rules, which entails additional costs, processes, disclosures, regulations, reporting and sharia audit. This has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Islamic banks have an exposure to social impacts relevance score of '3' (in contrast to a typical ESG relevance score of '2' for comparable conventional banks), which reflects that Islamic banks have certain sharia limitations embedded in their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and obligations, although this only has a minimal credit impact on Islamic banks.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Cumhuriyeti Ziraat Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook