\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 18:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 31 Mar 2022 12:40 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-31 March 2022: LEAD PARAGRAPH \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Yapi ve Kredi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .'s (YKB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B' with a Negative Outlook. The agency has also affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+' and removed it from Rating Watch Negative (RWN). The bank's Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is driven by its VR, has also been affirmed at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the VR, one notch above YKB's LTFC IDR, and the removal from RWN reflect the bank's solid FC liquidity buffer and that transfer, convertibility and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks are captured in its LTFC IDR but not in its VR.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  placed YKB's VR on RWN in February 2022 following the downgrade of the Turkish sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 LTFC IDR, SENIOR DEBT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 YKB's LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are driven by the bank's VR but capped at 'B', one notch below \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's LTFC IDR. This reflects our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks and our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, amid high sector deposit dollarisation and short-term maturing FC debt in volatile market conditions, make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its FC obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The Negative Outlook on the LTFC IDR mirrors that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR, which is also driven by its standalone creditworthiness, as expressed by its VR, is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting a lower risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in LC. The Negative Outlook on the LT IDRs mainly reflects risks to the bank's standalone credit profile amid heightened operating environment pressures, but also considers the sovereign Outlook.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of YKB's National Rating reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC, relative to other Turkish issuers, is unchanged. The Negative Outlook on the National Rating reflects that on the LTLC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Driver 1\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the VR, one notch above its LTFC IDR and the operating environment score, despite heightened operating environment pressures, reflects the bank's generally reasonable financial metrics, underpinned by its reasonable domestic franchise (end-2021: 8% of sector assets) and solid FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Risks to the bank's credit profile remain significant, given its concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish market. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has downgraded the operating environment score for Turkish banks to 'b'/negative signalling increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability at the lower sovereign rating level. Turkish banks are vulnerable to exchange-rate volatility due to refinancing risks, given high exposure to external FC wholesale funding in a market highly sensitive to investor sentiment, high sector FC lending, given the impact on asset quality, and deposit dollarisation, due to risks to FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 YKB is exposed to refinancing risks, given its high share of FC wholesale funding (end-2021: 25% of total funding) amid exposure to investor sentiment in volatile market conditions. Significant deposit dollarisation also creates risks to FC liquidity in case of outflows. Nevertheless, the bank has a record of external market access, despite challenging market conditions. Furthermore, available FC liquidity (USD10.1billion) covered the bank's maturing FC debt over 12 months plus about a third of FC deposits at end-2021.\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or deposit instability. In addition, FC liquidity largely comprises of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  , access to which could become uncertain at times of systemic stress or material FC deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 YKB's reported common equity Tier 1 (CET1)/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio was an adequate 13% at end-2021 (or 11.0% net of forbearance), representing a moderate buffer over the regulatory minimum. This included a 59bp uplift as a result of the initial phase-in of IRB implementation. The total capital ratio was stronger at 17.6% (15.0% net of forbearance), mainly reflecting additional Tier 1 and FC subordinated Tier 2 debt, which provide a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is supported by solid pre-impairment operating profitability (2021: equal to 5.3% of average gross loans, adjusted for hedging gains) and solid total reserves coverage of non-performing loans (NPLs; 151%; peer average: 137%), underpinned by the highest total provisioning/gross loans ratio among large privately-owned peers (6.9%; peer average: 5.6%).\par \par \uc2 Downside risks remain from lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs - although the risks are partly mitigated by regulatory forbearance) - and asset-quality pressures, given credit concentrations.\par \par \uc2 YKB's NPL ratio improved to 4.6% at end-2021 (end-2020: 6.2%) but was above the sector average of 3.2% and at the high end of the range for large bank peers. It improved despite operating environment pressures, reflecting strong collections and write-offs but also flattered by nominal loan growth. We expect the NPL ratio to rise moderately in 2022, although asset quality risks remain significant given macro uncertainty, high, lumpy Stage 2 loans (end-2021: 15% of gross loans, 19% reserves coverage; of which 56% restructured), exposure to the construction and real estate (10%; including infrastructure projects) and tourism (3%) sectors, and also unsecured retail borrowers (20%; including a high share of salaried borrowers), given sensitivity to rising unemployment, among others. Risks in energy sector lending (a fairly high 13%), are partly mitigated by government feed-in tariffs set in US dollars (renewable energy) and the current increase in energy prices.\par \par \uc2 A material share of FC lending (41% at end-2021, despite deleveraging), albeit broadly in line with the sector heightens risks, given that not all borrowers will be fully hedged against lira depreciation. Credit risk is partly mitigated by the fact that exposures are generally to large, diversified corporates and exporters with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or comprise project finance loans carrying a feed-in tariff (energy) or revenue or debt assumption guarantee.\par \par \uc2 Total reserve coverage of NPLs increased to 151% (end-2020: 126%), partly reflecting higher reserve coverage of Stage 2 loans (an above peers 19%).\par \par \uc2 YKB's operating profit/RWAs ratio improved to 3.1% in 2021, reflecting solid margins and sound cost control but also regulatory forbearance on risk-weighted assets. YKB's performance in 2022 will be supported by lower funding costs following the lira rate cuts, given its short-term negative repricing gap and the inflationary environment, which should support loan and fee volumes and CPI linked gains. However, inflation will pressure costs, while macro uncertainty could lead to impairment growth. LICs absorbed 43% of pre-impairment operating profit in 2021.\par \par \uc2 YKB has a large, granular and stable, but contractually short-term, deposit base (end-2021: 64% of total funding; 8% market share), with a high share of demand deposits (43%), which supports its below sector average cost of funding. Its loans/deposits ratio (108%) slightly underperforms peers but has improved with deposit growth (albeit partly reflecting lira depreciation). FC deposits were a high 66% of total deposits at end-2021, although this is broadly in line with private bank peers. We expect dollarisation to remain high, given its high base, despite the recently introduced \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -protected deposit scheme.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT SUPPORT RATING (GSR)\par \par \uc2 YKB's GSR has been affirmed at 'ns', notwithstanding its systemic importance, reflecting our view that support from the Turkish authorities, in case of need, cannot be relied upon given the sovereign's weak financial flexibility.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B-' subordinated debt rating has been affirmed and removed from RWN. The notes are notched once from its LTFC IDR anchor rating of 'B' rather than the VR, since \uc2 Fitch\uc2  considers this to be the most appropriate measure of non-performance risk (given government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk), reflecting reduced loss severity given that the main risk is to timely payment rather than recoveries.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to a sovereign downgrade and also to any increase in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. YKB's LTLC IDR would also be downgraded if its VR was downgraded.\par \par \uc2 YKB's VR is primarily sensitive to a sovereign downgrade. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  would downgrade the VR to the level of the LTFC IDR if there was material erosion in the bank's capital and FC liquidity buffers. The VR could also be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, including a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability that severely eroded the bank's FC liquidity buffer.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the bank's LT IDRs and senior debt rating are unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would reduce downward pressure on the bank's ratings, particularly if accompanied by a reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and could lead to similar action on the bank's ratings.\par \par \uc2 Downside pressure on the VR could ease in case of a marked improvement in the operating environment, likely characterised by a reduction in macroeconomic and financial volatility, and potentially also a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable, particularly if this reduces risks to capital and FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 31 Mar 2022 12:40 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B'; Outlook -2-\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's GSR is unlikely given the Negative Outlook on the sovereign rating and Turkey's weak ability to provide support in FC, as reflected in its low net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in the anchor rating (in case of a downgrade of the VR), and is also sensitive to a change in notching should \uc2 Fitch\uc2  change its assessment of loss severity and/or relative non-performance risk.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in YKB's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: Sovereign rating (negative) and macroeconomic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 The business profile score of 'b+' has been assigned below the implied 'bb' category implied score, due to the following adjustment reason: business model (negative). This reflects the bank's business model concentration on the high-risk Turkish market.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 Criteria Variation\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk means we view default risk of the bank as higher than that of the Turkey sovereign. As a result, we have capped the LTFC IDR at 'B', one notch below Turkey's sovereign rating. As such, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has varied its criteria to permit a VR one notch above the bank's LTFC IDR as this is not directly capped by Turkey's Country Ceiling of 'B+', but rather our broader view of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Yapi ve Kredi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; A+(tur); Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+; Rating Watch Off\par \par \uc2 ; Government Support Ratin