\par\par \b \uc2 Press Release: Fitch Affirms Akbank and Akbank AG at 'B'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,958 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 18:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-31 March 2022: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  has affirmed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Akbank T.A.S \f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (Akbank) Long-Term Foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B' with a Negative Outlook. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2  has also affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+' and removed it from Rating Watch Negative (RWN). The bank's Long-Term Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (LTLC) IDR has also been affirmed at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the VR, one notch above Akbank's LTFC IDR, and the removal from RWN reflect the bank's solid FC liquidity buffer and that transfer, convertibility and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks are captured in its LTFC IDR but not in its VR. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  placed Akbank's VR on RWN in February 2022 following the Turkish sovereign downgrade (see "\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades 22 Turkish Banks' Ratings; Outlooks Negative" on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 ), reflecting heightened risks to its standalone credit profile amid market volatility.\par \par \uc2 The support-driven ratings of Akbank AG have been affirmed, in line with the LTFC IDR of its 100% shareholder, Akbank.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 LT IDRS AND SENIOR DEBT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 Akbank's LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are capped at 'B', one notch below \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's LTFC IDR. This reflects our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk and our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, amid high sector deposit dollarisation and high short-term maturing FC debt in volatile market conditions, make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its FC obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The Negative Outlook on the LTFC IDR mirrors that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B+' LTLC IDR, which is driven by its VR, is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in our view. The Negative Outlook on the LTLC IDR mainly reflects risks to the bank's standalone credit profile amid heightened operating environment pressures, but also considers the sovereign outlook.\par \par \uc2 Akbank's senior debt ratings are aligned with its respective IDRs and reflect average recovery prospects in case of default.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of Akbank's 'b+' VR, one notch above its LTFC IDR and the operating environment score, reflects the bank's record of resilient financial metrics underpinned by its solid domestic franchise (end-2021: 8% of sector assets and deposits) and significant FC liquidity and capital buffers.\par \par \uc2 Risks to the bank's credit profile remain significant, given its concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish market. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has downgraded the operating environment score for Turkish banks to 'b'/negative signalling increased risks to macroeconomic and financial stability at the lower sovereign rating level. Turkish banks are vulnerable to exchange-rate volatility due to refinancing risks, given high exposure to external FC wholesale funding in a market highly sensitive to investor sentiment, high sector FC lending, given the impact on asset quality, and deposit dollarisation, due to risks to FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Akbank is exposed to refinancing risks, given its high, but falling share of FC wholesale funding (end-2021: 22% of total funding). Significant deposit dollarisation also creates risks to FC liquidity in case of outflows. Nevertheless, the bank has a record of external market access, despite challenging market conditions. Furthermore, available FC liquidity (USD12.6 billion) covered the bank's maturing FC debt over 12 months (USD2.7 billion; excluding FC repos) plus a fairly high share FC deposits at end-2021. Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability. In addition, as for the sector, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes FC liquidity largely comprises \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  , access to which could become uncertain at times of systemic stress or material FC deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 Akbank's reported common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio (16.2%) was the highest among the peer group at end-2021 and represents a solid buffer over the regulatory minimum, including net of regulatory forbearance (12.9%). Leverage remains reasonable - Akbank reports the lowest ratio among large bank peers - despite a fall in the equity/assets ratio (end-2021: 10.0%). Capitalisation is supported by strong pre-impairment operating profitability (equal to about 6% of average gross loans in 2021, adjusted for hedging gains), moderate reserves coverage of non-performing loans (NPLs) and limited free provisions (equal to about 30bp of risk-weighted assets; RWAs) against possible losses. Nevertheless, downside risks remain from lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs - albeit the risks are somewhat mitigated by regulatory forbearance) - and asset-quality pressures.\par \par \uc2 Akbank's NPL ratio fell to 4.4% at end-2021 (end-2020: 6.1%), a level broadly in line with private bank peers, despite operating environment pressures, primarily reflecting nominal loan growth, strong collections and write-offs. We expect this ratio to rise moderately in 2022, given risks to macroeconomic and financial stability, high inflation, and credit concentrations. FC lending also remains high (end-2021: 40% of loans; sector: 42%) despite deleveraging efforts (FC loans reduced 27% in US dollar terms between 2018-2021), heightening credit risks, given that not all borrowers will be fully hedged against lira depreciation. The risks are somewhat mitigated by exposures generally being to large, diversified corporates and exporters with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or project finance loans carrying a government feed-in tariff (energy loans) or revenue or debt assumption guarantee.\par \par \uc2 Stage 2 loans (end-2021: 9.7%; about two-thirds restructured) are moderate and at the lower end of the private banks' peer group. Abank's total reserve coverage of NPLs remains below peer and sector averages, despite having increased (end-2021: 104%; end-2020: 95%), mainly reflecting lower coverage of Stage 2 (14.0%) and Stage 1 (0.5%) loans.\par \par \uc2 Akbank's operating profit/RWAs ratio (3.5% in 2021; 2.3% in 2020) consistently outperforms the large Turkish banks' peer group average, reflecting good margins and sound cost efficiency. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects Akbank to report strong profitability metrics in 2022, given its short-term negative repricing gap and the lira interest rate cuts (in 4Q21), while the inflationary environment should support loan volumes and CPI-linked securities income, although costs will also rise. The bank is budgeting for a moderate loan impairment charges/gross loan ratio of about 100bp in 2022 (excluding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impacts) but the uncertain macroeconomic outlook creates downside risks.\par \par \uc2 Akbank is primarily funded by granular, retail deposits including a reasonable share of demand deposits, and its gross loans/deposits ratio fell to a reasonable 94% at end-2021, partly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation inflating reported FC deposits. However, the share of FC deposits is significant (66%, consolidated basis), although broadly in line with the sector, increasing FC liquidity risks in case of deposit instability. We expect dollarisation to remain high, notwithstanding the recently introduced \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -protected deposit scheme.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT SUPPORT RATING (GSR)\par \par \uc2 Akbank's GSR has been affirmed at 'ns', notwithstanding its systemic importance. This reflects our view that support from the Turkish authorities in FC, in case of need, cannot be relied upon given the sovereign's weak financial flexibility.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of Akbank's National Rating reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged. The Negative Outlook on the National Rating reflects that on the LTLC IDR.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The bank's 'B-' subordinated debt rating has been affirmed and removed from RWN. The notes are notched once from its LTFC IDR anchor rating of 'B' rather than the VR, since \uc2 Fitch\uc2  considers this to be the most appropriate measure of non-performance risk (given government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk), reflecting reduced loss severity given that the main risk is to timely payment rather than recoveries.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 Akbank's LT IDRs and senior debt ratings are primarily sensitive to a sovereign downgrade and also to any increase in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. Akbank's LTLC IDR would be downgraded if its VR was downgraded.\par \par \uc2 Akbank's VR is primarily sensitive to a sovereign downgrade. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  would downgrade the VR to the level of the LTFC IDR in case of a material erosion in the bank's capital or FC liquidity buffers. The VR could also be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, including a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability that severely eroded the bank's FC liquidity buffer.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in Akbank's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in Akbank's anchor rating (in case of a downgrade of the bank's VR), and is also sensitive to a change in notching should \uc2 Fitch\uc2  change its assessment of loss severity and/or relative non-performance risk.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the bank's LT IDRs and senior debt rating are unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks, the Negative Outlook on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would reduce downward pressure on the bank's ratings, particularly if accompanied by a reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and could lead to similar actions on the bank's ratings.\par \par \uc2 31 Mar 2022 12:55 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Akbank and Akbank AG -2-\par \par \uc2 Downside pressure on the bank's VR could ease in case of marked improvement in the operating environment, likely characterised by a reduction in macroeconomic and financial volatility, and potentially also a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable, particularly if this reduces risks to the bank's capital and FC liquidity.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's GSR is unlikely, given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign rating and its weak ability to provide support in FC, as reflected in its low net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in Akbank's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in Akbank's anchor rating (in case of a downgrade of the bank's VR), and is also sensitive to a change in notching should \uc2 Fitch\uc2  change its assessment of loss severity and/or relative non-performance risk.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARIES & AFFILIATES: KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 AKBANK AG\par \par \uc2 Akbank AG's ratings are equalised with those of its parent, reflecting its important role in, and integration with the Akbank group. Akbank AG's Shareholder Support Rating (SSR) has also been affirmed at 'b'.\par \par \uc2 Akbank AG's Deposit Ratings are in line with its support-driven IDRs as, in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's opinion, its debt buffers do not afford any obvious incremental probability of default benefit over and above the support benefit factored into the bank's IDRs.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARIES AND AFFILIATES: RATING SENSITIVITIES Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 Akbank AG's ratings are sensitive to a change in its parent's LTFC IDR. Negative rating action on Akbank's LTFC IDR would lead to similar action on Akbank AG.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Positive rating action on Akbank's LTFC IDR would lead to similar action on Akbank AG.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reasons: Sovereign rating (negative) and macroeconomic stability (negative). The latter adjustment reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 The business profile score of 'b+' has been assigned below the implied 'bb' category implied score, due to the following adjustment reason: business model (negative). This reflects the bank's business model concentration on the high-risk Turkish market.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 Criteria Variation\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's view of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk means we view default risk of the bank as higher than that of the Turkey sovereign. As a result, we have capped the LTFC IDR at 'B', one notch below Turkey's sovereign rating. As such, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has varied its criteria to permit a VR one notch above the bank's LTFC IDR as this is not directly capped by Turkey's Country Ceiling of 'B+', but rather our broader view of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's financial spreadsheets that has affected the bank's core and complimentary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2  's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Akbank AG; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Shareholder Support Ratings; Affirmed; b\par \par \uc2 ----long-term deposits; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ----short-term deposits; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 AKBANK T.A.S\uc2  .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; A+(tur); Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+; Rating Watch Off\par \par \uc2 ; Government Support Ratings; Affirmed; ns\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ----subordinated; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B-; Rating Watch Off\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Markus Glabach,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +49 69 768076 195\par \par \uc2 markus.glabach@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -- a branch of Fitch Ratings Ireland Limited\par \par \uc2 Neue Mainzer Strasse 46 - 50\par \par \uc2 Frankfurt am Main D-60311\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1027\par \par \uc2 huseyin.sevinc@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Maria Rodriguez,\par \par \uc2 Analyst\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1802\par \par \uc2 maria.rodriguez2@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Liddell,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1008\par \par \uc2 lindsey.liddell@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 redmond.ramsdale@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Louisa Williams, London,