\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Erdogan  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes Turkey \highlight\uc2 's statistics agency after violent price hike\b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Inflation in Turkey reached its highest level in 19 years, reaching 36.1% per year by December 2021. The result of President \uc2 Recep Erdogan\uc2  's irresponsible monetary policy was met with a backlash from the Turkish government.\par \par \uc2 Actual inflation in Turkey easily exceeded all the claims of the government, which had hoped to achieve a figure close to 30.6% by 2021. In the month of December 2021 alone, prices soared violently, marking a 13.58% rise over November. This was even higher than the monthly inflation recorded in Venezuela in the same month, and evidences the profound failure of the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan decided to dismiss the former president of the Turkish National Statistics Agency, Sait Erdal Dincer, just after the disastrous results of the heterodox monetary policy became known. He will be replaced by Erhan Cetinkaya, who had already held several positions as banking regulator. The Minister of Justice, \uc2 Abdulhamit Gul\uc2 , was also dismissed for opposing the President's decision.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish president did not give any kind of official and concrete explanation for his decision, so the situation bears a close resemblance to what Argentina experienced in October 2006 under N\u233\'00\'E9stor Kirchner' s administration, when the Argentine president \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  the INDEC to falsify official statistics and artificially favor the image of his government.\par \par \uc2 Turkish monetary policy adopted, almost religiously, an absolute opposition to the adjustment of monetary policy interest rates on primary and secondary markets. This refusal prevented the relevant monetary correction after large expansion packages in 2020 as a consequence of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The government tried to compensate for its dogmatism with interest rates by including, in return, an insurance against real losses for fixed term deposits, being permanently exposed to inflation. However, despite the initial success on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the measures were insufficient and failed to recreate confidence in the Turkish Lira.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220201ei21008t6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
