\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms ING Bank A.S at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 2,005 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2022\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 31012022\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed ING Bank A.S.'s (INGBT) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+'.\par \par \uc2 The Outlook is Negative. At the same time, the agency has affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SHAREHOLDER SUPPORT RATING (SSR) AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The 'B+' LTFC IDR of INGBT is underpinned by both shareholder support, as reflected in its SSR, and its standalone creditworthiness, as reflected in its VR.\par \par \uc2 The 'b+' SSR reflects the bank's strategic importance to 100% owner, \uc2 ING Bank N.V\uc2 . (AA-/Stable; ING), integration and role within the wider ING group, and small size relative to ING's ability to provide support, if needed.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps INGBT's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/Negative) and drives the Negative Outlook on the bank. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default. We believe government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk could increase given the latest heightened market volatility. The Negative Outlook also reflects significant operating environment risks, which heighten pressure on the bank's standalone credit profile.\par \par \uc2 INGBT's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR is also driven by shareholder support, and is one notch above the LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). The Negative Outlook on the LTLC IDR mirrors the Outlook on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of INGBT's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers' is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of INGBT's VR at 'b+', a level that is in line with that of the largest, systemically important banks in the sector despite the bank's only small size, reflects the bank's record of solid performance, adequate loss-absorption buffers underpinned by above-peer-average capital ratios, and below-peer-average risk appetite. However, the VR also considers the concentration of INGBT's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the high-risk Turkish operating environment, limited franchise, moderate market shares (1% of total sector assets at end-9M21) and limited competitive advantages.\par \par \uc2 Downside risks for the VR have increased given heightened market volatility in Turkey and its implications for the bank's financial metrics. Fitch revised the outlook on the operating environment score for Turkish banks to negative in December 2021. Turkish banks are vulnerable to refinancing risks, due to exchange-rate volatility, FC liquidity risks due to high deposit dollarisation, and asset-quality risks due to high FC lending.\par \par \uc2 INGBT has pursued a conservative growth strategy, deleveraging its loan book (in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) adjusted terms) between end-2018 and end-9M21, largely through FC loans. Lending contracted 8% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted basis) in 9M21, with LC and FC loans shrinking 11%, and 4% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted), respectively.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, FC lending remains high (end-9M21: 45% of gross loans) and above sector-average (36%), despite deleveraging. This heightens credit risks given not all borrowers will be fully hedged against lira depreciation, in our view. However, INGBT adopts a cautious approach to FC lending, primarily targeting exporters, borrowers with international \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  generating \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenue, or borrowers with robust financial positions and hedges in place.\par \par \uc2 INGBT's impaired loans (NPL) ratio improved slightly to 4.9% at end-9M21 (2020: 5.1%), despite contracting gross loans, due to strong collections. Stage 2 loans comprised 15.2% of gross loans, having declined significantly in recent years (2018: 24%). Asset-quality metrics, including an above-sector-average NPL ratio (sector: 3.6%), should partly be viewed in light of its below-sector-average loan growth in recent years and conservative classification approach that is in line with \uc2 ING Group\uc2  standards.\par \par \uc2 Impaired loans were 74% covered by specific reserves at end-9M21 (sector: 78%). Including Stage 1 and Stage 2 reserves, total reserves coverage was 95%, significantly below the sector average (142%), reflecting reliance on collateral. Stage 2 reserves coverage was 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 The bank's operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWAs) rose to 2.8% at end-9M21 (sector: 1.9%), from 1.9% at end-2020, reflecting margin expansion from loan repricing in the higher interest-rate environment and a recovery in fee income. Cost growth was also muted, reflecting sound cost control, and loan impairment charges (LICs) fell materially (9M21: equal to -4.5% of pre-impairment operating profit, down from 41.1% in 2020). However, inflationary pressures are set to weigh on costs for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is a relative strength and provides an adequate loss-absorption buffer to absorb risks under our base case. We believe ordinary capital support from \uc2 ING Bank N.V\uc2 . would be forthcoming, in case of need.\par \par \uc2 INGBT's common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio of 18.8% at end-9M21 (excluding uplift from forbearance) was significantly above the peer average, while the bank's 19.7% total capital ratio was significantly above the 12% recommended regulatory minimum. Pre-impairment operating profit (9M21: equal to 3.2% of average loans; annualised) provides a moderate buffer to absorb unexpected credit losses through the income statement. However, capital ratios remain sensitive to lira depreciation and asset-quality risks. The bank repaid USD530 million in total of FC subordinated debt to its parent in 1H21, which previously provided a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 INGBT is largely deposit-funded (end-9M21: 78% of total funding) and its deposit base is fairly granular, reflecting a high proportion of retail deposits (75% in total). Customer deposit growth, combined with loan deleveraging and a reduction in largely group sourced wholesale funding in recent years, drove an improvement in gross loans-to-deposits to 95% at end-9M21 from 113% at end-2020, and an exceptionally high 163% at end-2018. Group funding comprised 10% of total funding at end-9M21 (end-9M20: 23%). External FC debt was a moderate 8% of total funding.\par \par \uc2 The bank's available FC liquidity - comprising cash, maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, interbank balances (including balances placed with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2 ), and government securities - fully covered short-term non-deposit FC liabilities falling due within a year at end-9M21. We view refinancing risk as manageable, given INGBT's moderate external debt exposure and potential support from the bank's shareholder. Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from deposit instability, given high, though below sector-average, dollarisation (end-9M21: 46% of customer deposits), or from a prolonged loss of market access.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs and SSR are primarily sensitive to a change in Turkey's sovereign rating or in Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in Turkey's external finances or in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, or an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely result in a downgrade of INGBT's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in ING's propensity or ability to support INGBT could also result in a downgrade, but only if the bank's VR is simultaneously downgraded.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR is primarily sensitive to the Turkish operating environment and could be downgraded, due to marked deterioration as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic growth prospects, or deposit instability or liquidity pressures.\par \par \uc2 In addition, greater-than-expected deterioration of INGBT's asset quality and operating profitability, particularly if this leads to an erosion of capital buffers, or a weakening of the bank's FC liquidity, if not offset by shareholder support, would likely lead to a downgrade of the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR is downgraded or if Fitch believes INGBT's creditworthiness has weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LT IDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if Fitch believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, particularly if reflected in a sovereign upgrade and a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in by the challenging Turkish operating environment and the bank's limited franchise. However, a record of resilient financial metrics, notwithstanding the tough market conditions, and an improvement in asset quality and profitability metrics would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if Fitch believes INGBT's creditworthiness has improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b+' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reason: macroeconomic stability (negative). This reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 INGBT's IDRs are either driven or underpinned by shareholder support from ING.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on Fitch's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 RATING ACTIONS\par \par \uc2 Entity / Debt\par \par \uc2 Rating\par \par \uc2 Prior\par \par \uc2 ING Bank A.S.\par \par \uc2 LT IDR\par \par \uc2 B+\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B+\par \par \uc2 ST IDR\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 LC LT IDR\par \par \uc2 BB-\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 BB-\par \par \uc2 LC ST IDR\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 B\par \par \uc2 Natl LT\par \par \uc2 AA(tur)\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 AA(tur)\par \par \uc2 Viability\par \par \uc2 b+\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 b+\par \par \uc2 Shareholder Support\par \par \uc2 b+\par \par \uc2 Affirmed\par \par \uc2 b+\par \par \uc2 Page\par \par \uc2 of 1\par \par \uc2 VIEW ADDITIONAL RATING DETAILS\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 PARTICIPATION STATUS\par \par \uc2 The rated entity (and/or its agents) or, in the case of structured finance, one or more of the transaction parties participated in the rating process except that the following issuer(s), if any, did not participate in the rating process, or provide additional information, beyond the issuer's available public disclosure.\par \par \uc2 APPLICABLE CRITERIA\par \par \uc2 National Scale Rating Criteria (pub. 22 Dec 2020)\par \par \uc2 Bank Rating Criteria (pub. 13 Nov 2021) (including rating assumption sensitivity)\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Status\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Policy\par \par \uc2 ENDORSEMENT STATUS\par \par \uc2 ING Bank A.S. UK Issued, \uc2 EU\uc2  Endorsed\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020220201ei210001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
