\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Turk Ekonomi Bankasi at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2022\par 20:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 31 Jan 2022 14:09 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turk Ekonomi Bankasi  at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-31 January 2022:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .'s (TEB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SENIOR DEBT RATING, SHAREHOLDER SUPPORT RATING (SSR) AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 TEB's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are underpinned by standalone creditworthiness, as reflected by its VR, and potential institutional support from its majority owner, \uc2 BNP Paribas S.A\uc2  . (BNPP; A+/Stable), as reflected in the bank's 'b+' Shareholder Support Rating.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of shareholder support reflects TEB's strategic importance to, integration with and role within the wider BNPP group and small size relative to BNPP's ability to provide support. TEB is 55%-owned, but fully controlled, by TEB Holding, in which BNP Paribas Fortis (BNPPF; A+/Stable) has a 50% stake. TEB is fully consolidated by BNPPF, a core subsidiary of BNPP. BNPP ultimately holds a 72.5% stake in TEB, including a 23.5% stake held directly.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/Negative), and drives the Negative Outlook on the bank. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default, given the weak external financing position. We believe government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk could increase following the latest market volatility. The Negative Outlook also reflects significant operating environment risks, which heighten pressure on the VR.\par \par \uc2 TEB's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, is also driven by shareholder support. It is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). However, the Outlook on the LTLC IDR is also Negative, mirroring the sovereign Outlook.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TEB's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers remains unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TEB's VR at 'b+' considers the bank's reasonable business profile, although it has only a modest franchise (end-3Q21: 2% of banking sector assets), reasonable asset quality with significantly below sector average FC lending, stable and adequate profitability through the cycle and adequate funding and liquidity profile. However, the VR also reflects TEB's concentration in the challenging Turkish operating environment, high exposure to the fairly high-risk SME segment (end-3Q21: 33% of gross loans at end-3Q21) and only adequate core capitalisation for its risk profile.\par \par \uc2 Downside risks for the VR have increased, given heightened market volatility in Turkey and the implications for the bank's financial metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  revised the outlook on the operating environment score for Turkish banks to negative in December 2021. Turkish banks are vulnerable to refinancing risks due to exchange-rate volatility, FC liquidity risks due to high deposit dollarisation and asset quality risks due to high sector FC lending.\par \par \uc2 TEB has historically pursued a fairly conservative growth strategy typically reporting below sector average loan growth. However, lending grew by 13% in 9M21 (sector: 12%) despite operating environment pressures, including a rise in FC lending (end-9M21: up 15% in USD), following three consecutive years of FC loan book contraction. TEB continues to report one of the lowest shares of FC lending (end-9M21: 18%) in the sector and exposure to long-term FC investment and project finance loans is limited. Nevertheless, FC lending increases credit risks, given that not all borrowers are likely to be fully hedged against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 SME exposure increases asset quality risks, given the segment's sensitivity to macro volatility, as does the bank's exposure to the troubled construction and real estate (4% of loans) and energy (4%) sectors. One-third of loans consist of retail lending, mainly concentrated in general purpose loans (about 70% or one-fifth of total lending). These are all in Turkish lira and fixed rate, somewhat mitigating credit risks, but the portfolio is sensitive to unemployment.\par \par \uc2 TEB's impaired loans (NPL) ratio fell to 3.1% at end-3Q21 (sector: 3.6%), from 4.1% at end-2020, despite operating environment pressures, primarily reflecting high nominal loan growth (up 13% in 9M21), recoveries, NPL sales and write offs. TEB reported fairly high, albeit below peer average, Stage 2 loans at end-3Q21 (9.4% of loans; about one-fifth restructured), which could migrate to Stage 3 as loans season. We believe the bank has sufficient tolerance at its current rating to absorb likely asset quality weakening under our base case, despite operating environment weakness.\par \par \uc2 Total reserves coverage of impaired loans was below the sector average at 120%, partly reflecting TEB's focus on SME, and therefore collateralised lending.\par \par \uc2 Profitability is moderate (9M21: operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) of 1.8%), despite a high cost base that is set to rise further amid inflationary pressures, and the bank has limited economies of scale (costs/assets of 2.8%). In 9M21, bottom line profitability was supported by a provisions reversal, but we believe loan impairment charges are likely to increase in 2022. The bank reports an above-sector average net interest margin (NIM; 9M21: 5.5%), despite fairly high funding costs, reflecting its focus on higher-margin SME lending. The NIM should benefit from the lira rate cuts in the short term, due to short-term repricing nature of deposits.\par \par \uc2 We view TEB's core capitalisation as only adequate for its risk profile, internal capital generation and asset quality pressures. The common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio decreased to 11.3% at end-3Q21 (from 12.7% at end-2020), including the regulatory forbearance. The total regulatory capital was more comfortable at 16.7% (including forbearance) supported by FC subordinated Tier 2 debt from BNPP, which provides a partial hedge against potential lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation remains sensitive to lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs), asset quality weakening and weaker profitability, but it should remain adequate, given moderate buffers over minimum requirements and adequate NPL reserves coverage. Pre-impairment operating profit (9M21: 1.9% of average loans; 2020: 2.2%) provides an adequate buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 TEB is largely deposit-funded (77% of total funding at end-3Q21) and its loans/deposits ratio (97%) outperforms peers' and the sector average. Customer deposits, primarily from retail customers, are sticky despite being contractually short term and fairly granular, although FC deposits are significant (end-9M21: about 45%; sector: 55%). Nevertheless, wholesale funding is fairly high (23% of total funding) and largely in FC. Net of group funding it was a moderate 10% of total funding.\par \par \uc2 TEB reports good liquidity metrics (liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) at end-3Q21: 182%; FC LCR: 507%). At end-3Q21 available FC liquidity (mainly FC swaps with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  ) was sufficient to cover maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months plus a moderate share of FC deposits. We believe refinancing risks at the bank should be manageable, given low FC debt exposure, good FC liquidity and potential liquidity support from BNPP. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a change in investor sentiment, FC deposit instability or a prolonged loss of market access.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of Turkey's sovereign rating would likely lead to similar action on the bank's LT IDRs, SSR and senior debt rating. Increased government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk would likely also lead to negative rating action on the bank.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in BNPP's ability and propensity to support TEB could also result in a downgrade but only if the bank's VR was simultaneously downgraded.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR is primarily sensitive to the Turkish operating environment and could be downgraded due to a marked deterioration, as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic growth prospects as well as deposit instability or liquidity pressures.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a greater than expected deterioration of TEB's asset quality and a protracted weakening in operating profitability could lead to a downgrade of the VR. The headroom at the current VR level would reduce and TEB's VR would likely be downgraded, if its CET1 ratio fell and remained below 9% for a sustained period or if we viewed capitalisation as no longer commensurate with the bank's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR was downgraded and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed TEB's creditworthiness had weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LT IDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, likely reflected in a sovereign upgrade or resulting from a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 31 Jan 2022 14:09 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Turk Ekonomi Bankasi  -2-\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in light of the challenging Turkish operating environment and the bank's moderate franchise. However, a record of resilient financial metrics notwithstanding operating environment volatility would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed TEB's creditworthiness improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 VR ADJUSTMENTS\par \par \uc2 The operating environment score of 'b+' for Turkish banks is lower than the category implied score of 'bb', due to the following adjustment reason: macroeconomic stability (negative). This reflects heightened market volatility, high dollarisation and high risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 TEB IDRs are either driven or underpinned by institutional support from its majority shareholder, BNPP.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Shareholder Support Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ahmet Kilinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1272\par \par \uc2 ahmet.kilinc@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1027\par \par \uc2 huseyin.sevinc@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 redmond.ramsdale@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Status\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures For Unsolicited