ely under control after the lira weakened sharply in the last two months and he reiterated his unorthodox view that interest rates were the cause of inflation. The toll on the lira since November has been significant and at its worst point the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was down nearly 150% in 2021, and this follows marked falls every year since 2015. The initial trigger for this year's depreciation was political pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates in order to help revive Turkey's COVID-19 ravaged economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered the rate cuts despite surging inflation, further fanning price pressures and triggering a large move out of the lira, culminating in a record low of 18.40 versus the U.S. dollar. The market has steadied a little in the final days of 2021 but \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unorthodox monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  might continue to damage confidence in 2022. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/34aPi4C"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/34aPi4C}}}\uc2  Jan 20 is next big date in Turkish lira diary The lira looks set to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on their toes in 2022, with key event risks including monetary policy announcements from the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBRT), the first of which is scheduled for Jan. 20. The CBRT signalled a pause in its rate-cutting cycle on Dec. 16, when it reduced rates for the fourth time in four months. That rate cut propelled USD/TRY through 18 for the first time ever -- prompting the launch of Turkey's lira protection plan. Data published on Thursday showed the CBRT's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $3.53 billion to a 19-year low of $8.63 billion last week -- when USD/TRY traded a cavernous 