\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira Falls After Rising on  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2021\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1136 ET - The Turkish lira falls as investor worries persist over President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  's unorthodox strategy of cutting interest rates to curb inflation. The losses mark a reversal from the sizable gains made last week after Erdogan unveiled a new savings scheme to protect lira deposits against exchange-rate fluctuations, aimed at reducing downward pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , alongside \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find engineering further lira rallies harder going from here, and I will be watching their foreign reserve data going forward for more signals of when to re-enter the short Erdogan trade," Oanda analyst \uc2 Jeffrey Halley\uc2  says. USD/TRY rises 6.1% to 11.5030. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1026 ET - Cusip numbers, the common language of stock and bond markets, are getting their first new manager in more than half a century. Information provider \uc2 FactSet Research Systems\uc2  agrees to buy Cusip Global Services from S&P Global for more than $1.9B in a deal aimed at winning antitrust blessing for the latter's acquisition of \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  . The Cusip system works much like a Dewey Decimal system for securities, giving each stock, bond or other security a special number used to track and trade millions of securities. S&P and its predecessors have managed the system since 1968 on behalf of the American Banker Association, which owns the system's intellectual property. More than 62.8M Cusip identifiers have been assigned to date. (colin.kellaher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 ET - Longer-term Treasury yields tick down after last week's strong gains. The move would usually indicate risk aversion, but the last week of the year tends to bring lower-than-usual trading volumes that may distort market trends. Last week was marked by improving expectations as the Omicron wave looked less damaging than previously feared, but inflation concerns remain on the horizon, with food makers saying they will increase prices in the first half of 2022. With employment levels rising, many economists expect inflation to pressure the Fed to raise rates more aggressively. The 10-year benchmark falls to 1.483% from 1.492% at Thursday's close. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0450 ET - The dollar rises against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recovering some of last week's falls. It keeps within its recent trading range, however, with traders lacking any reason to push it back toward the multimonth highs reached in mid-December. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was lifted higher after an upbeat statement earlier this month from the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  , which boosted expectations that interest rates will start to rise next year. However, the Fed has "outlined a quite aggressive tightening course of action" and risks are growing for policymakers "to underdeliver," analysts at online platform ForexAnalytix say in a note. The dollar therefore spent much of last week "grinding lower," they say. The DXY dollar index, which measures the dollar's value against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rises 0.2% to 96.2200, while EUR/USD falls 0.1% to 1.1311. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 ET - Prospects that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will follow up December's interest-rate rise with further increases in 2022 are helping lift the pound in an otherwise quiet market due to year-end holidays, says Marshall Gittler, analyst at BDSwiss. "The market's estimate of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's bank rate has been creeping up over the last week," he says. This has come alongside optimism that the Omicron variant of Covid-19 isn't causing severe illness and won't require severe restrictions. Gittler notes that deaths related to Omicron have been relatively low in the U.K., despite spiraling case numbers. GBP/USD rises 0.1% to 1.3406, having traded as low as 1.3173 last week, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . EUR/GBP falls 0.1% to 0.8438. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 ET - The U.S. economy is expected to expand 3.5% in 2022 driven by a further reopening of the services sector, a boost to consumer spending from pent-up savings, and inventory restocking, economists from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  say. However, a sharp deceleration is expected in 1Q because of the spread of the Covid-19 Omicron variant, the bank says. The unemployment rate should continue to fall during 2022 and will likely reach its pre-pandemic 3.5% level by the end of that year, while the current inflation surge is likely to get worse before it gets better, \uc2 GS\uc2  says. "High inflation is likely to keep the Fed on a quarterly tightening path next year," it says. The bank expects the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates three times starting in March. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 ET - U.K. house prices have increased in 2021, though they are set to steady in the next six months as higher mortgage rates and falling disposable incomes due to high inflation will weigh on demand, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. The emergence of the Covid-19 Omicron variant could also keep consumers' confidence subdued, damping the mood for big purchases and keeping a lid on price growth, Pantheon's senior U.K. economist \uc2 Gabriella Dickens\uc2  says. However, house prices could still rise in 2022 as a whole, if households choose to devote a larger share of their incomes to mortgage repayments or if they spend on housing their excess savings accumulated during the pandemic, she says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 ET - Japanese stocks end lower, dragged by retail and e-commerce stocks amid uncertainty due to the Omicron variant and ensuing containment measures. Nitori Holdings dropped 6.5% after nine-month net profit fell 1.3%, and e-commerce company ZOZO lost 3.2%. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 0.4% to 28676.46. Investors are focusing on any developments over the Omicron variant and governments' responses. USD/JPY is at 114.47 compared with 114.39 late Friday in North America. No trading is being done in the newest 10-year Japanese government bonds. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0110 ET - China could further ease monetary policy, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says; it expects the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject more long-term liquidity into the banking system with more reserve requirement ratio cuts and various lending facilities. Goldman also reckons fiscal expenditure will be more supportive of growth and that local governments would ease property policies at local levels. However, the investment bank says risks to growth remain, including a likely-cooling property market, China's zero-Covid strategy amid recurring Covid waves and its anti-pollution measures prior to the Winter Olympics. (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 ET - USD/SGD edges lower amid thin trading with markets in Hong Kong, Australia and New Zealand closed Monday due to a holiday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are in the holiday mode and will likely remain that way until the middle of next week, Oanda says. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to range-trade as the region's interbank market seems to have closed shop for the year now, Oanda adds. USD/SGD is down 0.1% at 1.3567. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0004 ET - China's December \uc2 PMI\uc2  could slip, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. The investment bank forecasts December's manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  from the country's National Bureau of Statistics to come in at 49.9 versus 50.1 in November and the Caixin December manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  to edge lowerto 49.8 from 49.9 in November. The "Covid outbreak in Zhejiang province since mid-December probably affected industrial activities, and container throughput data also pointed to weaker trade growth in December compared with November," it says. Non-manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  may also drop, with an expected 52.1 in December versus 52.3 in November amid China's property-sector weakness, Goldman adds. (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2107 ET - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  consolidate against USD amid somewhat thin trading during the year-end holiday period. Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue to be range-bound until next week, when the U.S. December non-farm payrolls data are released. With unemployment dropping and inflation rising, the Fed has said that it will taper its support for the economy more quickly, and expects to raise rates three times next year, Phillip Futures notes. USD/KRW edges 0.1% lower to 1,185.83, USD/SGD is down 0.1% at 1.3566 and AUD/USD is up 0.1% at 0.7235. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 27, 2021 11:36 ET (16:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211227ehcr0015r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
