\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Turkish lira caps historic week with big lift from Erdogan government\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2021\par 21:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Data shows Turks did not sell dollars Monday, Tuesday\par \par \uc2 * State \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen $8 bln this week (Updates values, adds Erdogan comment)\par \par \uc2 By Nevzat Devranoglu and Jonathan Spicer\par \par \uc2 ANKARA, Dec 24 (Reuters) - Turkey's lira closed out its strongest week on record on Friday, spiking more than 50% with the support of billions of dollars of state-backed market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a promise that the government would cover \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses on certain deposits.\par \par \uc2 Turks did not sell dollars on Monday and Tuesday, according to official data that suggested they had played little role in the sharpest market gains. The state \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, cost the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than $8 billion this week, according to traders' calculations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained for five straight days and touched mid-November levels; it stood at 10.7 versus the dollar at 1919 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The lira had plunged on Monday to an all-time low of 18.4 per dollar, after a months-long slide due to unorthodox interest rate cuts and fears of an inflationary spiral.\par \par \uc2 But late on Monday President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  unveiled a scheme in which the Treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reimburse losses on converted lira deposits against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sparking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest intraday rally ever.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with broadcaster AHaber, Erdogan said Turks showed confidence in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lira deposits increased by 23.8 billion lira after the anti-dollarisation plan announcement.\par \par \uc2 But data from the BDDK banking watchdog showed that, after heavy accumulation of dollars last week, Turkish individual depositors held $163.7 billion of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday - virtually unchanged from Monday and Friday, when the total was $163.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the lira got a big boost from what traders and economists called backdoor dollar sales by state banks, supported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the first three days of this week alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net foreign reserves dropped by $8.5 billion, according to the calculations of three bankers who spoke to Reuters. The drop totalled nearly $18 billion in December, they said.\par \par \uc2 "We suspect positioning and stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to dominate the price action," said Win Thin, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . "Even if the lira stabilizes, there are still strong inflationary impulses in the economy that will continue to erode the value of any local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings."\par \par \uc2 After Reuters reported progress in talks over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines with Azerbaijan and the United Arab Emirates, Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Sahap Kavcioglu, said the bank could sign two \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap deals within two weeks.\par \par \uc2 'USING ALL INSTRUMENTS'\par \par \uc2 Citing four sources familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including a senior Turkish official, Reuters reported on Thursday that state banks heavily sold dollars earlier this week on the heels of Erdogan's announcement.\par \par \uc2 The state banks have not commented on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was not immediately available for comment, had announced dollar-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month but not this week.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Nureddin Nebati, discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on broadcaster NTV on Thursday, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "using all the instruments at its disposal in a positive way".\par \par \uc2 As of Dec. 17, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net foreign reserves tumbled to $12.2 billion from $21.2 billion a week earlier, to levels last hit in May in a reflection of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hakan Kara, former chief economist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales amounted to $17 billion-$20 billion this month, including $3 billion on Wednesday alone, though he said it was unclear specifically how they were used.\par \par \uc2 "State banks provided significant support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance but it's not only state banks that are selling dollars," said a bank trader who requested anonymity.\par \par \uc2 In 2019-2020, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed, via swaps, the sale of some $128 billion via state banks to stabilise the lira, depleting Turkey's foreign reserves and drawing sharp criticism from the political opposition.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from Erdogan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed its policy rates by 500 basis points to 14% since September despite a jump above 21% in inflation. Price rises are set to blow through 30% next year in part due to the lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting these concerns, \uc2 Turkish Airlines\uc2  will raise employee pay by the rate of inflation plus 65% for 2022, according to an agreement with its labour union. (Reporting by Nevzat Devranoglu and Jonathan Spicer; Editing by Jane Merriman, Catherine Evans and Leslie Adler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-12-24T21:09:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211224ehco01alt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
