\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish lira caps historic week with big lift from Erdogan government\b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2021\par 11:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ANKARA, Dec 24 (Reuters) - Turkey's lira was on track for its strongest week on record on Friday, having spiked 44% with the support of billions of dollars of state-backed market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a promise that the government would cover \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses on certain deposits.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had plunged on Monday to an all-time low of 18.4 to the dollar, after a months-long slide due to unorthodox interest rate cuts and fears of an inflationary spiral.\par \par \uc2 But late on Monday President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  unveiled a scheme in which the Treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reimburse losses on converted lira deposits against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sparking the biggest intraday rally ever.\par \par \uc2 The anti-dollarisation plan set off four straight days of gains as Turks converted some $900 million worth of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into lira, according to Finance Minister Nureddin Nebati.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooled 4% on Friday to 11.85 versus the dollar at 0918 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The lira got a big boost from what traders and economists called backdoor dollar sales by state banks supported by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the first three days of the week alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net foreign reserves dropped by $8.5 billion, according to the calculations of three bankers who spoke to Reuters. The drop totalled nearly $18 billion in December, they said.\par \par \uc2 As of Dec 17, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net foreign reserves tumbled to $12.2 billion, from $21.2 billion a week earlier, to levels last hit in May in a reflection of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 'USING ALL INSTRUMENTS'\par \par \uc2 Citing four sources familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including a senior Turkish official, Reuters reported on Thursday that state banks heavily sold dollars earlier this week on the heels of Erdogan's announcement.\par \par \uc2 The state banks have not commented on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was not immediately available for comment, had announced dollar-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month but not this week.\par \par \uc2 Nebati, discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on broadcaster NTV on Thursday, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "using all the instruments at its disposal in a positive way".\par \par \uc2 Hakan Kara, former chief economist at \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales amount to $17-20 billion this month, including $3 billion on Wednesday alone, though he said it was unclear specifically how they were used.\par \par \uc2 "State banks provided significant support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance but it's not only state banks that are selling dollars," said a bank trader who requested anonymity.\par \par \uc2 In 2019-2020, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed, via swaps, the sale of some $128 billion via state banks to stabilise the lira, depleting Turkey's foreign reserves and drawing sharp criticism from the political opposition.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from Erdogan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed its policy rates by 500 basis points to 14% since September despite a jump above 21% in inflation. Price rises are set to blow through 30% next year in part due to the lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting these concerns, \uc2 Turkish Airlines\uc2  will raise employee pay by the rate of inflation plus 65% for 2022, according to an agreement with its labour union. (Reporting by Nevzat Devranoglu and Jonathan Spicer. Editing by Jane Merriman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-12-24T11:11:04.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211224ehco00umt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
