\par\par \b \uc2 Eurozone Inflation Expected to Decelerate Slightly in December -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2021\par 13:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1232 GMT - \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects eurozone headline inflation to slow to 4.7% year-on-year in December from 4.9% in November, with core inflation set to ease by to 2.5%. Following a steep upward trend, energy prices will likely start easing in year-on-year terms this month, economists at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  say. Their disinflationary impact will probably be partly offset by a further acceleration in food prices, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The expected mild decline in core inflation mainly reflects a technical factor, as holiday-sensitive items--which typically record large price increases in December--have lower weights in the HICP basket compared to last year, the Italian bank says. \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  will publish the preliminary estimate of eurozone inflation for December on Jan. 7. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1231 GMT - The pound could fall over Christmas given the U.K.'s "grim" outlook but any declines should be limited, ING says. "The fast spread of the Omicron variant in the U.K. may keep some pressure on GBP around Christmas, in particular as the government may opt to impose some new restrictions," ING analysts say. Political turmoil may also weigh on sterling as markets re-assess Brexit policy and the stability of Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  's cabinet after the resignation of Brexit chief negotiator David Frost, they say. However, short-seller bets against sterling are still likely "overstretched" even after \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raised interest rates last Thursday, which could limit losses, they say. GBP/USD rises 0.3% to 1.3244 and EUR/GBP falls 0.2% to 0.8524. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1159 GMT - European bond outflows have been a major driver of the euro's weakness in 2021, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. This year's net bond outflows reflect soft foreign demand for European debt and strong European demand for foreign debt, SocGen \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kit Juckes says. "Even with a big current account surplus, the eurozone is a big net foreign investor," he says. "That's a large part of the reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weak, and a reflection of the world's borrowers being in a strong position relative to the world's savers." EUR/USD is last flat on the day at 1.1278 but has fallen nearly 8% since the start of the year, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1145 GMT - Turkey's government may struggle to deliver on its plan to compensate lira deposit holders for losses resulting from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. "The bill for the government can easily turn out gigantic and, therefore, unsustainable," \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  strategist \uc2 Cristian Maggio\uc2  says. The government will need to ensure a "very high level" of compensation for any losses incurred by holding lira savings instead of, say, dollars, but it cannot commit to the substantial costs involved as it would risk bankrupting Turkey in a short time, he says. The lira may find some reprieve, but only temporarily, given Turkey's long-term economic weaknesses and negative inflation-adjusted real rates that could fall further as inflation accelerates and interest rates are cut again, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1031 GMT - Turkey's new scheme that protects lira deposits against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations could backfire, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  on Monday said the government would compensate lira deposit holders if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines more than the interest rate offered by banks. "If Erdogan's pledge is challenged, and the lira loses value, someone will have to pick up the tab," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  strategist Bas van Geffen says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have worsened \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's already low foreign reserves, potentially eroding the credibility that the president can compensate for losses if the lira faced new headwinds, he says. The government's finances are also now even more linked to the lira's performance, which could exacerbate shocks if credibility risks materialize, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1028 GMT - The scale of the Turkish lira's rebound following measures designed to encourage people to hold deposits in lira rather than foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows how undervalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had become, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. The substantial rebound "reflects in large part that lira weakness had recently significantly overshot fundamentals as it became deeply undervalued," he says. The measures should "at least temporarily" push the lira higher and should help slow further lira weakness going forward "by discouraging dollarisation flows in the Turkish economy." However, selling should resume as a fundemantal lack of confidence in policymaking in Turkey--which has cut interest rates despite high inflation--"remains in place," Hardman says. USD/TRY last trades at 12.5895, sharply down from Monday's record high of 18.3674, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT - The Turkish lira recovered a substantial chunk of its recent losses late Monday after measures to encourage depositors to hold savings in lira, but selling could soon resume as long as President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  favors low interest rates, Victoria Scholar, head of investment at \uc2 Interactive Investor\uc2  , says. The government has said it will make up for losses incurred by holders of lira deposits if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's declines exceed interest rates promised by banks. However, Erdogan "is showing no signs of shifting away from his stance in favour of ultra-loose monetary policy, despite spiralling inflation, which should continue to keep the lira under pressure." USD/TRY last trades at 12.6174, sharply lower compared with Monday's record high of 18.3674, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT - Early evidence points to a surge in U.S. job creation in December, but the rise in Covid-19 cases due to the spread of the Omicron coronavirus variant clouds the outlook early in the new year, Pantheon Macroeconomics' Chief Economist \uc2 Ian Shepherdson\uc2  says. Data from \uc2 Homebase\uc2  --which supplies payroll software to small businesses--points to sharp employment growth in December, ranging from 850,000 to 1.35 million new jobs, he says. "The bad news is that January will likely be much less impressive, thanks to the Omicron Covid-19 wave, which is now gathering momentum," Shepherdson says. The more-infectious variant is likely to take large numbers of people out of the labor force for a time as they isolate after testing positive, he adds. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 GMT - Global sentiment is weakening, led by a decline in consumer sentiment and a modest retreat in business sentiment, according to Oxford Economics's global composite sentiment indicator. Oxford Economics says the indicator is likely to take a further hit in the near term as Omicron cases rise. However, sentiment overall remains above its precrisis levels, painting a relatively optimistic picture for the global outlook going forward, the economic-forecasting firm says. "With household and corporate balance sheets in good shape and sentiment still strong, we think it reasonable to expect that the global economy will manage to navigate the rough waters presented by the Omicron variant," says Tamara Basic Vasiljev, senior economist at Oxford Economics. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT - Germany's economy is being affected by supply-chain disruptions along with demand-supply mismatch for the automobile industry due to the global chip shortage, \uc2 GlobalData\uc2  says. The data company has revised down its 2022 real GDP growth forecast for the country to 4.4%, a downward revision of 0.1 percentage point due to concerns over the Omicron variant. "Tightening of measures to combat Covid-19 along with slow industrial growth are expected to retard economic growth prospects in the short-term," \uc2 GlobalData\uc2 's economic research analyst Gargi Rao says. Supply-chain disruptions in raw materials along with semiconductor chip shortages will continue to hamper German manufacturing output in the coming quarter, Rao says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT - The Turkish lira's recovery after President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  announced measures to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't last unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverses course by raising interest rates, \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2  analyst Ipek Ozkardeskaya says. The government's pledge to compensate for losses in lira deposits resulting from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines "should be taken with a pinch of salt," Ozkardeskaya says. It will "hardly convince investors to take the risk of sitting on the shaky lira, and will inevitably weigh on the budget," she says. Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep rates low even as inflation surges, she says. "Markets' tolerance for the low-rate policy won't be infinite, and the wind could rapidly change direction." USD/TRY falls 9.8% to 12.1208. On Monday, USD/TRY hit a record high of 18.3674, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0950 GMT - The dollar could edge lower heading into the year-end as investors take some profit on recent bets on further gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it might recover again in the new year, \uc2 MUFG\uc2  says. "The U.S. dollar has continued to hold up well though so far even though there is a risk that popular long positions could be pared back heading into year-end as positions are lightened to take risk off the table," \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. \uc2 MUFG\uc2 's seasonal analysis shows the dollar tends to underperform in December, before reversing losses in January, he says. The DXY dollar index falls 0.2% to 96.3640. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 21, 2021 07:33 ET (12:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211221ehcl00160\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
