\par\par \b \uc2 Turkey's Lira Deposit Scheme Could Backfire -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2021\par 11:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1031 GMT - Turkey's new scheme that protects lira deposits against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations could backfire, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  on Monday said the government would compensate lira deposit holders if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines more than the interest rate offered by banks. "If Erdogan's pledge is challenged, and the lira loses value, someone will have to pick up the tab," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  strategist Bas van Geffen says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have worsened \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's already low foreign reserves, potentially eroding the credibility that the president can compensate for losses if the lira faced new headwinds, he says. The government's finances are also now even more linked to the lira's performance, which could exacerbate shocks if credibility risks materialize, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 21, 2021 05:31 ET (10:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211221ehcl000vv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
