\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira at New Record Lows, Likely to Extend Losses in 2022 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2021\par 10:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0954 GMT - The Turkish lira falls to new record lows and the sellff is likely to persist as \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks poised to cut interest rates further in 2022, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. The bank last Thursday signalled it could keep its rates unchanged in the 2022 first quarter but additional rate cuts next year remain likely due to pressure from President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  , \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  economist Gokce Celik says. "Inflation will likely rise sharply in the coming months and depreciation pressure on the TRY could continue while other central banks are tightening." Meanwhile, the "inadequacy" of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves means \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely be sizeable enough to stabilize the lira, Celik says. USD/TRY rises 5.3% to 17.5013, near the record high of 17.5466 reached earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT - London gold prices are down 0.1% at $1,802.70 a troy ounce, although they have crept up so far in December, thanks to safe-haven appetite for the yellow metal, \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  's Ipek Ozkardeskaya says. While Ozkardeskaya doesn't expect any further rallies from gold, she says that may change "if there is a significant erosion in the risk sentiment and a further selloff across the risk assets." Ultimately, signals of tightening monetary policy out of the Fed makes the dollar a safer haven than gold at the moment, she adds. \uc2 LME\uc2  three-month copper futures are down 1% at $9,355 a metric ton, with equities and oil markets dropping and the dollar having risen in recent weeks, making dollar-denominated commodities more expensive for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holders. (david.hodari@wsj.com ; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 0848 GMT - The Japanese yen rises versus the dollar as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields fall on increased demand for safe havens, reflecting worries over the Omicron coronavirus variant and the prospect of tighter U.S. fiscal policy. "USD/JPY continues to be the most tightly correlated G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to U.S. yields, and has come under downward pressure alongside U.S. yields," \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. Investor sentiment is undermined by further evidence of the disruptive impact of Omicron and by Democratic Senator Joe Manchin's assertion that he won't back President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's $2 trillion spending bill, he says. USD/JPY falls 0.1% to 113.46. The 10-year Treasury yield drops 3 basis points to 1.372%, according to Tradeweb.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT - Turnover in accommodation and food services in Germany declined by a price-adjusted 0.6% on month in October, the German statistics office Destatis says. Calendar- and seasonally adjusted turnover in accommodation and food services was 17.8% below the level recorded in February 2020, the month before Covid-19 was declared a pandemic, Destatis says. In the hotel-and-other-accommodation sector, real sales increased by an adjusted 1.4% in October compared with September, though they remain 16.7% below their level in February 2020. Restaurants posted a 2.1% decline in turnover on month, Destatis says. Turnover in the food-services sector remains 18.6% below pre-pandemic levels, the statistics office says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0828 GMT - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's focus on starting to raise interest rates next year, with asset purchases due to end by the end of 1Q, will continue to shift the market's attention toward the dollar, boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. Monetary divergence between the U.S. and the eurozone should push EUR/USD down toward 1.10 in 12 months, \uc2 Danske\uc2  forecasts. "Market themes are increasingly pro dollar," it says. "The manufacturing cycle is clearly slowing but central banks need to tighten to catch-up with inflation pressures and this is quite negative for EUR/USD." \uc2 Danske\uc2  forecasts EUR/USD at 1.12 in three months and 1.11 in six months. EUR/USD is last up 0.2% on the day at 1.1257. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT - The anticipated high volume of government bond issuance in the eurozone at the start of the year could serve as an initial litmus test, Elmar Voelker, senior fixed-income analyst at LBBW, says. "Eurozone countries typically flood the market with a large number of large-volume new issues over the first several weeks of the year, mainly with long and ultra-long maturities," he says. The high level of support from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's asset purchase programs, which will be reduced only mildly over the short term, "is likely to moderate impending difficulties," Voelker says. Significant price concessions will need to be factored in given the fact that the net issue volume of euro sovereign bonds is likely to rise steeply in January and February, the analyst says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 GMT - Sentiment in the German export industry has deteriorated in December, the \uc2 Ifo Institute\uc2  says. The Ifo export expectations index fell to 12.1 points in December from 15.8 points in November. "Exports will probably increase in the first quarter of 2022, albeit at a slower pace," \uc2 Ifo\uc2 's president \uc2 Clemens Fuest\uc2  says. Expectations in the automotive industry took a substantial hit, Ifo says. While additional international orders are still expected in December, there will be far fewer than thought in the previous month. The picture is similar for manufacturers of electrical equipment, \uc2 Ifo\uc2  says. Textile manufacturers, food and beverage manufacturers, and printing companies expect a slight decline in exports, the German institute says. In contrast, expectations among manufacturers of machinery and equipment improved. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT - Sterling falls on concerns that the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  could tighten restrictions to contain the spread of the Omicron coronavirus variant. U.K. Health Secretary \uc2 Sajid Javid\uc2  refused to rule out further coronavirus containment measures in England before Christmas in an interview with the BBC 's Andrew Marr on Sunday. "There are no guarantees in this pandemic, I don't think," he said. "At this point we just have to keep everything under review." GBP/USD falls 0.3% to 1.3211 and EUR/GBP rises 0.4% to 0.8523. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 GMT - \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  is neutral on the 10-year Greek-Italian government bond yield spread dynamics at levels around 20 basis points, strategist Aman Bansal says. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided not to give Greek government bonds eligibility to be bought under the Asset Purchase Programme but it said that Greek government bonds could benefit from flexible reinvestment of redemptions from the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme to avoid an interruption of purchases. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has been buying Greek government bonds with a waiver in the PEPP given Greece's current sub-investment grade rating, but it isn't buying them under the APP. Flexible reinvestments "should add to already existing supports from the ongoing strong economic recovery, likely efficient utilization of NGEU funds and potential for rating upgrades," Bansal says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0802 GMT - \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2  is underweight duration through a bond curve steepening bias, meanwhile, it starts to take profit on its large over-exposure to corporates, \uc2 Patrick Barbe\uc2 , head of European investment grade fixed income, says. The backdrop to this positioning is \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2  's view that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's decisions announce the normalization of economic activity and that the market should follow as soon as the pandemic doesn't need to be considered a risk anymore, Barbe says. \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2  expects the market to go back to its pre-pandemic level, except for short-term bonds which will still benefit from the 'forward guidance' conditions, Barbe says, referring to the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's guidance that it won't raise interest rates as long as inflation stays continuously at or below 2%. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0706 GMT - Indosuez Wealth Management expects inflation to remain high in 1H 2022, near the current level in the U.S., but it will monitor any de-anchoring of inflation expectations, chief investment officer Vincent Manuel and investment strategy analyst Adrien Roure say. That could fuel more sustainable wage inflation, although this isn't Indosuez WM's current core scenario. In Europe, the inflation trend will be driven primarily by the resolution to the energy shock, they say. Indosuez WM expects short-term volatility, but with the ebbing of supply-chain tensions, inflation should come back down after spring and gradually move closer to the central banks' targets in 2023. It is forecast at 4.5%-5% in the U.S. and 2.5%-3% in Europe in the coming months. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT - Chinese home sales could remain weak in 2022 as homebuyers adopt a 'wait-and-see' approach amid weakening new home prices and developers' liquidity stress, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. Even though the rating company says "home buyers' sentiment remains at a high level relative to previous years," this may not be translated into actual sales as consumers remain spooked by defaults in the debt-laden sector. Home sales could pick up in the medium term, helped by improving affordability and the expected relaxation of the housing registration policy, or Hukou, in major cities which may spur more rural-to-urban migration, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  adds. (yongchang.chin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2021 04:54 ET (09:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211220ehck000ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
