\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2021\par 14:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0815 ET - The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are nothing short of a sign of desperation and are more likely to undermine its credibility than halt the lira's slide, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. "More extreme measures, such as capital controls, cannot be ruled out," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analysts say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep interest rates on hold over the next three to six months after cutting rates by 500 basis points since September but real inflation-adjusted rates will fall as inflation accelerates further, sending the lira lower, they say. Turkey's weakening economic fundamentals and tighter global financial conditions will add pressure on the lira, they say.USD/TRY rises 7% to a record high of 17.8163, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0759 ET - The euro could fall versus the dollar as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to delay monetary policy tightening if stricter coronavirus are imposed throughout the eurozone, BDSwiss Group says. "The U.S. is structurally incapable of instituting such widespread measures because of the federal system, which leaves such decisions up to the individual states," BDSwiss analyst Marshall Gittler says. The Netherlands has reimposed a strict lockdown while Germany has set new entry requirements for people from the U.K. and several other European countries. EUR/USD is last up 0.3% at 1.1268. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 ET - The Australian dollar falls on risk aversion but it's still one of the better-performing G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far in December, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. Leveraged funds have scaled back AUD short positions as Chinese policymakers signal more support and Australia's economy and labor market recover, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. The "downside risks" for AUD have eased in the near-term but it's "premature" to start building long AUD positions given the threat still posed by the Omicron coronavirus variant and China's property slowdown, he says. Short positions bet on an asset price falling and long position expect it to rise. AUD/USD drops 0.4% to 0.7103 after earlier hitting a near two-week low of 0.7082, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 ET - Brazil's inflation outlook improved slightly for this year and got slightly worse for next year, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  's weekly survey of economists. The median forecast by 134 economists for 2021 declined to 10.04%, from 10.05% seven days ago, the second consecutive weekly improvement after 35 weeks of the forecast getting higher. The median forecast for next year increased to 5.03% from 5.02%. The bank last week raised its estimate for inflation for 2021 to 10.2% in its quarterly inflation report, up from 8.5% in the September report. The bank also raised its forecast for 2022 inflation, to 4.7% from 3.7% three months ago. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0651 ET - MSF Investment Management expects eurozone government bond yields to rise next year, causing a modest 10-20 basis-point widening in spreads, Annalisa Piazza, fixed income research analyst at \uc2 MFS Investment Management\uc2  tells Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "The path for yields should be moving higher," she says. The combination of "slower QE and net government bond supply being more positive" should lead to some bear steepening on the curve, where long-term market rates rise faster than short-term rates, she says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  's flexibility on reinvestments from the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme is a strong backstop to avoid unwarranted widening of bond-yield spreads, however, she says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided a "relatively clear" message with a surprising amount of detail on its plans to scale back quantitative easing in 2022 at Thursday's policy meeting, Annalisa Piazza, fixed income research analyst at \uc2 MFS Investment Management\uc2 , tells Dow Jones Newswires. "I was surprised to see so many details, I was expecting them to err more on the cautious side and wait," she said. "Probably the 'hawkish' side of the Governing Council pushed hard on that," she says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  will end the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme as scheduled in March 2022 and partially offset that with a temporary increase in the regular Asset Purchase Programme. MFS IM had $668.7 billion assets under management on Nov. 30, 2021. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0632 ET - Sterling's gains after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raised interest rates on Thursday proved limited, as the move was largely priced into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ActivTrades says. The pound is now weaker on concerns about the prospect of another lockdown to contain rising Omicron cases, ActivTrades Director \uc2 Ricardo Evangelista\uc2  says. "Such measures may be needed to avoid overwhelming the country's health system, but will also further hurt the economy. "Another headwind for sterling is the political crisis brewing in the U.K., with the resignation of Brexit minister David Frost seen by many as part of a wider movement to challenge Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  's leadership, Evangelista says. GBP/USD drops 0.4% to 1.3194 and EUR/GBP rises 0.6% to 0.8542. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 ET - Widespread risk aversion on concerns over the Omicron coronavirus variant and U.S. Democratic Senator Joe Manchin's announcement that he won't back President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's spending bill leaves sterling vulnerable, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. "Sterling is a convenient punch bag on days like this," SocGen \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kit Juckes says. Short positions that bet on the pound falling versus the safe-haven Japanese yen could gather momentum if the pair drops below 149, he says. GBP/USD also has plenty of scope to remain under pressure while the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  argues over whether to impose tighter coronavirus restrictions in England, he says. GBP/JPY declines 0.4% to 149.871 and GBP/USD falls 0.4% to 1.3191. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 ET - Bitcoin and other cryptocurrency prices fall as fears over restrictions to halt the spread of the Omicron coronavirus variant weigh on risk appetite. "Cryptocurrencies are trading in parallel to the risk appetite right now, and in the absence of breaking news or impactful tweets, we could expect the negative pressure to continue," \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2  analyst Ipek Ozkardeskaya says. "The next important technical level to watch in Bitcoin is $45,000, a touch below this level, there is another major Fibonacci support on July-November rally, which could further call the death of the latest rebound." Bitcoin, the leading cryptocurrency, drops 2.4% to $45,986, according to CoinDesk. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0511 ET - The steep rise in Covid-19 cases in the U.K. due to the spread of the Omicron variant will hit the country's short-term economic growth, economists at Pantheon Macroeconomics say. In a best-case scenario in which the U.K. doesn't implement additional restrictions, GDP would decrease by about 1% in January compared with November, they say. The drop in activity will be much more severe if hospital admissions rise at such a rapid pace that a full lockdown is required in January, with GDP expected to fall 6% from November's level in this scenario, Pantheon says. If the U.K. had to return to a partial lockdown similar to the one experienced between April and May this year, GDP would fall around 2% in January from November, it says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0454 ET - The Turkish lira falls to new record lows and the sellff is likely to persist as \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks poised to cut interest rates further in 2022, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. The bank last Thursday signalled it could keep its rates unchanged in the 2022 first quarter but additional rate cuts next year remain likely due to pressure from President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  , \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  economist Gokce Celik says. "Inflation will likely rise sharply in the coming months and depreciation pressure on the TRY could continue while other central banks are tightening." Meanwhile, the "inadequacy" of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves means \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely be sizeable enough to stabilize the lira, Celik says. USD/TRY rises 5.3% to 17.5013, near the record high of 17.5466 reached earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 ET - London gold prices are down 0.1% at $1,802.70 a troy ounce, although they have crept up so far in December, thanks to safe-haven appetite for the yellow metal, \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  's Ipek Ozkardeskaya says. While Ozkardeskaya doesn't expect any further rallies from gold, she says that may change "if there is a significant erosion in the risk sentiment and a further selloff across the risk assets." Ultimately, signals of tightening monetary policy out of the Fed makes the dollar a safer haven than gold at the moment, she adds. \uc2 LME\uc2  three-month copper futures are down 1% at $9,355 a metric ton, with equities and oil markets dropping and the dollar having risen in recent weeks, making dollar-denominated commodities more expensive for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holders. (david.hodari@wsj.com ; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2021 08:15 ET (13:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211220ehck001fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
