\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira at New Record Lows, Likely to Extend Losses in 2022 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2021\par 11:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0954 GMT - The Turkish lira falls to new record lows and the sellff is likely to persist as \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks poised to cut interest rates further in 2022, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. The bank last Thursday signalled it could keep its rates unchanged in the 2022 first quarter but additional rate cuts next year remain likely due to pressure from President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 , Unicredit economist Gokce Celik says. "Inflation will likely rise sharply in the coming months and depreciation pressure on the TRY could continue while other central banks are tightening." Meanwhile, the "inadequacy" of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves means \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely be sizeable enough to stabilize the lira, Celik says. USD/TRY rises 5.3% to 17.5013, near the record high of 17.5466 reached earlier, according to FactSet. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20-12-21 1016GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020211220ehck00082\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
