\par\par \b \uc2 Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Crisis Escalates After  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Again Cuts Rates -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jared Malsin and Caitlin Ostroff \par \uc2 955 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2021\par 05:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ISTANBUL -- \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bowed to political pressure to slash interest rates, defying soaring inflation and deepening a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis that has dogged the economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its benchmark rate to 14% from 15% on Thursday, its fourth-consecutive rate cut since September.\par \par \uc2 Previous rate cuts demanded by Turkish President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  triggered a collapse in the Turkish lira. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 40% of its value against the dollar since September, making it one of the worst performing investments in the world.\par \par \uc2 The lira slid another 3.6% on Thursday -- crossing 15 lira to the dollar for the first time. The crisis has made tens of millions of Turks poorer, sparking protests and eroding popular support for Mr. Erdogan.\par \par \uc2 Investors and Turkish residents have sold the lira in recent days expecting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will once again slash rates when it releases a monetary policy statement at 6 a.m. ET. In a series of rate cuts in recent months, the bank has lowered the key one-week repo rate to 15% from 19% in early September.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Erdogan opposes higher interest rates, believing they stoke inflation -- the opposite of what economies around the world have experienced through history. Earlier this week, the country's new finance minister told a Turkish news organization that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't raise interest rates and voiced support for the president's unorthodox approach.\par \par \uc2 Economists and investors expect that the further reduction in interest rates will cause another leg down in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Turks' diminished purchasing power could eventually lead to a slowdown in the economy, or even a recession, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "The market has gone beyond giving signals. It is literally shouting at the government to not do this anymore," said Selim Sazak, a research fellow at Ankara's \uc2 Bilkent University\uc2 , who advises a Turkish opposition party.\par \par \uc2 Ordinary Turks have been fleeing the lira in recent months, rushing to swap their assets for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold.\par \par \uc2 In Istanbul's Grand Bazaar, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold traders jammed into an alleyway, shouting requests to buy and sell while awaiting the bank's decision. "Get ready for a bomb to explode," said one, minutes before the bank announced the cut.\par \par \uc2 "Normally when the price of gold rises this much, people sell their gold and buy a house or a car. But in this situation, there is an ever-increasing demand for gold," said Ozgur Anik, a gold dealer in the Grand Bazaar. "It helps our business but our country is getting poorer."\par \par \uc2 A strange quiet settled on the market when the decision was announced, as traders puzzled over the news. Then a frenzy erupted with people shouting, "Is anyone selling?" One trader selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was instantly swarmed by a crowd of buyers.\par \par \uc2 The weakening lira is stoking inflation, driving up the cost of importing necessary goods such as medicines, certain food items and natural gas, which are denominated in dollars and euros.\par \par \uc2 Official data for November showed that inflation in Turkey reached 21.3% from a year prior, though analysts estimate it is likely higher.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the rate cut, Mr. Erdogan announced that the country would raise its minimum wage to 4,250 lira per month, equivalent to $287, from 2,825.90 lira a month, equivalent to $191.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, Turkey has repeatedly cut interest rates as a means to buoy growth, only to end up hiking rates strongly when inflation gets too high. Investors worry that the current crisis could continue since there is no one left within the Turkish government with the power to oppose Mr. Erdogan's policies. The president has fired a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs and other senior officials who stood in his way.\par \par \uc2 "It's just too difficult to imagine an about-turn in the current situation, which is different from previous episodes," said Kieran Curtis, an emerging-markets fund manager at Aberdeen Standard Investments. "There's usually been someone to argue that interest rates needed to go up by the time we got to this level of intensity of lira weakness."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made several \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in recent weeks, selling foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and buying the lira, in an effort to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to what it has deemed as "unhealthy price formations in exchange rates."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has concerned investors, who estimate that \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liabilities than assets, giving it little firepower to steady the lira. Haluk Burumcekci, an economist based in Istanbul, estimates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position has fallen by a net $5.5 billion this month, during which it worked to stabilize the lira.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy statement on Thursday, officials said they would monitor the impact of recent rate cuts during the first three months of next year and reassess their policies.\par \par \uc2 "This talk that there will be a reassessment of policy in the first quarter, that can be interpreted many ways," said Erik Meyersson, a senior economist at Swedish bank Handelsbanken. "Their communication is not informative."\par \par \uc2 Turkish companies and banks hold substantial foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debts, which become harder to pay off as the lira plummets. S&P Global Ratings shifted its outlook for Turkey to negative from stable last week. The ratings agency noted that recent interest-rate cuts and the depreciation of the lira are likely to further boost inflation.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jared Malsin at jared.malsin@wsj.com and Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 17-12-21 0423GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020211217ehch0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
