\par\par \b \uc2 Turkish Lira Falls to Record Low on Fears of Further Rate Cuts -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2021\par 11:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1003 GMT - The Turkish lira falls to a new record low versus the dollar on the prospect of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cutting interest rates further on Thursday. The lira falls despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times in the past two weeks in an attempt to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's selloff, triggered by the bank following President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  's unorthodox strategy of lowering rates to combat inflation. "The TRY has plunged again this morning as investors worry about the prospect of another rate cut from \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. USD/TRY rises 4.3% to an all-time high of 14.6412, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT - Policy decisions from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve later this week could push the euro versus the dollar to new lows for the year, ING says. One of the challenges the \uc2 ECB\uc2  faces at Thursday's policy meeting will be exiting its Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme without causing too much disruption to the European bond market, particularly bonds of struggling peripheral countries, ING analysts say. "The core outcome, however, should be the \uc2 ECB\uc2  wanting to keep all its options open into 2022." In contrast, the Fed is under political pressure to rein in its loose policies at Wednesday's meeting, the analysts say. ING sees EUR/USD potentially falling below the "1.1200 lows." EUR/USD drops 0.4% to 1.1268. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT - Brent crude oil is up 0.6% at $75.60 a barrel and WTI futures are up 0.7% at $72.16 a barrel after both benchmarks extended their December gains last week. The prospect for the oil demand impact from the Omicron coronavirus variant to be limited is one of the factors behind last week's gains, Oanda's \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2  says. On top of that, "OPEC+ continues to have a firm handle on the direction of prices and can disrupt any selloffs with a quick reverse of their output increase," he adds. If the mild winter that Europe is currently experiencing turns colder and the continent moves beyond its current spate of Covid-19 restrictions, the oil price rally could accelerate, he adds. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  releases its monthly market report Monday. (david.hodari@wsj.com ; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT - France's Minister of Economy and Finance \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2  said Monday that he expects the French economy to expand by more than 6.25% in 2021, even if the ministry preferred to keep this forecast of the government for the time being. "We will keep the official forecast of 6.25%," he said on BMFTV-RMC, but he added that he thinks the French economy "will do better." \uc2 France's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has revised its forecast upward to 6.7% GDP growth in 2021. France's debt will be "around 115%" of GDP, the minister added. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT - Copper prices strengthen as investors look ahead to a key meeting of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  later in the week. Three-month copper on the \uc2 LME\uc2  gains 0.4% to $9,490.50 a metric ton. The metal, which is often seen as a gauge of global economic health, is ticking higher as central banks across the world are in the process of normalizing monetary policy as global economies recover. The Fed, which meets Tuesday and Wednesday, is expected to signal that its tapering of pandemic-era stimulus measures will quicken, thanks to a faster-than-expected rebound in the U.S. economy. Other base metals also rise Monday, with aluminum up 1% at $2,636 a ton. (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT - Spain's 'A-' rating and stable outlook was affirmed by \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  in its review on Friday. The ratings firm says Spain's economic recovery is under way following the contraction and deterioration in its fiscal position caused by the pandemic and is likely to gather pace next year as European funds are deployed to a larger extent and international tourism continues to recover. Spain's real GDP is likely to grow 6.3% in 2022, before slowing to 3.6% in 2023, says \uc2 Fitch\uc2 . Spanish 10-year bonds underperform eurozone peers, with the 10-year yield rising 3.4 basis points to 0.383%, with the selloff being slightly stronger than elsewhere, but thin liquidity may exacerbate moves. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT - The dollar rises ahead of a busy week for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings. Wednesday's \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  announcement should lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while Thursday's decisions by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  are likely to dent the euro and pound, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . The dollar "is likely to benefit from the Fed announcing an acceleration in the speed of its tapering process," it says. This prospect, combined with the \uc2 ECB\uc2  likely remaining "quite accommodative" should continue to weigh on EUR/USD. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is likely to delay plans to start raising interest rates, which won't help the pound, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The DXY dollar index rises 0.2% to 96.2780, while EUR/USD falls 0.2% to 1.1288 and GBP/USD loses 0.2% to 1.3237. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT - Oxford Economics has revised down its near-term eurozone growth forecast, owing to deteriorating health conditions and the emergence of the Omicron variant. This adds to existing headwinds relating to surging inflation, persistent equipment and labor shortages, and a gradual withdrawal of policy support, economists at Oxford Economics say. Oxford Economics now forecasts eurozone quarterly growth in the fourth quarter and 1Q 2022 to average just 0.4%, down from 0.7% quarter-on-quarter in November. "We expect the Omicron variant to send a chill, but not freeze, the eurozone recovery over the winter months," Oxford Economics' senior economist Daniel Kral says. The cut in growth during the winter quarters is expected to be fully compensated later next year, Kral says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0801 GMT - German retailers are being increasingly affected by stricter coronavirus rules, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. According to \uc2 Google\uc2  mobility data, there were 12% fewer customers in shops recently than before the pandemic. A week ago, there were 10% fewer customers than pre-pandemic, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The drop in customer numbers at stores isn't as drastic as for restaurants, which recently counted around 40% fewer guests than before the pandemic, the German bank says. However, the trend for shops is still clearly downward, economists at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  say. "The number of new infections continues to decline, but soon the Omicron variant is likely to cause the number of cases to rise again significantly," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT - The Austrian and Italian Treasuries will conduct their respective last government-bond auctions of the year on Tuesday, rounding off bond sales in the eurozone for 2021. Austria will auction EUR805 million in 0% February 2031 bonds, while Italy will launch a new 0% December 2024 BTP, offering EUR3 billion-EUR3.5 billion at the upcoming auction. Spain and Italy canceled their remaining government-bond auctions for the year; Spain's auction was originally scheduled for Dec. 16, while Italy canceled auctions scheduled for Dec. 29 and Dec. 30. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0739 GMT - Wholesale prices in Germany rose 16.6% in November compared with November 2020, the German statistics office says. This is the biggest increase year-on-year since the beginning of the calculation of the wholesale prices index in 1962, Destatis says. The high rates of change for wholesale prices in the annual comparison derive from increased prices for raw materials and intermediate products, Destatis says. The largest impact on the year-on-year price comparison was that of the increase in prices for mineral oil products, which rose 62.4% year-on-year. From October to November, wholesale prices rose 1.3%, Destatis says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain a "dovish standout" among the major central banks, announcing enhanced flexibility for the standard asset purchase facility even as the Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme expires on schedule in March 2022, Pietro Baffico, economist at \uc2 Abrdn\uc2  says. "We think the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will deliver an additional volume of purchases above the current EUR20 billion of the monthly net Asset Purchase Programme, with enhanced flexibility around these purchases, for an additional period of about 9 to 12 months," he says. \uc2 Abrdn\uc2  anticipates an up to EUR240 billion additional capacity, but adds that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  may not necessarily use this envelope in full. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 13, 2021 05:03 ET (10:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211213ehcd000q6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal60