\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2021\par 12:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1120 GMT - The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's attempts to stabilize the lira through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are bound to fail, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. Last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "quite obviously" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  without much success in terms of a sustainable lira recovery, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Tatha Ghose says. "Typically, either a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeds in convincing the market of its superiority in the first try, or it will never succeed--as any subsequent attempt will be conducted with lower amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, i.e. with less firepower." The bank's policy, which involves cutting interest rates despite high inflation, is "unhealthy" and the lira's weakness reflects that, Ghose says. USD/TRY rises 1.9% to 14.1384, having earlier hit a record high of 14.6412, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1111 GMT - India's Sensex ended lower amid a mixed performance in regional equities. Losses were led by financial and auto sectors. \uc2 Bajaj Finance\uc2  slid 3.1% and \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  lost 1.4%, but \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2  was the outlier with a 2.4% rise. For auto makers, \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra\uc2  and \uc2 Bajaj Auto\uc2  weakened 1.9% and 1.0%, respectively, but \uc2 Maruti Suzuki\uc2  added 1.2%. Investors are likely cautious ahead of a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions this week, with the FOMC meeting on Wednesday being the main event. The benchmark Sensex fell 0.9% to 58283.42. (clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1107 GMT - Should the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decide to borrow some extra time and delay further decisions to February 2022, eurozone government bond yields, and the periphery in particular, might show some volatility near term, also considering the year-end low liquidity conditions, says Annalisa Piazza, fixed-income research analyst at \uc2 MFS Investment Management\uc2 . More in the medium term, however, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's policy is likely to remain relatively supportive "as the economies still need fiscal support in the recovery phase," she says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  isn't willing to let fragmentation risks to hinder the transmission of past monetary policy accommodation, Piazza says. MFS IM doesn't rule out some modest widening in spreads in the short term, due to high uncertainties that deepen the internal division within the ECB 's Governing Council. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1104 GMT - Sterling falls against the dollar after U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  warned of oncoming "tidal wave" of cases of the Omicron coronavirus variant. Concerns about the spread of Omicron in the U.K. has prompted the market to scale back interest rate rise expectations before Thursday's \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policy meeting, ING analysts say. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  will "want to keep its options open" ahead of a possible rate rise in February, they say. Meanwhile, Wednesday's Federal Reserve meeting should see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announce a faster pace of tapering of its bond purchases, they say. GBP/USD drops 0.1% to 1.3250. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1056 GMT The March Gilt future contract, currently trading at 127.36, is on the upside and stands above its 50-period moving average on a 30-min chart. The intraday RSI also stands within its buying area between 50 and 70 and confirms the bullish bias. As long as 126.68 holds as a support, further advance is expected beyond horizontal resistance at 127.35 and toward Dec. 8 top at 127.67 in extension. A third target is set at horizontal resistance at 128.00. Alternatively, below horizontal support at 126.68, further weakness toward horizontal support at 126.44 and toward 126.16 would be expected. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 1022 GMT - October's U.K. gross domestic product data, which showed that the country's economy grew by a marginal 0.1% on month before the Omicron variant news, is likely to postpone any increase in rates, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. Even though that employment and inflation data have topped expectations, uncertainty about the near-term economic outlook is elevated enough to suggest that the Bank of England 's Monetary Policy Committee meeting likely will vote unanimously to keep rates at 0.10% on Thursday, Pantheon's chief U.K. economist \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2  says. "We now expect the committee to wait until March to begin to raise Bank Rate, given that the economic situation likely will not be any brighter by the time of its next meeting at the beginning of February," he says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1015 GMT - The global economy will continue to recover from the pandemic-induced shocked in 2022 and it is projected to expand 4.2%, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's group chief economist \uc2 Erik F. Nielsen\uc2  says. The forecast is lower than the 4.6% expansion previously expected as more severe and longer-lasting supply bottlenecks as well as higher inflation are likely to slow growth, he says. However, the outlook is subject to many uncertainties such as the evolution of the Covid-19 pandemic, supply-side strains, energy prices, behavior of central banks, and increasing geopolitical risks, he says. "We'll be entering 2022 with more unknowns--from economics, to politics and policies, to markets--than I can recall ever having faced in my almost 40 years as an economist," Nielsen says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 GMT - The Turkish lira falls to a new record low versus the dollar on the prospect of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cutting interest rates further on Thursday. The lira falls despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times in the past two weeks in an attempt to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's selloff, triggered by the bank following President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  's unorthodox strategy of lowering rates to combat inflation. "The TRY has plunged again this morning as investors worry about the prospect of another rate cut from \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. USD/TRY rises 4.3% to an all-time high of 14.6412, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT - Policy decisions from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve later this week could push the euro versus the dollar to new lows for the year, ING says. One of the challenges the \uc2 ECB\uc2  faces at Thursday's policy meeting will be exiting its Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme without causing too much disruption to the European bond market, particularly bonds of struggling peripheral countries, ING analysts say. "The core outcome, however, should be the \uc2 ECB\uc2  wanting to keep all its options open into 2022." In contrast, the Fed is under political pressure to rein in its loose policies at Wednesday's meeting, the analysts say. ING sees EUR/USD potentially falling below the "1.1200 lows." EUR/USD drops 0.4% to 1.1268. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT - Brent crude oil is up 0.6% at $75.60 a barrel and WTI futures are up 0.7% at $72.16 a barrel after both benchmarks extended their December gains last week. The prospect for the oil demand impact from the Omicron coronavirus variant to be limited is one of the factors behind last week's gains, Oanda's \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2  says. On top of that, "OPEC+ continues to have a firm handle on the direction of prices and can disrupt any selloffs with a quick reverse of their output increase," he adds. If the mild winter that Europe is currently experiencing turns colder and the continent moves beyond its current spate of Covid-19 restrictions, the oil price rally could accelerate, he adds. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  releases its monthly market report Monday. (david.hodari@wsj.com ; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT - France's Minister of Economy and Finance \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2  said Monday that he expects the French economy to expand by more than 6.25% in 2021, even if the ministry preferred to keep this forecast of the government for the time being. "We will keep the official forecast of 6.25%," he said on BMFTV-RMC, but he added that he thinks the French economy "will do better." \uc2 France's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has revised its forecast upward to 6.7% GDP growth in 2021. France's debt will be "around 115%" of GDP, the minister added. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT - Copper prices strengthen as investors look ahead to a key meeting of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  later in the week. Three-month copper on the \uc2 LME\uc2  gains 0.4% to $9,490.50 a metric ton. The metal, which is often seen as a gauge of global economic health, is ticking higher as central banks across the world are in the process of normalizing monetary policy as global economies recover. The Fed, which meets Tuesday and Wednesday, is expected to signal that its tapering of pandemic-era stimulus measures will quicken, thanks to a faster-than-expected rebound in the U.S. economy. Other base metals also rise Monday, with aluminum up 1% at $2,636 a ton. (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 13, 2021 06:20 ET (11:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211213ehcd00114\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
