\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Revises Outlooks on 13 Turkish Banks to Negative on Sovereign Outlook Change\b0\par\par\uc2 5,656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2021\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-10 December 2021: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has revised the Outlooks on 13 Turkish banks' support-driven Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) to Negative from Stable. The Outlooks on 20 banks' support-driven Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDRs have also been revised to Negative from Stable.\par \par \uc2 The Outlook revisions follow the change in the Outlook on Turkey's Long-Term IDRs to Negative from Stable. The latter was driven, among other factors, by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2 's premature monetary policy easing cycle, and the prospect of further rate cuts or additional economic stimulus ahead of the 2023 presidential election. Together these factors have led to a deterioration in domestic confidence - reflected in a sharp depreciation of the Turkish lira and rising inflation, creating risks to macroeconomic and financial stability and potentially re-igniting external financing risks.\par \par \uc2 We believe that the risks to macroeconomic and financial stability and external financing could increase the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system. This has driven the revision of the Outlooks on foreign-owned Turkish banks' LT IDRs to Negative. The Negative Outlooks on banks with ratings driven by state support reflect the sovereign Outlook, and the risk of a further weakening of the authorities' ability to provide support in case of need.\par \par \uc2 The Viability Ratings of the banks under review (where assigned) are unaffected by the sovereign Outlook revision, as are the VRs of other Fitch-rated Turkish banks, whose IDRs are driven by their VRs. This reflects our view that risks to standalone credit profiles are captured in the current ratings and in Negative Outlooks, despite persistent and heightened operating environment pressures. We consider risks to banks' financial profiles and VRs to be skewed to the downside, given macro and financial market developments.\par \par \uc2 Fitch has assigned Government Support Ratings (GSRs) or Shareholder Support Ratings (SSRs) to all banks under review. This follows the withdrawal of the banks' respective Support Ratings and Support Rating Floors as they are no longer relevant to our coverage following the publication of the updated Bank Rating Criteria on 12 November.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, GSRS AND SENIOR UNSECURED DEBT RATINGS OF STATE-OWNED BANKS AND TSKB\par \par \uc2 The Outlooks on the LTFC IDRs of Turkiye Ihracat Kredi Bankasi A.S. (\uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 ) and Turkiye Kalkinma ve Yatirim Bankasi (TKYB), the state-owned development banks, and Turkiye Emlak Katilim Bankasi A.S. (Emlak Katilim) and Vakif Katilim Bankasi AS (Vakif Katilim), two state-owned participation banks, have been revised to Negative from Stable, mirroring the Outlook on the sovereign, reflecting increased risks to the sovereign's ability to provide support in case of need.\par \par \uc2 Fitch regards the Turkish authorities as having a high propensity to support the four banks, given their majority state-ownership, policy roles (development banks), strategic importance to the authorities and the record of support.\par \par \uc2 TKYB's LTFC IDR is equalised with Turkey's sovereign rating, as reflected in its 'bb-' GSR, reflecting its small size relative to sovereign resources, still largely treasury-guaranteed funding base and the medium-term tenor of its non-guaranteed funding (as a result of which its potential need for support over the medium term is likely to be limited).\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 's 'b+' GSR, one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR and TKYB's GSR, reflects the bank's strategic policy role as the country's export credit agency but also its considerably larger balance sheet and volumes of external market funding when compared with TKYB. This is an important rating driver in light of Turkey's weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) reserves position.\par \par \uc2 The senior unsecured debt ratings of \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2  have been affirmed in line with its FC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Fitch has assigned GSRs of 'b' to four state-owned commercial banks - Turkiye Cumhuriyeti Ziraat Bankasi A.S. (Ziraat) and \uc2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi T.A.O\uc2 . (\uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 ), both considered systemically important and the two participation banks, Emlak Katilim and Vakif Katilim, and the privately-owned development bank Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S. (TSKB). Their GSRs are two notches below Turkey's LTFC IDR despite a high sovereign propensity to provide support, reflecting Turkey's weak financial flexibility to provide support in FC, in case of need.\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDRs of Ziraat, \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 , Emlak Katilim, Vakif Katilim, \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 , TKYB and TSKB are equalised with the sovereign rating at 'BB-' on the basis of support, reflecting a high sovereign propensity and ability to provide support in LC. The revision of the Outlooks to Negative from Stable mirror the sovereign rating action.\par \par \uc2 LTLC IDRS AND GSRS OF AKBANK, ISBANK AND YKB\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDRs of \uc2 Akbank T.A.S\uc2 . (Akbank), \uc2 Turkiye Is Bankasi A.S\uc2 . (Isbank) and \uc2 Yapi ve Kredi Bankasi A.S\uc2 . (YKB), have been affirmed at 'B+', one notch below the sovereign rating, and their Outlooks revised to Negative from Stable, mirroring the sovereign Outlook. At this rating level, the three banks' LTLC IDRs are now underpinned by both sovereign support and their standalone credit profiles. Our view of support is based on the banks' systemic importance, solid market shares and franchises, but also considers their private ownership.\par \par \uc2 The three banks' LTFC IDRs, also B+/Negative, continue to be driven by the banks' VRs, reflecting our view that sovereign support in FC cannot be relied upon - as reflected in their GSRs of 'b-'.\par \par \uc2 IDRS AND SSRS OF FOREIGN-OWNED BANKS, SENIOR UNSECURED DEBT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDRs of nine foreign-owned Turkish banks (\uc2 Alternatifbank A.S\uc2 ., Burgan Bank A.S., \uc2 Denizbank A.S\uc2 ., Kuveyt Turk Katilim Bankasi A.S. (Kuveyt Turk), ING Bank A.S., \uc2 Turkiye Garanti Bankasi\uc2  (\uc2 Garanti BBVA\uc2 ), \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi\uc2  (TEB), Turkiye Finans Katilim Bankasi A.S. (TFKB), QNB Finansbank A.S. are driven by shareholder support from higher-rated parent banks.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's view of support reflects the banks' strategic importance to their respective parents, ownership, integration and roles within their wider groups. Senior debt ratings, where assigned, are aligned with the banks' LT IDRs, reflecting average recovery prospects in case of default.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the foreign-owned banks' LTFC IDRs at 'B+', one notch below the sovereign LTFC IDR. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their FC obligations more likely than a sovereign default, given the weak external financial position. We believe government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk could increase following the latest market volatility, driving the revision of the Outlooks on the banks' LTFC IDRs to Negative.\par \par \uc2 The foreign-owned banks' LTLC IDRs - which are also driven by shareholder support - have been affirmed at 'BB-', one notch above their LTFC IDRs, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in LC. The Outlooks have been revised to Negative from Stable, mirroring the sovereign Outlook change.\par \par \uc2 The respective senior unsecured debt ratings have been affirmed along in line with their FC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF KUVEYT TURK, \uc2 GARANTI BBVA\par \par \uc2 The subordinated notes' ratings of Kuveyt Turk - issued through its SPV KT21 T2 Sukuk - and \uc2 Garanti BBVA\uc2  are notched down once from their support-driven IDRs and have been affirmed along with their anchor ratings. The notching in each case includes one notch for loss severity and zero notches for non-performance risk (relative to their anchor ratings).\par \par \uc2 BANK SUBSIDIARIES\par \par \uc2 Ziraat Katilim Bankasi A.S.'s ratings are equalised with those of its parent (Ziraat) reflecting its role as a core subsidiary. Our view of support considers its strategic importance in helping develop participation banking in Turkey, its role as the sole provider of Islamic banking products for the Ziraat group, its high integration and shared branding. The Outlooks on the bank's LT IDRs mirror those on its parent.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings of all issuers included in this review have been affirmed following the sovereign rating action. This reflects our view that their creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed as a result of the revision of the Outlooks on their LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 LT IDRS AND GSRS OF TKYB, \uc2 TURK EXIMBANK\uc2 , VAKIF KATILIM AND EMLAK KATILIM\par \par \uc2 Negative rating action on Turkey would likely lead to similar action on these banks. The banks' GSRs could be downgraded, and as a result their LTFC IDRs also downgraded, if further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support in FC for the banks from the Turkish authorities.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of Vakif Katilim's GSR would only lead to a downgrade of its LTFC IDR if the bank's VR was also downgraded.\par \par \uc2 TKYB's GSR could be downgraded by one notch and its LTFC IDR downgraded, if its proportion of non-guaranteed funding increases materially - particularly if Fitch believes this to be indicative of a weakening in TKYB's policy role - or if its balance sheet size sharply increases. \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 's GSR could be downgraded due to a material weakening in the bank's policy role.\par \par \uc2 All four banks' LTLC IDRs could be downgraded if Turkey's LTLC IDR was downgraded, Fitch believes the sovereign's propensity to support the banks has reduced or Fitch believes there is an increased likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in LC.\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 's senior unsecured debt ratings are primarily sensitive to changes in the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 LTLC IDRS AND GSRS OF ZIRAAT, VAKIFBANK, TSKB, AKBANK, ISBANK AND YKB\par \par \uc2 The LTLC IDRs of Ziraat, \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 , TSKB, Akbank, Isbank and YKB could be downgraded if Turkey's LTLC IDR was downgraded, if Fitch believes the sovereign's propensity to provide support in LC has reduced or our view of the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector increases. For Akbank, Isbank and YKB, this would only lead to a downgrade if the banks' VRs were also downgraded.\par \par \uc2 The banks' GSRs could be downgraded if Fitch concludes the reliability of support for the banks in FC from the Turkish authorities has weakened.\par \par \uc2 LT IDRS, SSRS AND SENIOR DEBT RATINGS OF FOREIGN-OWNED BANKS\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of Turkey's sovereign rating would likely lead to similar action on the banks. Increased government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk would likely also lead to negative rating action on the banks' LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The banks' ratings are also sensitive to Fitch's view of their shareholders' ability and propensity to provide support, in case of need. For banks with VRs at the level of their LTFC IDRs, a reduced likelihood of shareholder support would only lead to a downgrade of the banks' ratings if their VRs were also downgraded.\par \par \uc2 The respective senior unsecured debt ratings are primarily sensitive to changes in the banks' LTFC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 LT IDRS, GSRS OF TKYB, \uc2 TURK EXIMBANK\uc2 , VAKIF KATILIM AND EMLAK KATILIM\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the banks' LT IDRs are unlikely given the Negative Outlook on Turkey's sovereign rating. A revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would likely lead to similar action on the banks' LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 In the medium term, a material improvement in Turkey's external finances and net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, that resulted in a marked strengthening in the sovereign's ability to support banks in FC, could lead to positive rating action on the banks' GSRs and LTFC IDRs - with the exception of TKYB, as its rating is already equalised with the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 Turk Eximbank\uc2 's senior unsecured debt ratings are primarily sensitive to changes in the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 LTLC IDRS, GSRS OF ZIRAAT, VAKIFBANK, TSKB, AKBANK, ISBANK AND YKB\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the banks' LTLC IDRs are unlikely given the Negative Outlook on the sovereign rating. A revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would likely lead to similar action on the banks' LTLC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 In the medium term, a material improvement in Turkey's external finances and net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a marked strengthening in the sovereign's ability to support banks in FC, could lead to positive rating action on the banks' GSRs. However, only a multi-notch upgrade of the banks' GSRs would lead to an upgrade of their LTFC IDRs as their VRs are already rated one (Ziraat, \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 , TSKB) or two (Akbank, Isbank and YKB) notches above their respective GSRs.\par \par \uc2 LT IDRS, SSRS AND SENIOR DEBT RATINGS OF FOREIGN-OWNED BANKS\par \par \uc2 Upgrades of the banks' LT IDRs are unlikely given the Negative Outlook. However, a revision of the sovereign Outlook to Stable would likely lead to similar action on the banks' LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A material improvement in Turkey's external finances or net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a significant reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, could lead to an upgrade of the banks' LTFC IDRs to the level of Turkey's LTFC IDR. However, given Turkey's large external vulnerabilities and very weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, this is unlikely in the near term, in our view.\par \par \uc2 The respective senior unsecured debt ratings are primarily sensitive to changes in the banks' LTFC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF \uc2 GARANTI BBVA\uc2 , KUVEYT TURK\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt ratings of Garanti BBVA and Kuveyt Turk are primarily sensitive to changes in the banks' LTFC IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The ratings are also sensitive to a change in notching from their respective anchor ratings due to a revision in Fitch's assessment of the probability of the notes' non-performance risk or loss severity in the case of non-performance.\par \par \uc2 BANK SUBSIDIARIES\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Ziraat Katilim Bankasi are sensitive to changes in the LT IDRs of Ziraat.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Ratings of all issuers under review are sensitive to changes in their respective LTLC IDRs and their relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 All banks included in this review have ratings linked to the Turkish sovereign rating, given the ratings either rely on or are sensitive to our assessment of sovereign support or country risks.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 The state-owned banks - Ziraat, \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2 , Emlak Katilim, Vakif Katilim and Ziraat Katilim Bankasi have ESG Relevance Scores of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to a typical Relevance Score of '3' for comparable banks) due to potential government influence over their boards' effectiveness and management strategy in the challenging Turkish operating environment, which has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the Islamic banks' (Emlak Katilim, Vakif Katilim, Kuveyt Turk, TFKB, Ziraat Katilim Bankasi) ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure reflects their Islamic banking nature (in contrast to a typical ESG Relevance Score of '3' for comparable conventional banks). These banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and activities need to comply with sharia principles and rules, which entails additional costs, processes, disclosures, regulations, reporting and sharia audit. This has a negative impact on their credit profiles and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 The Islamic banks also have an ESG Relevance Score of '3' for Exposure to Social Impacts (in contrast to a typical ESG Relevance Score of '2' for comparable conventional banks), which reflects that Islamic banks have certain sharia limitations embedded in their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and obligations, although this only has a minimal credit impact on Islamic banks.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'/ ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on Fitch's ESG Relevance scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Cumhuriyeti Ziraat Bankasi A.S.; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rat