\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Erdogan promises more rate cuts as Bank of Turkey steps in to try to stabilise the lira\b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBRT) has been forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the face of the new collapse of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For the first time in seven years, the bank is getting rid of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The lira has risen as much as 7% but has fallen in high volatility. The previous falls have been caused by \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's statements. The president has promised more interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan had warned that there will be more interest rate cuts before the election to "free the economy" from dependence on "short-term foreign cash". He has also promised more jobs and lower inflation thanks to the interest rate cuts, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is making.\par \par \uc2 The elections are scheduled for 2023. CBRT at its last meeting hinted that the cuts would stop in December, after lowering rates to 15% from 16%. For some time now, the entity has lost its credibility because of Erdogan's interference. Last week it issued a statement blaming the market itself for the lira's decline.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish lira has bounced as much as 10% to $0.0764 after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that it is "directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market... for unhealthy price formations in exchange rates," according to the statement.\par \par \uc2 In the year the Turkish lira accumulates a depreciation of 43% by Erdogan's insistence on an unorthodox and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic policy. The country suffers an inflation rate close to 20%. Any economics textbook would advise raising interest rates to try to contain the price escalation. The CBRT's inflation target is set at 5%. Real interest rates (discounting inflation) are in very negative territory, almost -4%.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan has come to control the CBRT with an iron fist and has imposed his economic theories despite the risk to the country's financial stability. Last March he dismissed the governor of the institution, after the last rate hike that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertook in order to control inflation. Since then the Turkish lira has continued to plummet.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211201ehc1004i7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
