\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Lira plunge leaves Turkey in turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Turkey is right now a serial with no guaranteed happy ending. You only have to sit in a barbershop in Ankara to hear the same litany, before you've even taken off your mask. "The economy is bad, pretty bad." By the time the barber finishes with the scissors and moves on to singeing ear hairs with a spectacular flare, the lira is already worth less than when he started.\par \par \uc2 The country is not on fire, but it is in a bain-marie, like the frog in the story, while the temperature of the water keeps rising. If there is no panic, no queues at the bureaux de change, it is because Turkey, because of its recent history, is cured of its fright.\par \par \uc2 There are millions of Turks who have part of their savings denominated in euros or dollars, or bundles of banknotes under their mattresses. However, the combination of galloping inflation and the lira in free fall is something else this time.\par \par \uc2 Different from the meltdown of 2018, though still incomparable to the crisis of two decades ago, when an even worse inflationary wave threw the old forces into disarray and brought the ship of \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  and his Justice and Development Party (AKP) to power.\par \par \uc2 Barber Sinan, with premises in the affluent neighbourhood dedicated to the pirate Barbarossa, says he pays 8,000 liras in rent. This is \u199\'00\'C7ankaya - the heart of republican Turkey, with embassies and star-studded hotels - and it used to be a lot, but today the exchange rate is less than 640 euros. Even so, Sinan needs two hundred haircuts just to cover the rent. To this must be added "the four months of closure due to the pandemic".\par \par \uc2 The economy that is bad, quite bad, is his own, in small letters, and that of millions of Turks who have been squeezed by high inflation and low wages. But the economy in capital letters, that of the official reports, conveys a more nuanced message, despite the spectacular collapse of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Support The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given up on supporting the lira, a year after burning through its reserves.This has been worsened by the umpteenth cut in interest rates - on Thursday to 15% from 16% - contrary to orthodoxy which preaches that to contain inflation rates should be above this - officially, 19.9%.\par \par \uc2 Although the bars in Barbaros are full even on weekdays, a liquor store in the same neighbourhood - with good wines and better malt whiskies - confesses to having lived in more comfortable times, when the lira had a price tag. "I have to change the price tags every day," says Hassan. Upwards, of course.\par \par \uc2 His Angora cat glides among the hundreds of bottles, more expertly than others among the interest rates. "He has never broken any of them," says Has\u225\'00\'E1n, before adding: "Before, the customer would come in and take three wines almost without looking. Now he checks what each one is worth and in the end he only gets one, rather cheaply".\par \par \uc2 The straw that breaks the camel's back is "the 65% tax on imported wines and 300% on champagne," he exclaims. Erdogan has a strained relationship with Macron and the Pened\u232\'00\'E8s is a collateral victim.\par \par \uc2 So this time it's different, because almost no one is spared. Suddenly, the neighbor with the white BMW opens her heart to lament "how much the Turks are suffering", before confessing her own struggles to pay her daughter's rent in Boston. Suddenly, a thousand dollars has become a mountain, following the lira's 30% depreciation so far this year.\par \par \uc2 If this is happening in the airy diplomatic zone, truffled with secular followers of Atat\u252\'00\'FCrk and without a veil for two kilometres around, it's easy to imagine the hardship in the working-class neighbourhoods.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, the possibility of the end of the Erdogan era seems to be floating in the air. The same is true in the middle and upper class neighborhoods of Istanbul or throughout Izmir. But the portrait of Turkey is somewhat more complex.\par \par \uc2 It is true that, four years ago, Turks were shaking their heads because one euro was already worth four liras and today it is worth twelve and a half. But it is not so much an accident as a model, based on low wages and exports.\par \par \uc2 Impact Inflation and the decline in the value of the lira have undermined the confidence of all classes of people.A banker who has been based in Turkey for many years, but likes his anonymity, is reluctant to see the glass half empty. "If the lira is falling, it is because they are letting it fall. Because the priority is no longer to support the lira, but to avoid at all costs a downgrading by the rating agencies".\par \par \uc2 For several weeks now, ID cards have been required to exchange foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Erdo?an knows that a "corralito" would be his political death. The aim is therefore to present a healthy current account balance and growing reserves.\par \par \uc2 All this after Erdogan's son-in-law almost exhausted the latter in vain to support the lira a little over a year ago. Berat Albayrak was then relieved of his post as finance minister, but the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was soon replaced by an economist more sensitive to Erdogan's theses and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The export locomotive is going like a shot and \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to jump on the post-pandemic bandwagon that advocates cutting supply chains. A small China bordering the \uc2 EU\uc2 . With heavy tariffs, moreover, on Chinese products.\par \par \uc2 Not in vain, 56% of its exports go to the \uc2 EU\uc2 , with which it has an advantageous customs agreement, despite the fact that its minimum wage - in exchange, 225 euros - borders on social dumping.\par \par \uc2 However, not all companies are grateful for the rate cuts. The employers' association Tusiad, which brings together 600 large family-owned conglomerates, criticises the indifference to inflation and the depreciation of the lira.\par \par \uc2 In any case, according to Trade Minister Mehmet Mus, Turkey's GDP grew by 21.7% in the second quarter. This is largely due to the fact that exports have reached a record figure of 160 billion euros in the first ten months, helping the unemployment rate to fall slightly. Even so, one out of every nine Turks looking for work is unemployed.\par \par \uc2 But the devaluation is a double-edged sword and if the Turkish industry gains competitive advantage, on the other hand frown those who have invested in property in Istanbul, Ankara or Antalya. It is not always transparent money that seeks security, they say, rather than profit.\par \par \uc2 Hydrocarbon importer The country has seen a severe rise in transport, import and production costs.President Erdogan, a former mayor, after the previous bout of inflation, in 2018, threw a lifeline to "his people", setting up temporary markets for vegetables and pulses at highly subsidised prices. But with no election in sight, the aces are still hidden up his sleeve.\par \par \uc2 Although he abhors the data, Erdogan clings to the fact that Turkey was one of the few economies that grew last year - 1.75% - and that he expects to end 2021 with 10% growth. To this, or to bottlenecks in global supplies, Ankara attributes part of the inflation, and not only to the rate cut.\par \par \uc2 "As long as I am president, I will continue my fight against high interest rates," Erdogan told parliament on Wednesday, couching his arguments in the Koranic condemnation of usury.\par \par \uc2 His party bench applauded him, but not Finance Minister \uc2 L\u252\'00\'FCtfi Elvan\uc2 , which has been interpreted as a possible sign of an early handover.\par \par \uc2 It is true that, even now, the Turkish interest rate is double that of countries with much weaker economies, such as Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 Against the tide, with the help of its CEO, Onur Gen\u231\'00\'E7, \uc2 BBVA\uc2  has just doubled its Turkish bet, with the takeover bid for 51% of Garanti bank, which it does not yet control. On Wednesday, in the palace of a thousand and one rooms, Erdogan thanked them for their "trust", with Pedro Sanchez to his right.\par \par \uc2 Be that as it may, the dream of getting Turkey out of the middle-income trap is looking increasingly distant and it does not seem that it can be carried out by Erdogan, nor his generation.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211123ehbn001wm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
