\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2021\par 18:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 5 Oct 2021 12:58 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2   at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 -Frankfurt am Main-05 October 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Alternatifbank A.S \uc2 .'s (\uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  ) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LT FC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Stable Outlook. It has also affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b-'.\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has affirmed the support-driven LT FC IDR of Alternatif Finansal Kiralama A.S at 'B+' with a Stable Outlook, which mirrors that of the parent \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this Rating Action Commentary.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT FC IDR is driven by institutional support from its 100% shareholder, The Commercial Bank (P.Q.S.C.) (CBQ; A/Stable). Our view of the propensity and ability of CBQ to provide support reflects \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's ownership, strategic importance, integration and role within the wider group.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LT FC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's rating. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system impeding banks' ability to service their FC obligations more likely to happen than a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 The LT LC IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above the bank's LT FC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in LC.\par \par \uc2 The Stable Outlook on the bank's LT IDRs mirrors that on the sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's 'b-' VR is constrained by its weak core capitalisation, and weak, below-sector-average profitability. The VR also reflects the bank's limited franchise and the concentration of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the volatile Turkish market, adequate asset quality for its rating and manageable refinancing risks.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish operating environment remains volatile amid weak monetary policy credibility, exposure to investor sentiment (due to high FC debt), deposit dollarisation, and inflationary pressures. The lira depreciated by 11% against the US dollar in 8M21, exacerbated by the replacement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor in March 2021, and has come under further pressure following the latest 100bp cut in the policy rate.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we have revised the outlook on the operating environment score to stable, reflecting our view that the inherent uncertainty and volatility in the Turkish operating environment is captured at the 'b+' level, despite some persistent downside risk to banks' financial profiles. The outlook considers Turkey's resilient growth prospects (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 's 2021 GDP forecast: 9.2%; 2022: 3.5%), the easing in short-term external financing pressures and the Stable Outlook on the sovereign rating (BB-/Stable) - given the high interlinkages between the banking sector and sovereign balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the gradual removal (expected from 4Q21) of most regulatory forbearance measures that have supported reported asset quality and capital metrics during the pandemic, is likely to be limited for \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  has market shares of assets, loans and deposits of below 1%. Its growth has moderated in recent years (1H21: lending contracted by 4% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted); sector: up 4%), due to operating environment and capitalisation constraints. FC lending is still high (46%; sector: 37%), despite deleveraging, inflated by the lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's non-performing loans (NPLs) ratio at end-1H21 (3.0%; end-2020: 4.8%) is below sector average. Foreclosed assets comprised a further 2% of total assets (24% of common equity Tier 1 (CET1)). Stage 2 loans were a fairly high 12% of loans (50% restructured) - broadly in line with the sector - largely comprising FC, including legacy construction and real estate loans.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks are high though they have partly eased given the resilient growth outlook. Risks reflect concentrations (end-1H21: the top 25 cash loans comprised about 42% of total loans (4.3x of CET1)) and exposure to the construction (17%, including real estate development), energy (7%) and logistics (5%) sectors.\par \par \uc2 Total NPLs reserves coverage rose to 128% at end-1H21 (sector: 139%), mainly reflecting NPL collections. Specific Stage 3 coverage (61%) reflects reliance on collateral, though Stage 2 reserves coverage was adequate (12%).\par \par \uc2 Core capitalisation is very weak given concentration risks, the lira depreciation (given the inflation of FC risk-weighted assets) and weak internal capital generation. Leverage is also high (end-1H21: 6.3% tangible equity/tangible assets). Its CET1 ratio was 8.1% at end-1H21 (7.8% net of forbearance), implying less than a 100bp buffer above its 7% minimum regulatory requirement, though we expect capital support from CBQ to continue to underpin compliance with regulatory minimums. Unreserved NPLs (net of specific reserves) were equal to 12% of CET1 at end-1H21. Pre-impairment operating profit (annualised) comprised just 0.4% of average loans.\par \par \uc2 The total capital ratio is higher (end-1H21: 16.6%), reflecting additional Tier 1 capital from CBQ, which provides a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 CBQ has provided regular capital support to \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  (USD225 million since 2018), including a USD25 million cash injection (equal to 9% of end-2020 equity) in 1Q21, with a further USD25 million budgeted in 4Q21, which would provide about 60bp uplift to its CET1 ratio, and USD50 million by end-2023.\par \par \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's below-sector-average profitability in recent years reflects the loan book clean-up and low economies of scale. Margins have also tightened significantly since 3Q20 (1H21: 1.8%; sector: 3.7%) due to the high lira interest-rate environment, given that the bank's liabilities reprice quicker than its assets, and the bank's concentration in low-yielding corporate lending and expensive term deposit and FC wholesale funding. It reported an operating profit loss in 1H21, reflecting low growth and revenue generation, and the full absorption by loan impairments of pre-impairment operating profit.\par \par \uc2 We expect performance to remain weak and under pressure in the medium term. However, margins should benefit from lower lira interest rates and growth in granular deposit funding.\par \par \uc2 Customer deposits were 57% of total funding at end-1H21, of which 46% is in FC (sector: 56%). The bank's gross loans/deposits ratio of 130% underperforms the sector (105%), reflecting reliance on FC wholesale funding (43% of total funding). Excluding parent funding, FC wholesale funding is lower, but still high (28%). Refinancing risks remain high, as for the sector, but are partly mitigated by the presence of CBQ.\par \par \uc2 The bank's funding and liquidity profile is adequate, and supported by the presence of CBQ. FC wholesale funding maturities are fairly diversified, while FC liquidity is sufficient to cover short-term FC non-deposit liabilities (net of parent funding) due within a year, in addition to about 10% of FC deposits at end-1H21. Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from prolonged market closure or deposit instability, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Rating reflects our view that \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY RATING\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Alternatif Finansal Kiralama are equalised with those of the parent, reflecting \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view that it is a core and highly integrated subsidiary and the sole provider of leasing products within the group. Our view of support also considers Alternatif Finansal Kiralama's full ownership and shared branding. The Stable Outlook mirrors that of its parent bank.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 LT IDRs, SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs are sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, and could be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  assesses this risk as having increased. The ratings could also be downgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's assessment of the bank's owner's ability and propensity to provide support materially weakens.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to further erosion of its core capitalisation, given its limited buffer over the regulatory minimum, which could result from a significant increase in impaired loans leading to losses or if unreserved NPLs materially rise.\par \par \uc2 The VR is sensitive to a marked deterioration in the operating environment, given the concentration of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic market. A sharp weakening in asset quality, likely resulting from high concentration risks, or a weakening in the bank's FC liquidity position due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, could result in a downgrade if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY RATING\par \par \uc2 The ratings are sensitive to adverse changes in \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  's ratings; and \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of the ability and willingness of the parent to provide support in case of need.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 LT IDRs, SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of Turkey's LT IDRs or a revision of the Outlook to Positive would likely lead to a similar action on the bank's LT IDRs. A material improvement in Turkey's external finances or a marked increase in its net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a significant reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, could lead to an upgrade of the bank's LT FC IDR to the level of Turkey's LT FC IDR. However, given Turkey's large external vulnerabilities and weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, this would take time, in our view.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 Upside to the VR is unlikely at present unless there is a material strengthening in the bank's core capitalisation and underlying profitability.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY RATING\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2  would likely lead to a similar action on the subsidiary's ratings.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 5 Oct 2021 12:58 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 Alternatifbank\uc2   at -2-\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LT LC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Alternatif Finansal Kiralama A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\u