\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Burgan Bank Turkey at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,141 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2021\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 5 Oct 2021 15:50 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Burgan Bank Turkey at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-05 October 2021:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Burgan Bank A.S.'s (Burgan Bank Turkey) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+'. The Outlook is Stable. The bank's Viability Rating (VR) has been affirmed at 'b-'. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRs\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank Turkey's 'B+' LTFC IDR is driven by institutional support from its 99.4% shareholder, Kuwait-based Burgan Bank K.P.S.C. (Burgan Bank Kuwait; A+/Negative), in case of need. This reflects the bank's ownership and integration with the group, common branding and the record of support. Burgan Bank Turkey is Burgan Bank Kuwait's largest international subsidiary, and represented 15% of the parent's consolidated assets at end-2020.\par \par \uc2 Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which might impede banks' ability to service FC obligations, caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's rating. The bank's LT Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) IDR is one notch above its LTFC IDR, in line with \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating, given lower \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in LC. The Stable Outlook on the LT IDRs mirror that on the sovereign rating (BB-/Stable).\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The VR reflects the bank's exposure to Turkish operating environment risks, weak operating profitability and high asset quality risks, in light of which, and notwithstanding the record of capital support from its parent, we consider core capitalisation to be tight. Burgan Bank Turkey has a limited franchise (end-1H21: 0.4% of sector assets) and limited competitive advantages.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish operating environment remains highly volatile amid weak monetary policy credibility, banks' exposure to investor sentiment (due to high foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt), high deposit dollarisation, lira weakness and inflationary pressures (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecast: 17.2% inflation at end-2021). The lira depreciated 11% against the US dollar in 8M21, exacerbated by the abrupt replacement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in March 2021, and has come under further pressure following the latest 100bp cut in the policy rate.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we have revised the outlook on the 'b+' operating environment to stable, reflecting our view that the inherent uncertainty and volatility in the Turkish operating environment is captured at the 'b+' level, despite some persistent downside risk to banks' financial profiles. The stable outlook considers Turkey's resilient growth prospects (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 's 2021 GDP forecast: 9.2%; 2022: 3.5%) and the easing in short-term external financing pressures supported by a narrowing current account deficit and a recovery in gross sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, among others. It also reflects the Stable Outlook on the sovereign rating, given the high interlinkages between the banking sector and sovereign balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 The gradual removal (expected from 4Q21) of regulatory forbearance measures poses a risk to the bank's nonperforming loan (NPL) ratio, which is significantly higher net of forbearance.\par \par \uc2 The bank's asset quality metrics significantly underperform the sector (end-1H21: NPLs: 10.6% of loans; sector: 3.7%) and Stage 2 loans (19%). Exposure to FC lending (including project finance) reduced to 54% at end-1H21 (sector: 36%) from 59% at end-2020 but remains high.\par \par \uc2 In addition, exposure to single-name risk, and the risky tourism (11%), real estate (9%) and contracting (9%) sectors is material. The 25 largest cash exposures amounted to an exceptionally high 57% of gross loans (5.8x equity). Total reserves coverage of NPLs (80%; 88% including free provisions) is significantly below the sector average (139%), reflecting low specific reserves coverage (35%) due to a highly collateralised portfolio. However, reserves (24%) against Stage 2 loans are above the sector average. Provisions are calculated as per IFRS 9 methodology.\par \par \uc2 Operating profitability is weak and likely to remain under pressure, given a tighter net interest margin (1H21: 2.8%; 2020: 3.5%, including swap costs), limited economies of scale (Fitch-adjusted cost/income ratio: 62%; sector: 37%) and asset quality risks. The bank made a TRY9.8 million operating loss in 1H21 excluding gains from the sale of fixed assets of TRY96.3 million, which \uc2 Fitch\uc2  considers as non-recurring income, following a TRY300 million operating loss in 2020 (equal to 18% of common equity Tier 1 (CET1)). However, the latter included TRY195 million of free provisions (equal to under 1% of loans) set aside for unexpected losses, which were not released in 1H21 and are not expected to be fully released in the short term. The bank's after-tax profit was TRY75.9 million in 1H21, compared with an after-tax loss of TRY267 million in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The bank's CET1 ratio improved to 8.6% at end-1H21 (end 2020: 7.5%), following the conversion of subordinated debt from its parent. Core capitalisation remains very weak, given thin buffers over regulatory requirements, unreserved NPLs (equal to 22% of CET1), weak internal capital generation, concentration risks, and the lira depreciation (which inflates FC risk-weighted assets). We expect ordinary support from Burgan Bank Kuwait to support the bank's compliance with its 7.0% CET1 regulatory minimum.\par \par \uc2 The bank's total capital adequacy ratio (end-1H21: 18.7%) is supported by FC Tier 2 debt from its parent, which provides a partial hedge against lira depreciation. In 2020, the bank converted USD50 million of subordinated debt from its parent to a perpetual loan, supporting its Tier 1 ratio (end-1H21: 10.4%).\par \par \uc2 Customer deposits (end-1H21: 67.5% of total funding) are mainly in FC (63%; sector: 56%) and from retail depositors (72%). The deposit base is moderately concentrated, with the 20 largest deposits comprising 17%. A high loans/deposits ratio (end-1H21: 134%, including finance leases), reflects a high share of both loans and wholesale funding.\par \par \uc2 FC wholesale funding comprises 30% of total funding, but a lower 10% net of group funding, mitigating refinancing risks. FC liquidity is adequate, with additional comfort provided by the presence of the bank's parent, and is broadly sufficient to cover FC wholesale funding falling due within 12 months. However, it could come under pressure in the event of prolonged market closure or FC deposit outflows.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 IDRs, VR\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs are sensitive to a change in the sovereign rating and \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. Its ratings are also sensitive to our view of the parent's ability and propensity to provide support, in case of need.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to further erosion of its core capitalisation, given its limited buffer over the regulatory minimum, which could result from a significant increase in impaired loans leading to losses or if unreserved NPLs materially rise.\par \par \uc2 A marked deterioration in the operating environment given the concentration of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic market, or of the bank's FC liquidity position due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing FC debt, if not offset by shareholder support, could also result in a downgrade of the VR.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 IDRs, VR\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of Turkey's LT IDRs or revision of the Outlook to Positive would likely lead to similar action on the bank's LT IDRs. A material improvement in Turkey's external finances or a marked increase in its net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a significant reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, could lead to an upgrade of the bank's LTFC IDR to the level of Turkey's LTFC IDR. However, given Turkey's large external vulnerabilities and weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, this would take time, in our view.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be upgraded upon a marked strengthening of its core capitalisation, combined with an easing of asset quality risks and a sustained, significant improvement in underlying profitability.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: KEY RATING DRIVERS\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating has been affirmed at 'AA(tur)' with a Stable Outlook, reflecting our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 OTHER DEBT AND ISSUER RATINGS: RATING SENSITIVITIES Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to a negative rating action on the bank's LTLC IDR or a worsening in its relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTLC IDR and also in its relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 5 Oct 2021 15:50 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Burgan Bank Turkey -2-\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Burgan Bank Turkey are linked to the ratings of Burgan Bank Kuwait.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Burgan Bank A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b-\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Aurelien Mourgues,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1855\par \par \uc2 aurelien.mourgues@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Kurt Boere,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 2707\par \par \uc2 kurt.boere@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 redmond.ramsdale@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \