\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Turkiye Finans at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2021\par 14:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 28 Sep 2021 08:53 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Turkiye Finans at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-28 September 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Turkiye Finans Katilim Bankasi A.S.'s (Turkiye Finans) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Stable Outlook. The bank's Viability Rating (VR) has also been affirmed at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this rating action commentary.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 VR AND LTFC IDR\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans's 'B+' LTFC IDR is underpinned both by its standalone creditworthiness as expressed by its VR, and potential institutional support from The \uc2 Saudi National Bank\uc2  (SNB; A-/Stable).\par \par \uc2 The VR reflects the bank's reasonable company profile, notwithstanding its small overall domestic market share (1.3% of sector assets) and challenging operating conditions, underpinned by its well-established franchise in the niche, fast growing Turkish participation banking segment (end-1H21: 7.5% of banking sector assets), ordinary support from its parent, a sufficient FC liquidity buffer and limited refinancing risk, given low FC wholesale funding. However, it also considers Turkiye Finans's only moderate capitalisation, heightened credit risk profile and profitability below the sector average.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish operating environment remains highly volatile amid weak monetary policy credibility, banks' exposure to investor sentiment (due to high foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt), high deposit dollarisation, lira weakness and inflationary pressures (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecast: 17.2% inflation at end-2021). The lira depreciated 11% against the US dollar in 8M21, exacerbated by the abrupt replacement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in March 2021, and has come under further pressure following the latest 100bp cut in the policy rate.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we have revised the outlook on the 'B+' operating environment score to stable, reflecting our view that the inherent uncertainty and volatility in the Turkish operating environment is captured at the 'b+ level, despite some persistent downside risk to banks' financial profiles. The stable outlook considers Turkey's resilient growth prospects (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 's 2021 GDP forecast: 9.2%; 2022: 3.5%) and the easing in short-term external financing pressures supported by a narrowing current account deficit and a recovery in gross sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, among others. It also reflects the Stable Outlook on the sovereign rating (BB-/Stable) - given the high interlinkages between the banking sector and sovereign balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the gradual removal (expected from 4Q21) of most regulatory forbearance measures that have supported reported asset quality, capital and performance metrics during the pandemic is likely to be limited for the banking sector, including for Turkiye Finans.\par \par \uc2 The bank's financing was split 51%/35%/14% between the corporate/SME/retail segments at end-1H21. In the medium term, Turkiye Finans plans to gain market shares and increase retail financing up to a third of total financing. Rapid growth in 2020 (28% \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted; sector: 21%), outside of the Treasury-backed Credit Guarantee Fund and amid challenging market conditions, has created seasoning risks, despite it being under a tighter risk framework. The bank has more moderate medium-term financing growth targets (2021: 14%; 1H21: 2% realised).\par \par \uc2 Credit risk is heightened by a high level of risky SME exposures (end-1H21: 35% of gross financing; end-2020: 35%) and above sector average FC financing (end-1H21: 41% of gross financing; end-2020: 32%), given that not all FC borrowers are likely to be fully hedged against the lira depreciation. At end-1H21, FC financing was split between export financing (31%), working capital (and other) financing (51%) and project finance (19%).\par \par \uc2 Financing is concentrated in the fairly granular manufacturing (end-1H21: 38%) and trade (21%) sectors. Riskier exposures to construction and real estate (7%), tourism (7%), energy (6%), transport and communication (4%) are also sources of risk. At end-1H21, about a quarter of construction financing was classified in Stage 2.\par \par \uc2 The non-performing financing (NPF) ratio deteriorated only slightly to 5.7% at end-1H21 (end-2020: 5.6%), which underperforms peers but was supported by sound collections performance and lower NPF inflows. Stage 2 financing is moderate (7.7%) but largely restructured (79% of total Stage 2 financing). Total reserves coverage of NPFs (91%) is below the sector average (140%), partly reflecting the bank's focus on largely collateralised SME financing.\par \par \uc2 We expect the bank's asset quality metrics to remain broadly stable in 2021-2022, considering the limited impact of regulatory forbearance (less than 5bp on the NPF ratio), low deferred financing (2% of gross financing), the improving growth outlook and sound NPF collections.\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans's operating profitability has historically been below the sector average. It weakened in 1H21 to 0.5% of average total assets (sector: 1.3%) from 1.2% in 2020, reflecting subdued financing growth (1H21: 2%; -3% \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted), net financing margin pressure (2.9%; 2020: 4.4%) due to higher lira interest rates, higher swap costs as per the sector and still high - albeit decreasing - financing impairment charges (46% of pre-impairment operating profit; 2020: 51%).\par \par \uc2 The net financing margin is underpinned by its fairly low cost of funding (1H21: 3.0%; sector: 6.2%) reflecting its high share of low-cost demand, retail and precious metals deposits (25% of customer deposits). Potentially lower lira interest rates should support margin expansion in the short term, given the negative repricing mismatch between assets and liabilities.\par \par \uc2 The bank's capitalisation is only moderate in light of the lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC risk-weighted assets) and asset quality risks. The bank's common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio was a moderate 11.7% at end-1H21, and included 140bp uplift from regulatory forbearance. As an Islamic bank, risk weightings on Turkiye Finans's assets directly financed by profit share accounts are reduced by 50% (due to the implicit transfer of risk), resulting in an additional 170bp uplift to its CET1 ratio. This also contributes to the bank's low equity/total assets ratio (end-1H21: 6.9%; sector: 9.4%).\par \par \uc2 The bank's total capital ratio (17.2%) is supported by USD250 million of FC Tier 2 debt from \uc2 SNB\uc2  (renewed in December 2020), which acts as a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 The bank is primarily funded by fairly granular customer deposits (end-1H21: 82% of total funding) and its gross financing/deposits ratio was a sound 80% at end-1H21 (sector: 105%). However, deposits are short term, although largely behaviourally stable, and an above sector average share (a high 72%) were in FC at end-1H21 (end-2020: 73%; sector: 56%).\par \par \uc2 The bank's strategy has been to reduce external FC wholesale funding in recent years, facilitated by increased \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity in the form of FC deposits. Turkiye Finans's FC wholesale funding fell to a low 5% of total funding at end-1H21 (end-2019: 8%). Combined with potential FC liquidity support from \uc2 SNB\uc2 , this mitigates refinancing risk.\par \par \uc2 The bank's FC liquidity was sufficient to cover its more market-sensitive FC non-deposit liabilities due within a year in addition to about 23% of FC customer deposits at end-1H21. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure, primarily from deposit instability but also from prolonged market closure, if not offset by expected shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 In assessing Turkiye Finans's ratings, we consider the important differences between Islamic and conventional banks. These include a closer analysis of regulatory oversight, disclosure, accounting standards and corporate governance. Islamic banks' ratings do not express an opinion on the bank's compliance with sharia. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  will assess non-compliance with sharia if it has credit implications.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING, LONG-TERM LOCAL-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans's Support Rating of '4' reflects its strategic importance to its 67% parent, \uc2 SNB\uc2 , and its integration and role within the wider group.\par \par \uc2 Potential parental support underpins the LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rating, reflecting \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that could impede banks' ability to service their FC obligations more likely than a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans's 'BB-' LTLC IDR is driven by institutional support, and is one notch above the LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in LC. The Stable Outlook mirrors that on the sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Rating reflects our view that Turkiye Finans's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 VR, LT IDRs, SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 The VR and LTFC IDR could be downgraded if the bank's competitive position materially weakens, or if higher-than-expected growth and/or asset quality pressures erode its CET1 ratio to below 10%. The ratings are also sensitive to a marked deterioration in the operating environment and a weakening of FC liquidity due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade of the sovereign rating or revision of the Outlook to Negative would probably lead to similar action on the bank. Increased government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk would also lead to negative rating action on the bank's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 The LT LC IDR is sensitive to the ability and propensity of its parent to provide support. A reduced likelihood of institutional support would only lead to a downgrade of the bank's LTFC IDR if its VR was simultaneously downgraded.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 VR, LT IDRs, SUPPORT RATING\par \par \uc2 28 Sep 2021 08:53 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Turkiye Finans at -2-\par \par \uc2 Upside to the VR and LTFC IDR is currently limited but could come from a combination of a reduction in operating environment and government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, a strengthening of the bank's franchise and core capitalisation, and reduced dollarisation of the deposit base.\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of Turkey's LT IDRs or revision of the Outlook to Stable would likely lead to similar action on the banks' LT IDRs. A material improvement in Turkey's external finances or a marked increase in its net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a significant reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, could lead to an upgrade of the bank's LTFC IDR to the level of Turkey's LTFC IDR. However, given Turkey's large external vulnerabilities and weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, this would take time, in our view.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans's has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure due to its Islamic banking nature (in contrast to a typical Relevance Scores of '3' for comparable banks), which has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors. The bank needs to ensure compliance of its entire \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and activities with sharia principles and rules. This entails additional costs, processes, disclosures, regulations, reporting and sharia audit.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Islamic banks have an ESG Relevance Score of '3' for Exposure to Social Impacts (in contrast to a typical ESG relevance score of '2' for comparable conventional banks), which reflects that Islamic banks have certain sharia limitations embedded in their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and obligations, although this only has a minimal credit impact on Islamic banks.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Finans Katilim Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \h