\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Kuveyt Turk at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2021\par 16:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 28 Sep 2021 10:30 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Kuveyt Turk at 'B+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-28 September 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Kuveyt Turk Katilim Bankasi A.S.'s (Kuveyt Turk) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Stable Outlook. The bank's Viability Rating (VR) has been affirmed at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this rating action commentary.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING AND SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR is underpinned by both the bank's standalone creditworthiness as expressed by its VR and support from its 62% shareholder, Kuwait-based \uc2 Kuwait Finance House\uc2  (K.S.P.C.) (\uc2 KFH\uc2 ; A+/Negative), as reflected in its '4' Support Rating.\par \par \uc2 Our view of support reflects Kuveyt Turk's strategic importance to its owner, integration and role within the wider group. However, \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's rating. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations more likely than a sovereign default.\par \par \uc2 The bank's Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR is also driven by institutional support but is one notch higher at 'BB-', reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede banks' ability to service obligations in LC versus obligations in FC. The Stable Outlook on the LTLC IDR mirrors the sovereign Outlook.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk's VR reflects the bank's reasonable company profile (notwithstanding its small domestic market share; 2.6% of sector assets), underpinned by its leading market share in the niche participation banking segment (ranked first), very low funding cost base (due to a high share of current account deposits and gold deposits) and solid profitability metrics. This is balanced by exposure to the challenging Turkish operating environment, the bank's fairly high-risk appetite, as evidenced by its consistent record of above sector average financing growth, only moderate capitalisation and above-sector-average deposit dollarisation.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish operating environment remains highly volatile amid weak monetary policy credibility, banks' exposure to investor sentiment (due to high foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt), high deposit dollarisation, lira weakness and inflationary pressures (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecast: 17.2% inflation at end-2021). The lira depreciated 11% against the US dollar in 8M21, exacerbated by the abrupt replacement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in March 2021, and has come under further pressure following the latest 100bp cut in the policy rate.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we have revised the outlook on the 'B+' operating environment score to stable, reflecting our view that the inherent uncertainty and volatility in the Turkish operating environment is captured at the 'b+' level, despite persistent downside risk to banks' financial profiles. The stable outlook considers Turkey's resilient growth prospects (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 's 2021 GDP forecast: 9.2%; 2022: 3.5%) and the easing in short-term external financing pressures supported by a narrowing current account deficit and a recovery in gross sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, among others. It also reflects the Stable Outlook on the sovereign rating (BB-/Stable) - given the high interlinkages between the banking sector and the sovereign balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the gradual removal (expected from 4Q21) of most regulatory forbearance measures that have supported reported asset quality, capital and performance metrics during the pandemic is likely to be limited for the banking sector, including for Kuveyt Turk.\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk has grown consistently above the sector average in recent years. In 1H21 gross financing grew by 12% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted), mainly driven by FC loans, versus the sector average of 4%. However, growth is set to slow in 2H21, while financings are generally monthly amortising. Single-name concentration risk is moderate, with the top 25 financings equal to 14% of gross financing (1.5x CET-1) at end-1H21.\par \par \uc2 Asset quality risks remain significant, as for the sector, given exposure to FC financing (above the sector average at 46%; sector: 37%), given the impact of the lira depreciation on borrowers' ability to service their FC debt as not all exposures will be fully hedged, SMEs (34% of total financing), which are highly sensitive to the economic climate, and the high Turkish lira interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Kuveyt Turk has exposure to the risky construction (1H21: around 10% of total financings, bank calculation) and to a lesser extent energy sectors, which account for a high share of its Stage 2 book. These sectors have come under pressure from operating environment volatility, market illiquidity (real estate), the lira depreciation (as exposures are frequently in FC while revenues are in lira) and weak energy prices.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the bank's shares of non-performing financing (NPF) and Stage 2 financings at end-1H21 outperform the sector at 3.3% and 8.5% of gross financing, respectively, although its asset quality metrics are flattered by its above-average growth. Total NPF reserves coverage was a high 211%, boosted by fairly high reserves (33%) against Stage 2 exposures, resulting from the individual assessment of exposures due to single-name risk in its book.\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk reported an above-sector-average operating profit/average total asset ratio in 1H21 (1.9%, versus 1.3% for the sector). The bank is heavily reliant on net financing income, but its net financing margin is wide, underpinned by material low-cost demand and gold deposits (a high 64% of deposits), which support its low cost of funding. The net financing margin will also benefit from the latest lira rate cut, given its liabilities reprice more quickly than its assets. Profitability in 1H21 was further supported by high financing growth and a below-sector-average cost of risk (1H21: 1.4%, versus 2.1% for the sector).\par \par \uc2 Core capitalisation is only moderate, given Kuveyt Turk's risk profile and heightened operating environment risks. Leverage is also high, as reflected in a tangible common equity/tangible assets ratio of just 4.2% at end-1H21. Its common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio was 11.4% (including 237bp uplift from regulatory forbearance). As an Islamic bank, it benefits from a 50% reduction in risk weighting on assets financed by profit share accounts (193bp uplift to its CET1 ratio). The bank's end-1H21 total capital ratio was higher at 15% (including forbearance). It also issued a USD350 million Tier 2 sukuk (September 2021), which will give a further estimated 390bp uplift to the total capital ratio, while also providing a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is supported by full reserves coverage of NPFs. Pre-impairment operating profit provides an additional buffer to absorb losses through the income statement (1H21: equal to 4.4% of average financing; annualised basis). Our assessment of the bank's capitalisation also reflects ordinary support from \uc2 KFH\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Funding is largely sourced from customer deposits (end-1H21: a high 88% of total funding), a significant share of which is in FC, partly reflecting increased banking sector dollarisation in 2020. The share of FC wholesale funding is fairly limited (8% of total funding), and below sector average. Refinancing risks are mitigated by potential FC liquidity support from the parent.\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity is generally adequate. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  calculates that at end-1H21 FC liquidity (comprising mainly cash and interbank placements, FC reserves held under the reserve option mechanism and unpledged government Sukuk) sufficiently covered the bank's short-term FC non-deposit liabilities due within a year in addition to about 24% of FC customer deposits at end-1H21. FC liquidity is also supported by the largely monthly amortising nature of the bank's financing book. Nevertheless, it could come under pressure in the event of prolonged market closure or deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 In assessing Kuveyt Turk 's ratings, we consider the important differences between Islamic and conventional banks. These include a closer analysis of regulatory oversight, disclosure, accounting standards and corporate governance. Islamic banks' ratings do not express an opinion on the bank's compliance with sharia. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  will assess non-compliance with sharia if it has credit implications.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating has been affirmed with a Stable Outlook reflecting our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers has not changed.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk's subordinated notes' rating is one notch below its 'B+' LTFC IDR. We include zero notches for incremental non-performance risk, reflecting the fact that the terms of the certificates do not provide for loss absorption on a 'going concern' basis, and only one notch for loss severity, reflecting our view that institutional support (as reflected in the bank's LTFC IDR) helps mitigate losses and incorporating the fact that the bank's LTFC IDR is already capped at 'B+', one notch below the sovereign, reflecting our view that government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which would impede the bank's ability to service its FC obligations, is more likely than a sovereign default. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  considers recoveries on the notes in the event of default to be below average, as evidenced by a Recovery Rating (RR) of 'RR5'.\par \par \uc2 Rating Sensitivities Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING AND SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk's LTFC IDR could be downgraded or its Outlook revised to Negative if our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector has increased.\par \par \uc2 Negative action on the sovereign rating, particularly if triggered by further weakening in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances that leads to increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely be mirrored on the bank's Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 28 Sep 2021 10:30 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Kuveyt Turk at 'B+'; -2-\par \par \uc2 The bank's ratings are also sensitive to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of \uc2 KFH\uc2 's ability and propensity to provide support, in case of need. A reduced likelihood of institutional support would only lead to a downgrade of Kuveyt Turk's ratings if its VR is simultaneously downgraded.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The VR is sensitive to a marked deterioration in the operating environment, given the concentration of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic market.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to a sharp weakening in capitalisation, which could result from a sharp rise in NPFs or aggressive growth, or a weakening of its FC liquidity position due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, if not offset by shareholder support.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTLC IDR and also in its relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk's subordinated debt rating is sensitive to changes in the bank's LTFC IDR anchor rating but also to a change in notching from the anchor rating due to a revision in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's assessment of the probability of the notes' non-performance risk or in our view of loss severity.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING AND SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of Turkey's LT IDRs or revision of the Outlook to Positive would likely lead to similar action on the bank's LT IDR. A material improvement in Turkey's external finances or a marked increase in its net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, resulting in a significant reduction in our view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector, could lead to an upgrade of the bank's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR could come from a reduction in operating environment risks, a strengthening of the bank's franchise and core capitalisation, an improvement in investor sentiment and reduced dollarisation of the deposit base.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The bank's LTFC IDR is underpinned by both the bank's VR, or standalone creditworthiness, and shareholder support (\uc2 Kuwait Finance House\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 As an Islamic bank, Kuveyt Turk needs to ensure compliance of its entire \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and activities with sharia principles and rules. This entails additional costs, processes, disclosures, regulations, reporting and sharia audit. This results in a Governance Structure relevance score of '4' for the bank (in contrast to a typical ESG relevance score of '3' for comparable conventional banks), which has a negative impact on its credit profile in combination with other factors.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Kuveyt Turk has an exposure to social impacts relevance score of '3' (in contrast to a typical ESG relevance score of '2' for comparable conventional banks), which reflects that Islamic banks have certain sharia limitations embedded in their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and obligations, although this only has a minimal credit impact on the entities.\par \par \uc2 Except for the matter discussed above, the highest level of ESG credit relevance, if present, is a score of 3 - ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity.\par \par \uc2 KT Kira Sertifikalari Varlik Kiralama A.S\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 Kuveyt Turk Katilim Bankasi A.S; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 KT21 T2 Company Limited\par \par \uc2 ----subordinated; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Aurelien Mourgues,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1855\par \par \uc2 aurelien.mourgues@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Cem Bilgin,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1230\par \par \uc2 cem.bilgin@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Sen