\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2021\par 18:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 18 May 2021 12:05 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-18 May 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S.'s (TSKB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 LTFC IDR, VR, AND SENIOR DEBT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 TSKB's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt ratings are driven by its VR. The affirmation of the VR despite heightened operating environment pressures, reflects the bank's niche policy role and development bank focus and record of relatively consistent performance. However, the VR also reflects the concentration of TSKB's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the high-risk Turkish operating environment, which heightens pressure on its credit profile, and only adequate capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) gross reserves were USD87.9 billion and net international reserves USD12.0 billion as of end-April 2021. Adjusting for FC swap transactions, net FC reserves were negative. This implies a marked reduction in the ability of the authorities to provide FC liquidity support to the bank in case of need. However, Turkey's net FC reserves position should be considered in light of its own limited short-term external debt requirements, while potential access to external funding markets, dependent on market conditions, could underpin the sovereign's ability to raise FC liquidity in case of need.\par \par \uc2 The recent replacement of the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 's governor and ensuing damage to monetary policy credibility and investor sentiment - as evidenced by renewed market volatility and lira depreciation - increases funding and liquidity risks. Furthermore, ongoing uncertainty over the pandemic, particularly given the latest resurgence and lockdown restrictions, create downside risks both to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's GDP forecast of 6.7% in 2021 and banks' asset quality. Consequently, operating environment pressure, exacerbated by the economic downturn and financial market volatility, is the main driver of the Negative Outlook on TSKB's LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 The bank has posted below-sector average loan growth since end-2017, reflecting its more conservative risk appetite as the operating environment has deteriorated, but also low demand and capital constraints. It plans muted loan growth of 3%-5%, mainly in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Despite the bank's reasonable risk framework, asset quality risks are significant for TSKB, given operating environment pressures, single-name risk (notably in project finance; around 60% of loans at end-2020), material FC lending (90% of loans), as not all borrowers are fully hedged against the lira depreciation, and exposure to pressured sectors. Energy lending (a high 45%) has come under pressure from the lira depreciation and weak energy prices, but as exposures largely relate to renewable projects, they are mainly covered by a government-guaranteed feed-in tariff set in FC, mitigating the credit risk.\par \par \uc2 The bank's impaired loans ratio has risen only slightly (end-1Q21: 3.8%; end-2019: 3.5%). However, its Stage 2 loans ratio is fairly high (11.3%; largely restructured loans). In addition, the long-term, slowly amortising nature of FC lending means asset quality problems will feed through fairly slowly.\par \par \uc2 TSKB's profitability metrics generally remain reasonable and the bank outperformed the sector in terms of its operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio in 1Q21 (TSKB: 2.3% vs sector: 1.8%). Performance has been underpinned by its reasonable net interest margin - reflecting access to concessional Treasury guaranteed funding and exposure predominantly to FC interest rates - reasonable cost control and manageable but increased loan impairment charges. The bank front-loaded pandemic-related provisions in 2020, resulting in an increase in the cost of risk (2020: 2.4%, 2019: 1.3%).\par \par \uc2 Profitability is likely to remain muted in 2021 given low growth and net interest margin pressure, and could deteriorate significantly in case of a marked weakening in asset quality, particularly given concentration risks.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation has come under pressure from the lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWA). TSKB's common equity Tier 1 ratio fell to 9.7% (excluding forbearance) at end-1Q21, representing only a moderate buffer over the regulatory minimum. Its total capital ratio is stronger at 14.9% (excluding forbearance), supported by FC-denominated Tier 2 capital. Potential asset quality deterioration also represents a risk to capital, given high restructured and Stage 2 loans (average reserves coverage of 14%), as well as high FC lending and single-name risks. Pre-impairment operating profit (1Q21: equal to about 5.7% of average loans, annualised) provides a reasonable buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 TSKB is fully wholesale funded, primarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and by development financial institutions (DFIs), and a significant portion is Treasury guaranteed. Funding maturities are reasonably diversified with well-spaced out repayment schedules. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity is only adequate and TSKB would rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan repayments and undrawn \uc2 DFI\uc2  funding to cover short-term maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  wholesale borrowings up to one year. FC liquid assets typically comprise mainly cash and placements with foreign and domestic banks and deliverable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LTLC IDR, NATIONAL RATING, SUPPORT RATING FLOOR (SRF) AND SUPPORT RATING (SR)\par \par \uc2 TSKB's 'B' SRF is equalised with the SRFs of the state-owned systemically important commercial banks. This reflects our view that TSKB is of strategic importance to the Turkish authorities, given its policy role, expertise in development lending (particularly renewable energy) and access to long-term funding from DFIs (largely under a Turkish Treasury guarantee). At end-1Q21, Treasury-guaranteed funding made up 54% of TSKB's total funding.\par \par \uc2 The bank's Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR is equalised with Turkey's sovereign LTLC IDR at 'BB-' on the basis of support, reflecting the sovereign's greater ability to provide support in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). The Stable Outlook on the rating also mirrors that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TSKB's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF TSKB\par \par \uc2 TSKB's 'B-' subordinated notes rating is notched twice from its VR anchor rating reflecting our expectation of poor recoveries in case of default.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 VR, LTFC IDR AND SENIOR DEBT RATINGS\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 - The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to a change in TSKB's VR, which could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, if the fallout from the latest pandemic resurgence is more severe than expected, or if economic recovery is significantly weaker than expected.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a greater than expected deterioration in underlying asset quality, including due to further increases in restructured loans, could put pressure on the VR. A prolonged funding market closure that severely erodes TSKB's FC liquidity buffer could also lead to a VR downgrade.\par \par \uc2 - Negative action on the sovereign rating, particularly if triggered by further weakening in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, that leads to increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely be mirrored on the bank's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 - Upside for the VR is constrained by Turkish operating environment risks. However, a reduction in operating environment risks, including lower market or exchange rate volatility and an improvement in investor sentiment, would reduce downside risks to the VR and could support a revision of the Outlook to Stable.\par \par \uc2 - A record of resilient financial metrics, notwithstanding heightened operating environment risks, could also support a revision of the Outlook to Stable.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORT RATING, SRF AND LTLC IDR\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 - TSKB's SR could be downgraded and the SRF revised down if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support for the bank in FC from the Turkish authorities or if a weakening in its policy role leads to a lower sovereign propensity to provide support, in our view.\par \par \uc2 - TSKB's LTLC IDR could be downgraded if Turkey's LTLC IDR is downgraded, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes the sovereign's propensity to provide support has reduced or the likelihood of bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk increases.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 - A significant improvement in Turkey's external finances, including a material increase in net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, could lead to an upward revision TSKB's SRF and upgrade of the SR. However, only a multi-notch upward revision of the SRF would lead to an upgrade of TSKB's LTFC IDR, given the bank's VR is one notch above the SRF.\par \par \uc2 - An upgrade of the sovereign's LTLC IDR or a revision of the sovereign's Outlook would likely lead to similar action on the bank.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in TSKB's LTLC IDR and creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATINGS OF TSKB\par \par \uc2 TSKB's subordinated debt rating is primarily sensitive to a change in its VR anchor rating.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 18 May 2021 12:05 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms TSKB at 'B+'; -2-\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's financial spreadsheets for TSKB that has impacted \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's core and complementary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The bank has ratings linked to the Turkish sovereign, given its ratings are sensitive to our assessment of sovereign support and country risks.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Sinai Kalkinma Bankasi A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Stable\pa