\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,101 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2021\par 16:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 May 2021 10:25 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-04 May 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Yapi ve Kredi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .'s (YKB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 LTFC IDR, VR, SENIOR DEBT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 YKB's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt ratings are driven by its VR. The affirmation of the VR, despite heightened operating environment pressures, reflects the bank's generally reasonable financial metrics, underpinned by its solid domestic franchise (end-2020: 8% of sector assets), and significant capital and liquidity buffers. However, the VR also reflects the bank's concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish operating environment.\par \par \uc2 The recent replacement of the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) governor and ensuing damage to monetary policy credibility and investor sentiment - as evidenced by renewed market volatility and lira depreciation - increases funding and liquidity risks, as does high increased deposit dollarisation. Furthermore, ongoing uncertainty over the pandemic, particularly given the latest resurgence and lockdown restrictions, create downside risks both to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's GDP forecast of 6.7% in 2021 and banks' asset quality. Operating environment pressure, and the implications for the bank's financial metrics, drives the Negative Outlook on YKB's LTFC IDR and is a high importance factor for the VR.\par \par \uc2 YKB's loan growth (end-2020: 21% nominal; 8% \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted) has slowed in recent years and was below the sector and peer averages in 2020, although it was still high. Growth was largely outside of the framework of the Treasury guaranteed Credit Guarantee Fund while the bank deleveraged FC lending (down 4% in US dollar terms). YKB targets high-teens loan growth in 2021, driven by small-ticket local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lending.\par \par \uc2 Asset quality risks are significant for YKB, given exposure to unsecured retail borrowers (19%), which are sensitive to rising unemployment, and the pressured energy (14%), construction (10%) and tourism (3%) sectors. Stage 2 loans are also fairly high and lumpy (15% of loans, of which about half are restructured).\par \par \uc2 A high share of FC lending (37% at end-2020; consolidated basis) also heightens risks, given that not all borrowers will be fully hedged against lira depreciation. These risks are somewhat mitigated by the fact that exposures are generally to large, diversified corporates with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or project finance loans carrying a government feed-in tariff (energy loans) or revenue or debt assumption guarantee.\par \par \uc2 YKB's reported impaired loans (NPL) ratio improved to 6.2% at end-2020, from 7.4% at end-2019, despite operating environment pressures, primarily reflecting forbearance on loan classification (40bp uplift; \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimate), collections, write-offs and loan growth. We expect NPLs to rise moderately in 2021, given waning government stimulus and regulatory forbearance (due to expire by end-1H21) and maturing loan deferrals (end-2020: equal to a below-peer-average 3% of loans; largely now repaying), although loan restructurings could delay the recognition of problematic loans.\par \par \uc2 Total reserve coverage of NPLs increased to 126% at end-2020 (end-2019: 84%) as the bank frontloaded pandemic-related provisions and increased Stage 2 reserve coverage (17.4%). YKB has the highest total provisioning/average gross loans ratio of the peer group (7.8% vs, 5.5% for peers).\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects YKB's performance to remain reasonable despite short-term margin pressure - following the lira interest rate hikes and given the bank's negative repricing gap - and high loan impairment charges (LICs; 2020: equal to 57% of pre-impairment operating profit). Its operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWAs) ratio improved to 1.9% in 2020 despite the pandemic, reflecting better margins supported by low funding costs in 2020, growth and good cost control.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is supported by solid pre-impairment operating profit (2020: equal to 5.4% of gross loans) and moderate reserves coverage. YKB's Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1)/RWAs ratio of 12.8% at end-2020 (or 12.4% net of forbearance) represents a significant buffer above the regulatory minimum to absorb unexpected losses and lira depreciation. In addition, the bank plans to adopt the Internal Ratings Based approach from June 2021. This will provide an uplift of about 80bp to the CET1 ratio (Tier-1 about 90bp and total CAR about 60bp). Its total capital ratio was higher at 17.2% (16.7%) due to FC subordinated Tier 2 debt and additional Tier-1, which provide a partial hedge against lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 YKB has a large, granular and stable (but short-term) deposit base (end-2020: 7% market share; 65% of total funding), and high demand deposits (36%) support its below sector average cost of funding. However, its loans/deposits ratio underperforms peers (122%). FC deposits are significant (60%) and above peer and sector averages.\par \par \uc2 High FC wholesale funding (end-2020: 24% of total consolidated funding) increases refinancing risks, given exposure to investor sentiment amid volatile market conditions. YKB has retained reasonable access to international funding markets, rolling over a high share of FC debt.\par \par \uc2 At end-2020, YKB's available FC liquidity - much of which will likely be placed with the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  - was sufficient to cover maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months (USD3.7 billion on a bank-only basis; excluding interbank deposits). Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of YKB's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 LTLC IDR, SUPPORT RATING FLOOR (SRF) AND SUPPORT RATING (SR)\par \par \uc2 YKB's 'B-' SRF is at the level of the domestic systemically important bank SRF for Turkish banks, reflecting it private ownership and our view that support from the Turkish authorities in FC cannot be relied upon given the sovereign's weak net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 YKB's 'B+' LT Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) IDR is driven by potential state support, reflecting the sovereign's greater ability to provide support in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Stable Outlook mirrors that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 YKB's 'B-' subordinated notes rating is notched twice from the VR, reflecting our expectation of poor recoveries in case of default.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade: -\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is constrained by Turkish operating environment risks. However, a reduction in operating environment risks, including lower market- or exchange rate- volatility and an improvement in investor sentiment, would reduce downside risks to the VR and could support a revision of the Outlook to Stable.\par \par \uc2 -A record of resilient financial metrics, notwithstanding heightened operating environment risks, could also support a revision of the Outlook to Stable.\par \par \uc2 -A significant improvement in Turkey's external finances, including materially higher net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, could lead to an upward revision of YKB's SRF and upgrade of the SR, although this is unlikely in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 -The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to a change in YKB's VR, which could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, if the fallout from the latest pandemic resurgence is more severe than expected, or if economic recovery is significantly weaker than expected.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a greater than expected deterioration of YKB's underlying asset quality could put pressure on the VR, including due to a rise in restructured loans. A prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability that severely eroded the bank's FC liquidity buffer could also lead to a VR downgrade.\par \par \uc2 -Negative action on the sovereign rating, particularly if triggered by further weakening in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances, that leads to increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely be mirrored on the bank's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 -The SR and SRF could be downgraded and revised down, respectively, if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support for the bank in FC from the Turkish authorities.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in YKB's VR anchor rating.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in YKB's LTLC IDR and its creditworthiness relative to other Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 YKB has ratings linked to the Turkish sovereign rating, given the ratings either rely on or are sensitive to our assessment of sovereign support or country risks.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 4 May 2021 10:25 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms YKB at 'B+'; Outlook -2-\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Yapi ve Kredi Bankasi A.S \uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; A+(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 5\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating Floor; Affirmed; B-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----subordinated; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B-\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Ahmet Kilinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1272\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1027\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Stat