\par\par \b \uc2 Press Release: Fitch Affirms Vakifbank at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2021\par 17:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-04 May 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  has affirmed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi T.A.O \f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vakifbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  ) Long-Term Foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'. A full list of rating actions is below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's LTFC IDR and senior debt ratings are driven by the bank's standalone credit profile as expressed in the VR. The affirmation of its ratings, despite operating-environment pressures, reflects its solid franchise as Turkey's second largest bank (end-2020: 11% of sector assets) and a reasonable funding and liquidity profile. However, the VR also reflects the bank's concentration in the volatile and challenging Turkish operating environment.\par \par \uc2 The recent replacement of the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) governor and ensuing damage to monetary- policy credibility and investor sentiment - as evidenced by renewed market volatility and lira depreciation - increases funding and liquidity risks, as has high, increased, deposit dollarisation. Furthermore, uncertainty over the pandemic, particularly given the latest infection resurgence and lockdown restrictions, create downside risks to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's GDP forecast of 6.7% in 2021 and banks' asset quality.\par \par \uc2 The VR also reflects \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's high risk appetite and rapid growth in recent years, which is indicative of the bank's role in supporting Turkey's economic policy. Both factors are of high importance for the VR and the main drivers of the Negative Outlook on the LTFC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Nominal loan growth (bank-only) was a rapid 54% (or 36% \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 )-adjusted) in 2020 (sector: 35%), despite operating-environment weakness, reflecting \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's state bank mandate to support economic growth. Lending largely comprised low-cost, fixed-rate local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans in the form of Treasury-backed Credit Guarantee Fund (CGF) loans, somewhat mitigating credit risks, and, to a lesser extent, in housing loans (rose 72%), Its FC lending (mainly to exporters) also rose (up 8% in US dollars; sector: 4% contraction).\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's reported impaired loans (NPLs) ratio improved to 4% at end-2020 (end-2019: 5.9%), despite operating-environment pressures, reflecting forbearance on loan classification (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates 40bp uplift), loan growth and collections. We expect NPLs to rise moderately in 2021 given waning government stimulus and forbearance (expire at end-1H21), and maturing loan deferrals (end-2020: 5% (solo basis); largely now repaying). However, loan restructurings could delay the migration of problem loans to Stage 3.\par \par \uc2 Total reserve coverage of impaired loans increased to 123.5% at end-2020 (end-2019: 90.7%) and compared well with peers' as \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  front-loaded provisions due to the pandemic and increased reserve coverage of Stage 2 loans (12.6%; below peers').\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks are high due to loan seasoning, exposure to SMEs (end-2020: 25% of loans) and unsecured retail borrowers (18%) - which are sensitive to the growth environment and unemployment, respectively - and the struggling transportation (11%), construction (9%), energy (6%) and real-estate (4%) sectors. Stage 2 loans were fairly high at 9.5% of gross loans, of which a third have been restructured.\par \par \uc2 FC lending (29%) - while lower than at private bank peers - heightens risks given that not all borrowers are fully hedged against lira depreciation. This is partly mitigated by exposures being generally to large, diversified corporates with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues, or project-finance loans under government feed-in tariff (energy loans) with revenue- or debt-assumption guarantees.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's common equity Tier-1 (CET1)/risk-weighted assets (RWAs) rose to 11.3% at end-2020 (end-2019: 10.3%) despite growth, reflecting low risk-weights on CGF loans (40bp uplift), a TRY7 billion capital increase from the authorities and zero dividends. Pre-impairment operating profit (2020: equal to 3.6% of average loans) provides an additional moderate buffer to absorb losses through the income statement. Nevertheless, capitalisation represents only a moderate buffer above regulatory minima to absorb unexpected losses and potential further lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's profitability metrics are in line with peer group averages. They remain sensitive to margin pressure following lira-rate hikes, due to the bank's short-term negative repricing gap and high share of fixed-rate loans; loan impairment charges (LICs; 2020: at 47% of pre-impairment operating profit); and slower loan growth. Its operating profit/RWAs improved to 1.8% in 2020 (sector: 1.9%) despite the pandemic's economic fallout, high LICs and TRY220 million of free provisions.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has a large, stable and granular deposit base (end-2020: 62% of total funding; 11% market share), and an above peer-average share of lira deposits, reflecting its solid franchise and state-related deposits (20%), but FC deposits are still high (48%). The bank's above-peer-average cost of funding reflects only moderate demand deposits (20%) and a higher share of more expensive lira.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's loans/deposits (end-2020: 114%) underperforms peers', reflecting significant wholesale funding (38% of total funding; over half in FC). This heightens refinancing risks given exposure to investor sentiment amid market volatility but is partly mitigated by \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's healthy funding diversification by instrument and tenor.\par \par \uc2 At end-2020 available FC liquidity (a large portion of which comprises FC swaps with the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) was sufficient to cover maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months (USD4.7 billion, bank-only basis; excluding interbank deposits). Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 LONG-TERM LOCAL-CURRECNY (LTLC) IDR, NATIONAL RATING, SUPPORT RATING FLOOR (SRF) AND SUPPORT RATING (SR)\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's 'B' SRF is two notches below Turkey's LTFC IDR, despite the bank's state ownership, systemic importance, role in supporting government policy, state-related funding and the record of capital support from the government. This reflects our view that support from the authorities in FC is limited given the sovereign's weak net FC reserves.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's 'BB-' LTLC IDR is equalised with the sovereign rating, reflecting Turkey's greater ability to provide support in LC. The Stable Outlook mirrors that on the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of the National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's creditworthiness in LC relative to other Turkish issuers' is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 The subordinated notes rating of 'B-' is notched down twice from \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's VR, reflecting our expectation of poor recoveries in case of default.\par \par \uc2 ESG Factor: \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to a typical Relevance Score of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over its board's strategies and effectiveness in the challenging Turkish operating environment.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is constrained by Turkish operating-environment risks. A reduction in operating- environment risks, including lower market- or exchange-rate volatility and an improvement in investor sentiment, would reduce downside risks to the VR and could support a revision of the Outlook to Stable. A reduction in \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's risk appetite would also reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 - A record of improved, resilient financial metrics notwithstanding heightened operating-environment risks, could also support a revision of the Outlook to Stable.\par \par \uc2 -A significant improvement in Turkey's external finances and net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, could lead to upward revision of \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's SRF and upgrade of the SR, although this is unlikely in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are primarily sensitive to a change in \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's VR, which could be downgraded due to further marked deterioration in the operating environment, if the fallout from the latest pandemic resurgence is more severe than expected, or if economic recovery is significantly weaker than expected.\par \par \uc2 - The VR could be downgraded in case of further increases in risk appetite, as reflected in rapid, above-sector-average loan growth despite operating-environment weakness, or strategic decisions that increase pressure on underlying asset quality, profitability and capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 - Greater-than-expected deterioration in asset quality, including due to a rise in restructured loans, could put pressure on the VR, as could a prolonged funding market closure or deposit instability that severely erodes \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's FC liquidity buffer.\par \par \uc2 -A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances that leads to an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely be mirrored on \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's SR and SRF could be downgraded and revised lower, respectively, if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  concludes further stress in Turkey's external finances materially reduces the reliability of support for the bank in FC from the Turkish authorities.\par \par \uc2 SUBORDINATED DEBT\par \par \uc2 The subordinated debt rating is sensitive to a change in \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  's VR.\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The National Rating is sensitive to changes in the LTLC IDR and in the bank's relative creditworthiness to other Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 4 May 2021 11:51 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2   at 'B+'; -2-\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The bank's ratings are linked to the Turkish sovereign's.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Vakifbank\uc2  has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure and Management Strategy (in contrast to a typical Relevance Score of '3' for comparable banks), due to potential government influence over its board's strategies and effectiveness in the challenging Turkish operating environment. This has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the ratings in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Turkiye Vakiflar Bankasi T.A.O \uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; 