\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-London Turkish lira overnight swap rate spikes, Refinitiv data show\b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, detail, context)\par \par \uc2 By Tom Arnold, Karin Strohecker and Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 23 (Reuters) - Interest rates on overnight swaps for Turkey's lira briefly spiked to 1,400% on Tuesday, their highest in at least a decade, Refinitiv data showed, days after an upheaval in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  abruptly fired his hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor at the weekend and installed a critic of the country's tight monetary stance. That pushed Turkish markets into turmoil on Monday with the lira plunging as much as 15%.\par \par \uc2 The move in Turkish lira overnight swap rates evoked echoes of 2019, when rates rocketed to more than 1,000% as Turkish banks withheld liquidity from London markets when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under pressure before local elections.\par \par \uc2 By early afternoon trading time in London, the level had eased to 500%.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign investors are trying to liquidate long TRY (lira) position in a rush this week," said Onur Ilgen, head of treasury at MUFG Bank Turkey.\par \par \uc2 "This resulted in a significant liquidity squeeze in the offshore lira swap market, driven from tenor mismatch for outstanding TRY placements as foreigners lending longer-term TRY via forward market have an immediate need for TRY cash to unwind long spot position on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the cash markets by selling dollars and buying lira, while a squeeze in forward markets was driven by state-run banks, said a London-based trader.\par \par \uc2 There was no evidence that state banks had been instructed by the BDDK bank regulator to cut the limit for Turkish banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap, spot and forward transactions to foreign entities as they had been in 2019.\par \par \uc2 An economic adviser for Erdogan said earlier in the day that Turks sold $5.1 billion on Monday to profit from high exchange rates. Corporations sold $2.5 billion. (Editing by Andrew Cawthorne, Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-23T14:22:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210323eh3n01ta1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
