\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms ING Bank A.S at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2021\par 12:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 Feb 2021 06:16 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms ING Bank A.S at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-04 February 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed ING Bank A.S.'s (INGBT) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+'. The Outlook is Negative. At the same time, the agency has affirmed the bank's Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this commentary.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 The LTFC IDR of INGBT is underpinned by both potential institutional support from its 100% owner, \uc2 ING Bank N.V\uc2  . (ING; AA-/Negative) and the bank's standalone creditworthiness (as defined by its VR). INGBT's '4' Support Rating reflects the bank's strategic importance to, integration and role within the wider ING group, and small size relative to ING's ability to provide support, if needed.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/Negative) and is also the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the bank. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would result in some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The sovereign's significantly weaker net FC reserves, together with weak monetary-policy credibility, have exacerbated external financing risks and this is a key driver of the Negative Outlook on the sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 INGBT's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above the LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). However, the LTLC IDR is also on Negative Outlook, mirroring the sovereign Outlook, reflecting our view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in LC is not lower than the probability of a sovereign default in LC obligations.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of INGBT's National Rating reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers' remains unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of INGBT's VR at 'b+', a level that is in line with that of the largest, systemically important banks in the sector despite the bank's only small size, reflects the bank's record of solid performance, adequate loss absorption buffers and a below-peer-average risk appetite. However, the VR also consider the concentration of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the high-risk Turkish operating environment, limited franchise, moderate market shares and limited competitive advantages.\par \par \uc2 We see downside risks to INGBT's VR given uncertainty over the impact of the pandemic and potential further market volatility. A deeper and more extended economic contraction than expected would exacerbate operating-environment risks, heightening pressure on the bank's asset quality, capitalisation and performance metrics. In addition, regulatory forbearance measures, which are currently providing uplift to banks' reported asset quality and capital metrics, are due to expire at end-June 2021.\par \par \uc2 INGBT has pursued a conservative growth strategy by deleveraging its loan book from end-2018 to end-2020, largely reflecting a reduction in FC loans. The loan book contracted 3% in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) adjusted terms in 9M20, despite the asset ratio requirement (AR), designed to stimulate sector loan growth, coming into force in May 2020. The bank aims to slightly increase loan growth in 2021, which will be focused mainly on LC, while it will avoid high-risk sectors such as real-estate, energy and infrastructure projects.\par \par \uc2 High FC lending (end-9M20: 42% of gross loans) heightens credit risks given that not all borrowers will be fully hedged against lira depreciation. INGBT adopts a cautious approach to new FC lending and has appetite mainly for export and manufacturing loans. It does not, as a matter of internal policy, undertake real-estate development projects and has a conservative policy towards energy projects.\par \par \uc2 The bank's impaired loans improved to 5.7% of total loans in 9M20 (2019: 7%), due primarily to collections and, to a lesser extent, regulatory forbearance. Stage 2 loans comprised 12.5% of gross loans at end-9M20, having declined significantly in recent years (2019: 10%; 2018: 24%). This decline should partly be considered in light of the high starting point resulting from the bank's conservative classification approach (mostly in line with \uc2 ING Group\uc2  standards). INGBT has subsequently reviewed its significant increase in credit risk (SICR) thresholds for large corporates by adjusting its portfolio accordingly.\par \par \uc2 Impaired loans were only 63% covered by specific reserves at end-9M20 reflecting reliance on collateral, which could be challenging to realise in the challenging Turkish market. Including Stage 1 and Stage 2 reserves, total loan loss allowance coverage increased to 80%, which was still significantly below the sector average (9M20: 127%).\par \par \uc2 We believe the improving growth outlook in Turkey (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 3.5% real GDP growth in 2021), recent appreciation of the lira and greater market stabilisation should be moderately supportive of Turkish banks' asset quality and collection capacities. Nevertheless, risks remain to the downside given uncertainty over the pandemic, operating-environment volatility and a higher lira interest-rate environment.\par \par \uc2 The bank's profitability metrics deteriorated in 9M20 due to a slight decline in margins, loan-book contraction, but also to lower fees (due largely to regulatory restrictions) and weaker trading income. Cost growth remained muted in 2019 and 9M20, reflecting sound cost control.\par \par \uc2 Loan impairment charges (LICs) have remained manageable (9M20: equal to 25% of pre-impairment profit; 2019: 27%) but the bank reports weaker reserve coverage than peers. The bank's operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWAs) declined to a still reasonable 2.6% at end-9M20 (sector: 2%) from 4% at end-2019.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects further contraction in net interest margin (NIM) in 2021, given higher lira interest rates and that liabilities reprice more quickly than assets, as for the sector, which may only be partially offset by higher loan growth. We believe that the cost of risk (LICs/ gross loans) should remain manageable in 2021, given the improving macro backdrop and the front-loading of provisions.\par \par \uc2 Capitalisation is reasonable. INGBT's CET1 ratio was above peer average at 17% (excluding uplift from forbearance) at end-9M20. Its total capital ratio was a higher 23.3%, significantly above the 12% recommended regulatory minimum, supported by USD524 million of FC subordinated debt from ING, which provides a partial hedge against FC RWAs. However, as for the rest of the sector, capitalisation remains sensitive to lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs) and asset-quality deterioration.\par \par \uc2 Unreserved non-performing loans (NPLs) amounted to 6% of CET1 capital at end-9M20, although pre-impairment operating profit (amounting to 4.3% of average loans at end-9M20, annualised) provides a significant buffer to absorb losses through the income statement before hitting capital.\par \par \uc2 The bank is largely deposit-funded (end-9M20: 68% of total funding) and its deposit base is fairly granular, reflecting a high proportion of retail deposits. Customer deposits contracted 9% in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted terms in 9M20. Combined with loan deleveraging and a reduction in wholesale funding (largely from the ING group) in recent years, this led to further improvement in the gross loans-to-deposits to, albeit still high, 126% at end-9M20 from an exceptionally high 163% at end-2018 (end-2019: 116%).\par \par \uc2 Parent funding remains material (around 23% of total funding at end-9M20), but has been declining, reflecting INGBT's strategy to become increasingly self-funded, largely through customer deposits. External wholesale funding - comprising largely trade-finance and bilateral loans - amounted to a low 4% of total funding at end-9M20, mitigating refinancing risks.\par \par \uc2 The bank's available FC liquidity - comprising cash and interbank balances (including balances placed with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  ), maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and government securities - fully covered short-term non-deposit FC liabilities falling due within a year at end-9M20. This means INGBT should be able to cope with a short-lived market closure. Consequently, and given its moderate external debt exposure, refinancing risks should be manageable, particularly considering potential liquidity support from ING. Nevertheless, FC liquidity could come under pressure from deposit instability or a prolonged loss of market access.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs and Support Rating are primarily sensitive to a change in Turkey's sovereign rating or in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in Turkey's external finances or in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, or an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely result in a downgrade of INGBT's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of ING's propensity or ability to support INGBT (not our base case) could also result in a downgrade of the LTFC IDR, but this would only occur if at the same time \uc2 Fitch\uc2  also downgrades the bank's VR.\par \par \uc2 4 Feb 2021 06:16 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms ING Bank A.S at -2-\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded due to marked deterioration in the operating environment - as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic-growth prospects - or if economic recovery is not as swift as currently expected by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 . In addition, greater-than-expected deterioration of INGBT's asset quality, protracted weakening in operating profitability and a weakening of the bank's FC liquidity, most likely due to deposit outflows or an inability to refinance maturing external obligations, if not offset by shareholder support would likely lead to a downgrade of the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR is downgraded and if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes INGBT's creditworthiness has weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LT IDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, likely reflected in a sovereign upgrade or resulting from a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in light of the challenging Turkish operating environment, the bank's limited franchise, and the 'BB-' sovereign LTFC IDR. However, a record of resilient financial metrics notwithstanding the pandemic would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes INGBT's creditworthiness has improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers'.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 INGBT's IDRs are either driven or underpinned by institutional support from ING.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ING Bank A.S.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \u