\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms Denizbank at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 4,330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2021\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 4 Feb 2021 07:00 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Denizbank at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  -London-04 February 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Denizbank A.S\uc2 .'s Long-Term Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 The support-driven ratings of the bank's subsidiary Deniz Finansal Kiralama A.S. (Deniz Leasing), which are equalised with the parent, have also been affirmed. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SENIOR DEBT RATING, SUPPORT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt rating are underpinned by shareholder support and the bank's standalone creditworthiness, as reflected in its VR.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's '4' Support Rating reflects its strategic importance to its 100% owner, UAE based Emirates NBD Bank PJSC (ENBD, A+/Stable), given its increasing integration and role within the wider group following \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's acquisition from \uc2 Sberbank of Russia\uc2  (BBB/Stable) in July 2019. ENBD's LTFC IDR is driven by an extremely high probability of support from the UAE authorities given its flagship status in Dubai. We believe support for \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  would flow through from the UAE authorities via ENBD, in case of need, given ENBD's systemic importance to the UAE banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Integration between \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  and ENBD continues to increase, particularly in terms of the banks' risk management and control frameworks. \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  continues to be run largely by local management, but ENBD representatives dominate the bank's board, while a number of key ENBD management members, including the credit risk officer, are closely involved in the Turkish bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and oversight.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/Negative), and is also the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the bank. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The sovereign's significantly weaker net FC reserves position together with weak monetary policy credibility have exacerbated external financing risks, and this is a key driver of the Negative Outlook on the sovereign LT IDR.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). However, the Outlook on the LTLC IDR is also Negative, mirroring the sovereign Outlook, reflecting our view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its obligations in LC is not lower than the probability of a sovereign default in LC obligations.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers' is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's 'b+' VR reflects the bank's reasonable strategy, moderate, but weakened, profitability record and adequate funding and liquidity profile. However, the VR also considers its moderate franchise (end-9M20: 3.3% of sector assets; unconsolidated basis) in the challenging Turkish operating environment, high exposure to risky sectors, asset quality that is weaker than peers, and only adequate core capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we see downside risks to \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's VR given uncertainties over the impact of the pandemic and potential further market volatility. A deeper and more extended economic contraction than expected would exacerbate operating environment risks, heightening pressure on the bank's financial metrics. In addition, regulatory forbearance measures, which are currently providing uplift to banks' reported asset quality and capital metrics, are due to fall away at end-June 2021.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's strategy has been largely unchanged since its acquisition by ENBD. It continues to target more granular SME and retail lending, primarily in lira, while also serving corporate and commercial customers. The bank also has a strong presence in the niche agriculture segment with a 13% market share of sector loans, supported by its large branch network.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks were already high for \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  entering the crisis, exacerbated by the below-trend growth in Turkey and high exposure to risky segments and sectors. The bank reports above-sector-average foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) lending (52% of loans at end-3Q20; peer average, 37%), which heightens credit risk where borrowers are not fully hedged against lira depreciation (the lira depreciated by 19% against the US dollar in 2020). However, about half of FC loans at end-3Q20 were issued by the bank's Austrian subsidiary \uc2 Denizbank AG\uc2  , half of which were to EU -based companies with access to FC revenues, or cash collateralised, partly mitigating the risks. FC exposures include significant project finance (about a fifth of total loans, equal to 1.6x common equity Tier 1 (CET1) capital at end-3Q20), which mainly comprise long-term, slowly amortising energy and infrastructure loans, meaning asset quality problems are likely to feed through only gradually.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's impaired loans ratio fell moderately to 6.9% at end-3Q20, from 7.5% at end-2019, despite operating environment pressures, primarily reflecting regulatory forbearance in respect of loan classification and high nominal loan growth (22% in 9M20; or 5% on an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted basis). Net of forbearance, we estimate \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's impaired loans ratio was 100bp higher. We expect impaired loans to rise moderately in absolute terms as government stimulus measures subside, regulatory forbearance is phased out (expected by end-1H21) and due to maturing pandemic-related loan deferrals.\par \par \uc2 Asset quality is also vulnerable to above-peer average Stage 2 loans (17% at end-9M20; flat on end-2019) of which about half were restructured, which could migrate to Stage 3 as loans season, and significant exposure to the risky SME segment (24% of loans). \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  had outstanding loan deferrals equal to 4% of gross loans at end-3Q20, of which 20% were classified as Stage 2. Exposures to the troubled energy (11% of loans at end-3Q20), tourism (11%) and construction (6%) sectors, which make up a high share of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's Stage 2 and restructured loan portfolios, are also sources of risk. Energy loans largely comprise project finance, which benefits to some extent from a government-guaranteed feed-in tariff set in US dollars. Similarly a high share infrastructure loans also benefit from state debt and revenue assumption guarantees, partly mitigating the credit risks.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's total loan loss allowances increased materially to 125% of impaired loans at end-3Q20 (end-2019: 90%), which compares well with the peer average, partly reflecting the frontloading of pandemic-related provisions, but also higher reserve coverage of impaired loans and Stage 2 loans. We believe the improving growth outlook in Turkey (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 3.5% real GDP growth in 2021), recent appreciation of the lira, and greater market stabilisation should be moderately supportive of Turkish banks' asset quality and collection capacities. Nevertheless, risks remain to the downside given uncertainty over the pandemic, operating environment volatility and the higher lira interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 The bank's profitability metrics are weaker than most peers and have come under pressure since 2018 from significant loan impairment charges (LICs). LICs absorbed a high 73% of pre-impairment operating profit in 9M20 (78% in 2019). As a result, the bank's cost of risk (LICs/average gross loans: 4.4% in 9M20; 2019: 4%) significantly underperforms peers, although to some extent this is a consequence of conservative loan provisioning since the bank's acquisition, and in 9M20, the frontloading of provisions against the pandemic and increased provisioning against exposures to high risk sectors, in particular in FC.\par \par \uc2 Despite this, \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's annualised operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWAs) ratio moderately increased to 1.6% in 9M20, from 1.0% in 2019, as rapid loan growth in 2Q and 3Q20, accompanied with falling funding costs supported net interest income. Nevertheless, asset-quality weakness is likely to continue to weigh on performance, given operating environment risks and loan book seasoning and as forbearance measures are removed. We expect earnings to remain broadly flat in 2021 given the high interest rate environment, but margins could start to normalise in 2H21 if interest rates stabilise. In addition, pre-impairment operating profit (9M20: 6.3% of average loans; annualised) provides a significant buffer to absorb unexpected credit losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 We view \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's capitalisation as only adequate for it its risk profile, given high asset quality risks, credit concentrations, weakened internal capital generation and sensitivity to lira depreciation (given a high share of FC loans). The CET1 ratio increased to 12.6% at end-3Q20 (from 10.4% at end-2019), supported by a TRY2.4billion capital increase from ENBD in 1Q20 (the equivalent of 1.4% of end-2019 RWAs), although net of regulatory forbearance (set to expire at end-1H21) it was a lower 10.7%. The total capital adequacy ratio was a higher 17.1% (14.7% net of forbearance) supported by US dollar-denominated, Tier-2 subordinated debt (from ENBD, for which the tenors were extended in 2Q20).\par \par \uc2 4 Feb 2021 07:00 ET  Press Release: Fitch Affirms Denizbank at 'B+'; -2-\par \par \uc2 We expect capitalisation to remain under pressure in the near term due to an increase in RWAs (as forbearance is removed), asset quality risks and weaker profitability, although it should remain adequate, supported by the bank's significant pre-impairment profitability buffer and increased provisioning (unreserved NPLs were equal to minus 15% of CET1 capital at end-3Q20). We also believe ordinary capital support would be forthcoming from ENBD, if needed.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  is largely customer deposit-funded (78% of total funding at end-3Q20), mainly from retail customers (about 70%). A significant 71% of total customer deposits were in FC at end-3Q20. This reflects both sector-wide dollarisation but also deposits from \uc2 Denizbank AG\uc2  (equal to 35% of the total), given that these are mainly in euros (85%) and US dollars (15%). The share of FC deposits in \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's unconsolidated accounts is a lower 55%, in line with the sector average. Wholesale funding (22% of total funding at end-3Q20), nearly all in FC, comprises short-term borrowings (8% of total funding), long-term borrowings (6%), subordinated debt (3%) and bank deposits (2%). Net of parent funding (6% of total funding) it was a more reasonable 16%. Wholesale funding could increase over the medium term, given that the bank is no longer subject to \uc2 EU\uc2  sanctions under its new owner and the bank is looking to diversify and extend the tenor of its funding.\par \par \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's loans/deposits ratio increased to a still reasonable 106% at end-3Q20, from 100% at end-2019, as loans grew faster than deposits in 9M20 as the bank complied with the asset ratio requirement. \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  reported good liquidity coverage ratios (LCR) at end-3Q20 (total LCR: 168%; FC LCR: 375%), while its available FC liquidity was sufficient to cover maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months plus a moderate share of FC deposits.\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity comprises mainly FC swaps (primarily with the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 )), cash and interbank placements, and FC placed under the reserve option mechanism at the \uc2 CBRT\uc2 . Given only moderate FC market funding exposure and good FC liquidity, we believe refinancing risks at the bank should be manageable, particularly considering potential liquidity support from ENBD and improved market sentiment. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a change in investor sentiment, a prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 Deniz Leasing's IDRs are driven by potential support from its 100% owner \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 . Our view of parental support reflects its strategic importance to and close integration with \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 , including the sharing of risk-assessment systems, management resources, branding and also customer referrals.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs are primarily sensitive to a change in Turkey's sovereign rating or in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in Turkey's external finances or in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, or an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk would likely result in a downgrade of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in ENBD's ability and propensity to support \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  could also result in a downgrade but only if the bank's VR was simultaneously downgraded. This is not our base case, given \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's only recent acquisition and its strategic importance to ENBD.\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to a marked deterioration in the operating environment - as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic growth prospects - or if economic recovery was not as swift as currently expected by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 . In addition, a greater than expected deterioration of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's asset quality and a protracted weakening in operating profitability would likely lead to a downgrade of the VR. The headroom at the current VR level would reduce and \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's VR would likely be downgraded, if its CET1 ratio fell and remained below 9% for a sustained period or if we viewed capitalisation as no longer commensurate with the bank's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR was downgraded and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's creditworthiness had weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LTIDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, likely reflected in a sovereign upgrade or resulting from a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in light of the challenging Turkish operating environment, the bank's moderate franchise, and the 'BB-' LTFC IDR of the Turkish sovereign. However, a record of resilient financial metrics notwithstanding the pandemic, an improvement in underlying asset quality and the maintenance of adequate core capitalisation in line with its risk profile would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's creditworthiness had improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 Deniz Leasing's ratings are sensitive to changes in \uc2 Denizbank\uc2 's Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's financial spreadsheets of \uc2 Denizbank\uc2  that has had an impact on the core and complimentary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan that was classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 All IDRs of the rated entities are either driven or underpinned by institutional support from their respective shareholders.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more 