\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Affirms \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi\uc2  at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 3,774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2021\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-04 February 2021: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S\uc2 .'s (TEB) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Rating (LTFC IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'. A full list of rating actions is below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SENIOR DEBT RATING, SUPPORT RATING AND NATIONAL RATING\par \par \uc2 TEB's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt rating is underpinned by its standalone creditworthiness as reflected in its VR, and potential institutional support from its majority owner, \uc2 BNP Paribas S.A\uc2 . (BNPP; A+/Negative), as reflected in the bank's '4' Support Rating.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's view of institutional support reflects TEB's strategic importance to, integration with and role within the wider BNPP group and small size relative to BNPP's ability to provide support. TEB is 55%-owned but fully controlled by TEB Holding, in which BNP Paribas Fortis (BNPPF; A+/Negative) has a 50% stake. TEB is fully consolidated by BNPPF, a core subsidiary of BNPP. BNPP ultimately holds a 72.5% stake in TEB, including a 23.5% stake held directly.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/Negative), and is also the main driver of the Negative Outlook. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The sovereign's significantly weaker net FC reserves position together with weak monetary policy credibility have exacerbated Turkey's external financing risks, and this is a key driver of the Negative Outlook on the sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 TEB's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). However, the Outlook on the LTLC IDR is also Negative, mirroring the sovereign Outlook, reflecting our view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its obligations in LC is not lower than the probability of a sovereign default in LC obligations.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TEB's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of TEB's VR at 'b+' considers the bank's asset quality metrics, which are better than peers, reasonable profitability and adequate funding and liquidity profile. However, the VR also reflects TEB's moderate franchise (end-3Q20: 2.2% of banking sector assets; unconsolidated basis) in the challenging Turkish operating environment, high exposure to the fairly high-risk SME segment (end-3Q20: 34% of gross loans at end-3Q20) and only adequate core capitalisation.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, we see downside risks to TEB's VR given uncertainties over the impact of the pandemic and potential further market volatility. A deeper and more extended economic contraction than expected would exacerbate operating environment risks, heightening pressure on the bank's financial metrics. In addition, regulatory forbearance measures, which are currently providing uplift to banks' reported asset quality and capital metrics, are due to fall away at end-June 2021.\par \par \uc2 TEB has historically pursued a fairly conservative growth strategy, typically reporting below sector average loan growth. However, lending grew by 9% (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted basis) in 9M20 (peer average: 6%), despite operating environment pressures, and in response to the asset ratio requirement introduced by the regulators to stimulate sector growth during the pandemic (but was subsequently removed at end-2020). The asset ratio requirement also drove an increase in government securities and swaps with the \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBRT) at TEB. In 2021, TEB aims to continue focussing on granular SME and retail lending, following a clean-up of the loan portfolios since 2018.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks were already high for TEB entering the crisis, exacerbated by the below-trend growth in Turkey, above-sector-average exposure to the risky SME segment and exposure to the troubled construction and real estate (6% of loans) and energy (5%) sectors. Exposure to the pandemic-hit tourism sector (3%) is relatively low. A moderate share of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) lending (18% of loans at end-3Q20; peer average: 37%) also increases credit risks given that not all borrowers are likely to be fully hedged against lira depreciation (the lira depreciated by 19% against the US dollar in 2020). However, exposure to long-term FC investment and project finance loans is limited.\par \par \uc2 TEB's impaired loans ratio fell moderately to 4.6% at end-3Q20, from 5.8% at end-2019, despite operating environment pressures, primarily reflecting regulatory forbearance in respect of loan classification and high nominal loan growth (16% in 9M20). Net of forbearance, we estimate the impaired loans ratio was about 75bp higher. We expect impaired loans in absolute terms to rise moderately as government stimulus measures subside, regulatory forbearance is phased out (expected by end-1H21) and due to maturing pandemic-related loan deferrals.\par \par \uc2 In addition, TEB reported fairly high, albeit below peer average, Stage 2 loans at end-3Q20 (9.9% of loans; about one-fifth restructured), which could migrate to Stage 3 as loans season. Loan deferrals were equal to about 5% of gross loans at end-2020 and about a third were classified as Stage 2. Total reserve coverage of impaired loans was below the sector average at 104% at end-9M20, partly reflecting the bank's focus on SME, and therefore collateralised, lending.\par \par \uc2 The improving growth outlook in Turkey (Fitch forecasts 3.5% real GDP growth in 2021), recent appreciation of the lira and greater market stabilisation should be moderately supportive of Turkish banks' asset quality and collection capacities. Nevertheless, risks remain to the downside given uncertainty over the pandemic, operating environment volatility and the higher lira interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 TEB's profitability is moderate (9M20: operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) of 2.3%), although broadly in line with peers', dampened by its high cost base, limited economies of scale (costs/assets of 2.8%), and asset quality pressures. The bank reports an above-sector average net interest margin (NIM; 9M20: 6.0%, or 5.1% \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap-adjusted), reflecting its focus on higher-margin, SME lending. However, we expect recently increased interest rates to put pressure on profitability in 2021, given that TEB's liabilities reprice faster than its assets, although margins could normalise in 2H21 if interest rates stabilise.\par \par \uc2 TEB's cost of risk (loan impairment charges/average gross loans ratio) compared well with the peer average in 9M20 (0.9% versus 2.1%) reflecting the clean-up of its loan book prior to the pandemic (since 2018), ensuing identification of, and adequate provisioning against, higher risk exposures and relatively high non-performing loans (NPL) collections in 9M20 (equivalent to about a third of end-2019 impaired loans). Nevertheless, we believe loan impairment charges (25% of pre-impairment operating profit in 9M20; 2019: 50%) could increase in 2021 as regulatory forbearance measures are withdrawn and given the only moderate loan loss coverage of problematic exposures. Pre-impairment operating profit (9M20: 3.5% of average loans; 2019: 4.2%) provides a reasonable, although reduced, buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 We view TEB's core capitalisation as only adequate for its risk profile, internal capital generation and asset quality pressures. The common equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio increased to 13.0% at end-3Q20 (from 12.0% at end-2019), but would have been a lower 10.8% excluding uplift from regulatory forbearance (set to expire by end-1H21). The total regulatory capital ratio was more comfortable at 18.8% (or 15.9% net of forbearance) supported by FC subordinated Tier 2 debt from BNPP, which provides a partial hedge against potential lira depreciation.\par \par \uc2 We expect capitalisation to remain under pressure in the near term due to an increase in RWAs (as forbearance is removed), asset quality risks, sensitivity to lira depreciation and weaker profitability. However, it should remain adequate, given the bank's moderate buffers over minimum regulatory requirements and adequate reserves coverage (net unreserved NPLs: minus 1% of CET1 at end-3Q20). We also believe ordinary capital support would be forthcoming from BNPP, if needed.\par \par \uc2 TEB is largely deposit-funded (75% of total funding at end-3Q20) and its loans/deposits ratio (end-9M20: 95%) outperforms peers and the sector average. Customer deposits, primarily from retail customers, are sticky despite being contractually short term and also fairly granular, although FC deposits are significant (end-9M20: about half, albeit slightly below the sector average). Wholesale funding is nevertheless fairly high (end-9M20: 25% of total funding) and is largely in FC, but it includes material BNPP group funding. Net of the latter, external market funding amounted to a more moderate 18% of total funding at end-3Q20.\par \par \uc2 TEB reports good liquidity metrics (Liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) at end-3Q20: 168%; FC LCR: 375%). At end-3Q20 available FC liquidity was also sufficient to cover maturing FC wholesale debt over 12 months plus a moderate share of FC deposits. FC liquidity comprises mainly FC swaps (primarily with CBRT), FC cash and interbank placements and FC placed under the reserve option mechanism at the CBRT. We believe refinancing risks at the bank should be manageable given low FC debt exposure, good FC liquidity, potential liquidity support from BNPP and improving market sentiment. However, FC liquidity could come under pressure from a change in investor sentiment, FC deposit instability or a prolonged loss of market access.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs are primarily sensitive to a change in Turkey's sovereign rating or in Fitch's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in Turkey's external finances or in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, or an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely result in a downgrade of TEB's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in BNPP's ability and propensity to support TEB could also result in a downgrade but only if the bank's VR was simultaneously downgraded.\par \par \uc2 The VR could be downgraded due to a marked deterioration in the operating environment - as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic growth prospects - or if economic recovery was not as swift as currently expected by Fitch. In addition, a greater than expected deterioration of TEB's asset quality and a protracted weakening in operating profitability would likely lead to a downgrade of the VR. The headroom at the current VR level would reduce and TEB's VR would likely be downgraded, if its CET1 ratio fell and remained below 9% for a sustained period or if we viewed capitalisation as no longer commensurate with the bank's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR was downgraded and Fitch believed TEB's creditworthiness had weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LT IDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if Fitch believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, likely reflected in a sovereign upgrade or resulting from a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in light of the challenging Turkish operating environment, the bank's moderate franchise, and the 'BB-' sovereign LTFC IDR. However, a record of resilient financial metrics notwithstanding the pandemic, an improvement in underlying asset quality and the maintenance of adequate core capitalisation in line with its risk profile would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if Fitch believed TEB's creditworthiness improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 TEB IDRs are either driven or underpinned by institutional support from its majority shareholder, BNPP.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on Fitch's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 Turk Ekonomi Bankasi A.S\uc2 .; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B+; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; BB-; Rating Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Short Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 ; National Long Term Rating; Affirmed; AA(tur); Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Viability Rating; Affirmed; b+\par \par \uc2 ; Support Rating; Affirmed; 4\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; B+\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Short Term Rating; Affirmed; B\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Huseyin Sevinc,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1027\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade, Canary Wharf\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Cem Bilgin,\par \par \uc2 Senior Analyst\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1230\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Redmond Ramsdale,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1836\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Louisa Williams, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 2452, Email: louisa.williams@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/dodd-frank-disclosur