\par\par \b \uc2 *Fitch Affirms QNB Finansbank at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\b0\par\par\uc2 4,041 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2021\par 18:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 3 Feb 2021 12:06 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2   at 'B+'; Outlook Negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 -Frankfurt am Main-03 February 2021: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed QNB Finansbank A.S.'s Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTFC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) at 'B+' with a Negative Outlook and Viability Rating (VR) at 'b+'.\par \par \uc2 The support-driven LTFC IDRs of the bank's subsidiaries QNB Finans Finansal Kiralama A.S. (QNB Finansleasing) and QNB Finans Faktoring A.S. (QNB Faktoring), which are equalised with that of their parent, have also been affirmed.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is detailed below.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 IDRS, SUPPORT RATING, NATIONAL RATING, SENIOR DEBT RATING\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's 'B+' LTFC IDR and senior debt rating is underpinned by both potential institutional support and its standalone creditworthiness, as measured by its VR. The Support Rating of '4' reflects its strategic importance to its 99.9% owner, \uc2 Qatar National Bank\uc2  (Q.P.S.C.) (\uc2 QNB\uc2 ; A+/Stable), integration with and role within the wider \uc2 QNB\uc2  group and small size relative to \uc2 QNB\uc2 's ability to provide support, if needed.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk caps the bank's LTFC IDR at 'B+', one notch below Turkey's LTFC IDR (BB-/ Negative), and is also the main driver of the Negative Outlook on the bank. This reflects our assessment that weaknesses in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances would make some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system that would impede banks' ability to service their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) obligations more likely than a sovereign default. The sovereign's significantly weaker net FC reserves position together with weak monetary policy credibility have exacerbated external financing risks, and this is a key driver of the Negative Outlook on the sovereign ratings.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's 'BB-' Long-Term Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LTLC) IDR, which is also driven by institutional support, is one notch above its LTFC IDR, reflecting our view of a lower likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service obligations in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC). However, the Outlook on the LTLC IDR is also Negative, mirroring the sovereign Outlook, reflecting our view that the likelihood of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would impede the bank's ability to service its obligations in LC is not lower than the probability of a sovereign default in LC obligations.\par \par \uc2 The affirmation of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's National Rating with a Stable Outlook reflects our view that the bank's creditworthiness in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to other Turkish issuers is unchanged.\par \par \uc2 VR\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's 'b+' VR is in line with that of the largest, systemically important banks in the sector despite the bank's small size. The affirmation of the VR considers the concentration of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the challenging Turkish operating environment, which exposes it to market volatility and political and geopolitical uncertainty. However, the VR also considers the bank's moderate franchise (end-9M20: 4% share of banking-sector assets; unconsolidated basis) and ensuing limited competitive advantages.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  provides a mix of services to corporate and commercial customers (a segment where it is looking to increase its market shares) small and medium-sized companies (SMEs, which are highly sensitive to the macro outlook) and retail customers. As a key subsidiary of \uc2 QNB\uc2 , we also see increasing integration with the wider group in its risk management and control environment and policies and procedures.\par \par \uc2 We see downside risks to \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's VR, given uncertainties over the impact of the pandemic and potential further market volatility. A deeper and more extended economic contraction than expected could exacerbate Turkish operating environment risks, heightening pressure on the bank's asset quality capitalisation and performance metrics. In addition, regulatory forbearance measures, which are currently providing uplift to banks' reported asset quality and capital metrics, are due to fall away at end-June 2021.\par \par \uc2 Loan growth at the bank was 11% in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted terms in 9M20, despite operating environment pressures. However, growth was below sector average (of 19%) as the bank curtailed its expansion plans (targeting both retail and business lending) in response to the weaker growth environment and pandemic-related disruption.\par \par \uc2 The bank's impaired loans ratio improved to 5.7% at end-9M20 (sector average: 4.1%) from 7% at end-2019, due to lower inflows (reflecting regulatory forbearance) and the denominator impact from nominal loan growth. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  calculates the impaired loans ratio would have been 30bp higher net of regulatory forbearance. Stage 2 loans were also fairly high (8.1% of gross loans at end-9M20), although below the peer average, and nearly two-thirds were restructured. Total reserve coverage of non-performing loans was solid at 120% end-9M20 (sector average: 127%), with specific reserve coverage of 76% (sector: 74%) and average Stage 2 reserve coverage of 19%.\par \par \uc2 Asset-quality risks were already high for \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  entering the crisis, exacerbated by the below-trend growth in Turkey, exposure to troubled sectors and segments and high, although slightly below the sector average, FC foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) lending (35% of loans at end-3Q20; sector: 36%), given that not all borrowers are likely to be fully hedged against lira depreciation (the lira depreciated by 19% against the US dollar in 2020).\par \par \uc2 FC loan exposure mainly relates to the corporate and commercial segments and includes significant project finance (end-9M20: around a fifth of gross loans), whereby loans are long term and slowly amortising, meaning asset quality problems will feed through only gradually. Project finance comprises public-private-partnership loans (PPPs, 48%), largely covered by state government revenue and debt assumption guarantees, and energy loans (15%), including renewable energy projects eligible for government-guaranteed feed-in tariffs set in US dollars. As a result, around 60% of the bank's project-finance exposure was covered by some form of state guarantee, mitigating the credit risk. Real estate comprised a further 27% of total project finance.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's sectoral exposures are reasonably diversified, typically at 10% or less of the loan book. However, the bank has exposure to the troubled construction and real estate sector (9%) and energy & commodities sector (8%). Borrower concentration is moderate compared with peers, with the top 25 cash loans equal to 19% of loans or 1.6x common equity Tier 1 (CET1) capital.\par \par \uc2 We believe the improving growth outlook in Turkey (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts 3.5% real GDP growth in 2021), recent appreciation of the lira, and greater market stabilisation should be moderately supportive of Turkish banks' loan portfolios and collection capacities. Nevertheless, risks are to the downside given uncertainty over the pandemic, operating environment volatility and the higher lira interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's profitability metrics remained in line with the sector average in 9M20. It reported an operating profit/risk-weighted assets (RWA) ratio of 2.1% (annualised) at end-9M20 (sector: 2.0%). Its performance in recent years has been underpinned by its above-sector-average net interest margin (reflecting its historical focus on higher-yielding but more risky segments), above-sector-average growth and only moderate impairment charges. However, loan impairment charges rose to 45% of pre-impairment operating profit in 9M20 (2019: 36%) and remain sensitive to asset-quality weakening.\par \par \uc2 The bank's cost efficiency is weaker than the sector average as the bank does not benefit from economies of scale. However, cost efficiency ratios have improved steadily in recent years (9M20: cost/income ratio of 41%; sector: 32%) and are in line with that of mid-sized bank peers as the bank has focused on optimisation measures and network rationalisation and achieved efficiency gains through volume growth.\par \par \uc2 Core capitalisation is only moderate given the bank's risk profile (end-9M20: CET1 ratio of 11.3% including forbearance uplift), growth appetite, sensitivity to lira depreciation (due to the inflation of FC RWAs) and asset-quality risks. The CET1 ratio would have been even lower at 9.1% excluding the impact of regulatory forbearance (set to expire at end-1H21). The bank's total regulatory capital is stronger, at 16.7%, and comfortably above the regulatory minimum, supported by FC subordinated debt from \uc2 QNB\uc2 , which provides a partial hedge against potential lira depreciation. Pre-impairment operating profit (9M20: equal to 4.5% of average loans) also provides a solid buffer to absorb losses through the income statement.\par \par \uc2 The bank is mainly customer deposit funded (9M20: 66% of total funding) and deposits are sticky despite being contractually short term. Deposits are fairly granular with retail accounts comprising 72% of the total. Customer deposits rose by 10% in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjusted terms in 9M20 mainly driven by FC deposits. The bank reports an above sector average share of FC deposits (63% versus 54% for the sector). It also reports a slightly above-sector-average loans/deposit ratio (119% at end-9M20, sector: 108%), reflecting reliance on wholesale funding. FC wholesale funding amounted to 30% of total funding at end-9M20. However, parent funding comprised 4% of the total, resulting in net FC debt exposure of 26%.\par \par \uc2 FC liquidity is adequate and was broadly sufficient to cover the bank's short-term liabilities maturing within one year as at end-9M20, meaning it should be able to cope with a short-lived market closure. Available FC liquidity comprises cash and interbank placements (including balances with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Turkey\uc2  ), maturing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and government securities. Refinancing risks are also mitigated by potential liquidity support from \uc2 QNB\uc2 . However, FC liquidity could come under pressure from prolonged loss of market access or FC deposit instability.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 3 Feb 2021 12:06 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2   at -2-\par \par \uc2 The support-driven ratings of QNB Finansleasing and QNB Faktoring are equalised with those of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  , reflecting their strategic importance to and close integration with their parent (including the sharing of risk-assessment systems, customers, branding and management resources).\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 The bank's LT IDRs and Support Rating are primarily sensitive to a change in Turkey's sovereign rating and in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the banking sector. A sovereign downgrade, particularly if triggered by further weakening in Turkey's external finances or in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, or an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, would likely result in a downgrade of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's LT IDRs.\par \par \uc2 A marked reduction in \uc2 QNB\uc2 's ability and propensity to support \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  could also result in a downgrade but only if the bank's VR was simultaneously downgraded. However, this is not our base case, given the bank's strategic importance to \uc2 QNB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank's VR could be downgraded due to a marked deterioration in the operating environment - as reflected in adverse changes to the lira exchange rate, interest rates, and economic growth prospects - or if economic recovery was not as swift as currently expected by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 . In addition, a greater than expected deterioration of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's asset quality and a protracted weakening in operating profitability would likely lead to a downgrade of the VR. The headroom at the current VR level would reduce and \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's VR would likely be downgraded, if its CET1 ratio fell and remained below 9% for a sustained period or if we viewed capitalisation as no longer commensurate with the bank's risk profile.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be downgraded if the bank's LTLC IDR was downgraded and \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's creditworthiness had weakened relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 An upgrade of the bank's LTIDRs is unlikely in the near term given the Negative Outlooks. The Outlook on the LT IDRs could be revised to Stable if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believes that the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system has abated, for example as a result of greater confidence in the sustainability of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external financing position. A significant reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, likely reflected in a sovereign upgrade or resulting from a marked improvement in Turkey's net \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves position, could result in an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 Upside for the VR is limited in the near term in light of the challenging Turkish operating environment, the bank's moderate franchise, and the 'BB-' sovereign LTFC IDR. However, a record of resilient financial metrics notwithstanding the pandemic, an improvement in underlying asset quality and the maintenance of adequate core capitalisation in line with its risk profile would reduce downside risks to the VR.\par \par \uc2 The National Rating could be upgraded if \uc2 Fitch\uc2  believed \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's creditworthiness had improved relative to other rated Turkish issuers.\par \par \uc2 SUBSIDIARY AND AFFILIATED COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 The ratings of QNB Finansleasing and QNB Faktoring are sensitive to changes in \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  's Long-Term IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Financial Institutions and Covered Bond issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 Summary of Financial Adjustments\par \par \uc2 An adjustment has been made in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's financial spreadsheets of \uc2 QNB Finansbank\uc2  that had an impact on core and complimentary metrics. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken a loan that was classified as a financial asset measured at fair value through profit and loss in the bank's financial statements and reclassified it under gross loans as we believe this is the most appropriate line in \uc2 Fitch\uc2  spreadsheets to reflect this exposure.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 All IDRs of the rated entities are either driven or underpinned by institutional support from their respective shareholders.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.