\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : Turkey Improves Policy Mix but External Finances Still Weak\b0\par\par\uc2 1,594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2021\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Related \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  Content:\par \par \uc2 Turkey: Policy Mix Improved but External Finances Are Still Weak: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10150657"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10150657}}}\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-02 February 2021: Positive steps have been taken to improve policy credibility but this process will take time, as will rebuilding Turkey's depleted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says in a new report.\par \par \uc2 Fitch's revision in August 2020 of the Outlook on Turkey's 'BB-' Issuer Default Rating (IDR) to Negative from Stable was driven by greater external financing risks, partly reflecting lower \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) reserves and weak monetary policy credibility.\par \par \uc2 Since then, policy tightening has been stepped up through a more orthodox approach, and a new economic team has placed strong emphasis in communications on sustaining disinflation and repairing external finances. The real average cost of funding from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  (CBRT) has risen 7.4pp since June and credit stimulus has been withdrawn. As a result, Turkey has seen a moderate rise in capital inflows and in gross \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves since November.\par \par \uc2 However, this change in policy was precipitated by lira weakness, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressure (net reserves excluding \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps fell by near USD70 billion in 2020), a higher deposit dollarisation ratio, widening current account deficit (5.2% of projected GDP in 11M20), and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 CBRT has limited independence and the combination of the government's current targets for GDP growth, inflation and the current account lacks credibility. How policymakers will resolve the trade-offs over time between boosting economic growth and reducing external and domestic imbalances remains unclear. It will take time to rebuild policy credibility, which was already weak ahead of significant loosening and large CBRT \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Fitch forecasts a stabilisation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external finances but not a sharp near-term improvement in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 External financing positions of the private sector have remained resilient, a feature that has provided support to the sovereign rating during periods of stress.\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Douglas Winslow\par \par \uc2 Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1721\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Ltd\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Ed Parker\par \par \uc2 Managing Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1176\par \par \uc2 Paul Gamble\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1623\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Peter Fitzpatrick, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1103, Email: peter.fitzpatrick@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "ht